const GW_DECKS = {"intro": {"name": "Module 1: Introduction", "n": 31}, "ivb": {"name": "Module 2: IV Bolus Administration", "n": 31}, "inf": {"name": "Module 3: Intravenous Infusions", "n": 20}, "cl": {"name": "Module 4: Clearance and Elimination", "n": 26}, "oral": {"name": "Module 5: Pharmacokinetics of Oral Absorption", "n": 24}, "rep": {"name": "Module 6: Repetitive IV Bolus and Intermittent IV Infusions", "n": 27}, "mo": {"name": "Module 6a: Multiple Oral Doses", "n": 21}, "ba": {"name": "Module 7a: Bioavailability and Bioequivalence", "n": 19}, "pd": {"name": "Module 7b: Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics", "n": 13}, "nonlin": {"name": "Module 8: Nonlinear Pharmacokinetics", "n": 22}};
const GUIDE_WALK = {"gobj-m1-1": {"title": "Pharmacokinetics and related disciplines", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective one. What pharmacokinetics is, and the disciplines around it. A drug's journey has two halves. Pharmaceutics covers the dosage form up to the point where the drug is dissolved. Pharmacokinetics, P K, covers everything after that: from drug in solution, into the systemic circulation, out to the tissues, through metabolism and excretion, and on to the pharmacologic effect.", "s": "intro:3", "c": [[3, "pharmaceutics: dosage form → drug in solution"], [4, "pharmacokinetics: drug in solution → circulation, tissues, metabolism, excretion → effect"]]}, {"t": "In short, P K is the study of A D M E: absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion. Absorption is the passage of drug molecules from the administration site into the systemic circulation. Distribution is the reversible transfer of a drug to and from the site of measurement. Metabolism is the conversion of one chemical species to another, also called biotransformation. Excretion is the removal of intact drug or metabolite from the body. Other faculty add a letter, an L for liberation or a T for toxicology, but in this course it is these four.", "s": "intro:4", "c": [[0, "PK = ADME"], [1, "absorption: site → systemic circulation &nbsp; distribution: reversible transfer"], [3, "metabolism: biotransformation &nbsp; excretion: removal of drug or metabolite"]]}, {"t": "Several neighbouring disciplines borrow P K methods for a particular purpose, such as treating patients, comparing population groups or judging safety. The first is biopharmaceutics. It is how the drug's physicochemical properties, the dosage form and the route of administration together affect the rate and extent of systemic absorption.", "s": "intro:6", "c": [[1, "biopharmaceutics"], [2, "properties + dosage form + route → rate and extent of absorption"]]}, {"t": "The others each have a one line definition. Clinical pharmacokinetics is applying pharmacokinetic methods to drug therapy. Population pharmacokinetics is the study of pharmacokinetic differences between population groups. Pharmacodynamics, P D, is the relationship between drug concentration at the site of action and the pharmacological response. Clinical toxicology is the study of adverse effects of drugs in the body. Toxicokinetics is applying pharmacokinetic principles to drug safety evaluation studies.", "s": "intro:8", "c": [[1, "clinical PK: drug therapy &nbsp; population PK: population groups"], [3, "PD: concentration at site of action → response"], [4, "clinical toxicology: adverse effects &nbsp; toxicokinetics: safety studies"]]}, {"t": "The quickest way to separate P K from P D is what each one relates. P K links concentration to time, and P D links concentration to response. Put simply, pharmacodynamics is concentration and response, and pharmacokinetics is concentration and time.", "s": "intro:8", "c": [[1, "PK: concentration and time"], [1, "PD: concentration and response"]]}, {"t": "Her questions read out a definition almost word for word, and the options are bare discipline names, so you go from definition to name. In her own poll item, the stem gave the biopharmaceutics definition, and the options were biopharmaceutics, toxicology, pharmacodynamics and pharmacology. The answer was biopharmaceutics. A few students picked pharmacodynamics, but P D is about response, not absorption. Pharmacology is also a distractor, and it is not one of the disciplines this module defines. The key words decide it: absorption from dosage form and route means biopharmaceutics, response means P D, drug therapy means clinical P K, and safety studies means toxicokinetics.", "s": "intro:17", "c": [[2, "rate and extent of absorption → biopharmaceutics ✓"], [3, "✗ pharmacodynamics: response, not absorption"], [5, "absorption → biopharm &nbsp; response → PD &nbsp; drug therapy → clinical PK &nbsp; safety → toxicokinetics"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m1-2": {"title": "Types of pharmacokinetic models", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective two. The types of pharmacokinetic models. A pharmacokinetic model is a simplified description of the body that lets you predict drug levels and choose a dosing regimen. Models are also used to estimate accumulation, to link concentration to effect or toxicity, to compare formulations, and to explain how disease or drug interactions change P K. Comparing the rate or extent of availability between two formulations of a drug is called bioequivalence.", "s": "intro:13", "c": [[2, "model → predict drug levels, choose a dosing regimen"], [4, "bioequivalence: rate or extent of availability, two formulations"]]}, {"t": "There are two broad types. Physiologic models, also called perfusion or blood flow models, are built from known anatomic and physiologic data. Compartment models treat the body as one or more boxes that the drug moves between at set rate constants. Physiologic models need more input data than the course uses, so this course uses compartment models almost entirely, and physiologic appears mainly as a wrong answer choice.", "s": "intro:14", "c": [[1, "physiologic (perfusion): anatomy and blood flow"], [2, "compartment: boxes joined by rate constants"]]}, {"t": "The way a drug behaves in the body decides how many boxes are needed and how they are joined. In a mammillary model there is a central compartment one, and each other compartment is joined directly to it, for example two on one side and three on the other. Drug moves from one to two by k one two and back by k two one, and from one to three by k one three and back by k three one. A rate constant between compartments, such as k one two, is in reciprocal time, per hour.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[1, "mammillary: every outer box joined to central 1"], [2, "k<sub>12</sub>, k<sub>21</sub> &nbsp; k<sub>13</sub>, k<sub>31</sub>"], [3, "k<sub>12</sub>: 1 → 2, in hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"]]}, {"t": "In a catenary model the compartments are joined in a chain, one to two to three. So drug reaches compartment three only by passing through two, by k one two and k two one, then k two three and k three two. Both models can have three compartments, so what separates them is how they are joined, not the count.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[0, "catenary: 1 → 2 → 3"], [1, "k<sub>12</sub>, k<sub>21</sub>, then k<sub>23</sub>, k<sub>32</sub>"], [2, "mammillary vs catenary: how joined, not how many"]]}, {"t": "Her questions ask by how the compartments are joined, not by how many there are. Her poll asked which model has compartments joined to one another like the compartments of a train, with the options catenary, mammillary and physiologic. The answer is catenary. The trap is mammillary, which also has three compartments. So ask whether every outer box connects to the centre, which is mammillary, or whether they form a chain, which is catenary. Physiologic appears only as a distractor in these wiring questions.", "s": "intro:15", "c": [[2, "joined like a train → catenary ✓"], [3, "✗ mammillary: also three compartments"], [4, "all to centre: mammillary &nbsp; chain: catenary"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m1-3": {"title": "Fundamental pharmacokinetic terms", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective three. Fundamental pharmacokinetic terms. These are the working words of the whole course, and later modules use them without redefining them. After a dose, the plasma concentration, C p, rises from zero to a peak and then falls. The aim of dosing is to keep it above the level that works and below the level that harms.", "s": "intro:9", "c": [[3, "C<sub>p</sub>: zero → peak → falls"], [4, "above the level that works, below the level that harms"]]}, {"t": "The lower line on the concentration time curve is the minimum effective concentration, M E C. It is the concentration that must be met or exceeded for the desired pharmacologic response. The upper line is the minimum toxic concentration, M T C, the concentration above which adverse effects start, and what counts as toxic differs between drugs. A third term, the minimum inhibitory concentration, M I C, is used for antibiotics. It is the lowest concentration needed to kill or inhibit the bacteria.", "s": "intro:9", "c": [[0, "MEC: lower line, met or exceeded for response"], [2, "MTC: upper line, adverse effects start"], [3, "MIC: antibiotics, kill or inhibit bacteria"]]}, {"t": "Her questions word the stem as the property and ask for the name, and every option is a real term from the same family. Her poll asked which concentration must be met or exceeded for the desired response. It offered steady state, minimum toxic, minimum effective and minimum inhibitory concentration. Steady state is the plateau concentration reached when the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out. The answer is minimum effective concentration.", "s": "intro:10", "c": [[1, "met or exceeded for the desired response?"], [3, "✗ steady state &nbsp; ✗ MTC &nbsp; ✗ MIC"], [4, "MEC ✓"]]}, {"t": "Elimination is the irreversible loss of drug from the body by all routes, so metabolism and excretion are both forms of elimination. Disposition is everything that happens to a drug after it is absorbed into the systemic circulation, that is, distribution and elimination. So to tell the two apart, check whether distribution is included. If it is, the term is disposition.", "s": "intro:5", "c": [[0, "elimination = metabolism + excretion"], [1, "disposition = distribution + elimination"]]}, {"t": "The first pass effect is the rapid metabolism of an oral drug, mainly by the liver, before it reaches the general circulation. It lowers the bioavailability. Bioavailability, F, is a measure of the systemic availability of a drug. F is the fraction of the dose that reaches the plasma, and it has no units.", "s": "intro:5", "c": [[0, "first-pass: oral drug, liver, before general circulation"], [1, "first-pass → lower F"], [3, "F: fraction reaching plasma, no units"]]}, {"t": "Drug levels are measured in a fluid sample. Whole blood is venous blood with an anticoagulant such as heparin or E D T A, and it contains all the cellular and protein elements. Serum is the liquid left after blood is allowed to clot and the clot is removed, so it has no cells, no fibrinogen and no other clotting factors. Plasma is the liquid supernatant after centrifuging non clotted, anticoagulated blood, so it has no cells but all the proteins, including albumin.", "s": "intro:11", "c": [[1, "whole blood: anticoagulant, all elements"], [2, "serum: clotted, no cells, no fibrinogen"], [3, "plasma: anticoagulated, centrifuged, all proteins"]]}, {"t": "Serum or plasma is used most, because they contain fewer components for the drug to interact with than whole blood does. Her poll asked which sample is most commonly used for drug measurement: saliva, urine, whole blood, or serum or plasma. The answer is serum or plasma, even though all four are possible samples. C p is the drug concentration in plasma, C s in serum, and a plain C is unspecified, so take it as whichever fluid applies.", "s": "intro:11", "c": [[0, "serum or plasma: fewer components to interact with"], [2, "most common sample: serum or plasma ✓"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> plasma &nbsp; C<sub>s</sub> serum &nbsp; C unspecified (mg/L, mcg/mL)"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m1-4": {"title": "Orders of reaction and parameters from data", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective four. Orders of reaction, and parameters from a data set. In a zero order process the same amount of drug is lost every hour, however much is left. In a first order process the same fraction is lost every hour, so more is lost per hour when more drug is present. When a stem says I V bolus, a single dose injected into a vein all at once, assume first order. When it gives a data table, you decide the order yourself.", "s": "intro:19", "c": [[2, "zero order: same amount lost per hour"], [3, "first order: same fraction lost per hour"], [4, "IV bolus → first order"], [5, "data table → decide the order"]]}, {"t": "For zero order, d C by d t equals minus k, and C equals C zero minus k t. That k is in milligrams per milliliter per hour, and the line is straight on linear axes. For first order, d C by d t equals minus k C, so the rate depends on the concentration present. Then C equals C zero times e to the minus k t, and the natural log of C equals the natural log of C zero minus k t. That k is in per hour, so k t has no units, and the line is straight on a semilog axis. Axis marks of one, ten, one hundred and one thousand mean a log scale, labelled or not.", "s": "intro:21", "c": [[0, "zero: {{frac:dC|dt}} = &minus;k &nbsp; C = C<sub>0</sub> &minus; kt"], [1, "k in (mg/mL)/hr; straight on linear axes"], [2, "first: {{frac:dC|dt}} = &minus;kC"], [3, "C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &nbsp; ln C = ln C<sub>0</sub> &minus; kt"], [4, "k in hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>; straight on a semilog axis"], [5, "1, 10, 100, 1000 → log scale"]]}, {"t": "Her practice set: a solution sampled at two, six, twelve, twenty four, thirty six and forty eight hours gave two hundred ninety four point three, two hundred eight point one, one hundred twenty three point eight, forty three point eight, fifteen point five and five point five milligrams per liter. It asks for k, C zero, the half-life and the time to ninety percent decomposed. Equal ratios over equal time spans mean first order, and equal differences per hour mean zero order. From twelve to twenty four hours, one hundred twenty three point eight over forty three point eight is two point eight three. From twenty four to thirty six hours, forty three point eight over fifteen point five is two point eight three. Equal ratios, so first order.", "s": "intro:24", "c": [[0, "t: 2, 6, 12, 24, 36, 48 hr"], [0, "C: 294.3, 208.1, 123.8, 43.8, 15.5, 5.5 mg/L"], [1, "find k, C<sub>0</sub>, t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, time to 90% decomposed"], [2, "equal ratios: first &nbsp; equal differences: zero"], [3, "12 → 24 hr: {{frac:123.8|43.8}} = 2.83"], [4, "24 → 36 hr: {{frac:43.8|15.5}} = 2.83"], [5, "<b>first order</b>"]]}, {"t": "For k, take the six hour and twenty four hour points. The first order line is k equals the natural log of C one minus the natural log of C two, over t two minus t one, with C one the earlier point so that k comes out positive. The natural log of two hundred eight point one is five point three three eight zero, and the natural log of forty three point eight is three point seven seven nine six. The difference, one point five five eight four, is divided by twenty four minus six, eighteen hours. That gives zero point zero eight six five eight, so k is zero point zero eight six six per hour, because the logs have no units and the bottom is in hours.", "s": "intro:21", "c": [[1, "k = {{frac:ln C<sub>1</sub> &minus; ln C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> &minus; t<sub>1</sub>}}"], [2, "= {{frac:ln 208.1 &minus; ln 43.8|24 &minus; 6 hr}} = {{frac:5.3380 &minus; 3.7796|18 hr}}"], [3, "= {{frac:1.5584|18 hr}} = 0.08658"], [4, "<b>k = 0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"]]}, {"t": "Next, C zero, worked back from the twelve hour point. For first order, C zero equals C times e to the plus k t. The sign is plus because C zero must be larger than any later concentration. k t is zero point zero eight six six per hour times twelve hours, which is one point zero three nine two, with no units. e to the one point zero three nine two is two point eight two seven. One hundred twenty three point eight times two point eight two seven is three hundred forty nine point nine eight, so C zero is three hundred fifty milligrams per liter.", "s": "intro:21", "c": [[1, "C<sub>0</sub> = C e<sup>+kt</sup>"], [3, "kt = 0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &times; 12 hr = 1.0392"], [4, "e<sup>1.0392</sup> = 2.827"], [5, "C<sub>0</sub> = 123.8 mg/L &times; 2.827 = 349.98"], [5, "<b>C<sub>0</sub> = 350 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "The first order half-life is zero point six nine three over k. Zero point six nine three over zero point zero eight six six per hour is eight point zero zero two, so eight hours. She said, I expect you to give me half-life in units of time. For ninety percent decomposed, ten percent remains, so C is one tenth of C zero. The time to fall to C is the natural log of C zero minus the natural log of C, over k, so C zero cancels and the time is the natural log of ten over k. That is two point three zero two six over zero point zero eight six six per hour, twenty six point five eight nine, so twenty six point six hours.", "s": "intro:22", "c": [[0, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [1, "= {{frac:0.693|0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 8.002 &nbsp; <b>t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 8 hr</b>"], [3, "90% decomposed → 10% remains: C = 0.1 C<sub>0</sub>"], [4, "t = {{frac:ln C<sub>0</sub> &minus; ln C|k}} = {{frac:ln 10|k}}"], [5, "= {{frac:2.3026|0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 26.589 &nbsp; <b>t = 26.6 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Where the percentage lost is a power of one half, count half-lives instead. One half-life leaves fifty percent, two leave twenty five, and three leave twelve point five. With an eight hour half-life, seven hundred fifty milligrams decomposing by eighty seven point five percent takes three half-lives, twenty four hours, and the seven hundred fifty milligrams is not needed. Her true or false items pair rate with half-life. First order: the rate changes with concentration, and the half-life is constant. Zero order: the rate is constant, and the half-life depends on C zero.", "s": "intro:22", "c": [[1, "1 t<sub>&frac12;</sub> → 50% left &nbsp; 2 → 25% &nbsp; 3 → 12.5%"], [2, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 8 hr, 87.5% decomposed"], [2, "3 &times; 8 hr = <b>24 hr</b>"], [4, "first: rate changes, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> constant"], [5, "zero: rate constant, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> depends on C<sub>0</sub>"]]}, {"t": "The same stem with a zero order table, at the same six times: three hundred thirty eight, three hundred fourteen, two hundred seventy eight, two hundred six, one hundred thirty four and sixty two milligrams per milliliter. Three hundred thirty eight minus three hundred fourteen is twenty four milligrams per milliliter in four hours, and three hundred fourteen minus two hundred seventy eight is thirty six in six hours. Both are six per hour, so zero order. For zero order, k equals C one minus C two, over t two minus t one. From the two and twenty four hour points, three hundred thirty eight minus two hundred six is one hundred thirty two milligrams per milliliter, over twenty two hours, so k is six milligrams per milliliter per hour.", "s": "intro:20", "c": [[0, "C: 338, 314, 278, 206, 134, 62 mg/mL"], [1, "338 &minus; 314 = 24 mg/mL in 4 hr &nbsp; 314 &minus; 278 = 36 mg/mL in 6 hr"], [2, "both 6 (mg/mL)/hr → <b>zero order</b>"], [3, "k = {{frac:C<sub>1</sub> &minus; C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> &minus; t<sub>1</sub>}}"], [4, "= {{frac:338 &minus; 206|24 &minus; 2}} = {{frac:132 mg/mL|22 hr}}"], [4, "<b>k = 6 (mg/mL)/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Working back, C zero equals C plus k t: three hundred thirty eight plus six times two, three hundred fifty milligrams per milliliter. The zero order half-life is C zero over two k. It comes from C equals C zero minus k t with C set to half of C zero, so a lower starting concentration gives a shorter half-life. Here it is three hundred fifty over two times six, which is three hundred fifty over twelve, twenty nine point one seven, so twenty nine point two hours, because milligrams per milliliter cancel. For ninety percent decomposed, ten percent of three hundred fifty is thirty five milligrams per milliliter. The time is three hundred fifty minus thirty five, over six: three hundred fifteen over six, fifty two point five hours.", "s": "intro:22", "c": [[0, "C<sub>0</sub> = C + kt = 338 + 6 &times; 2 = 338 + 12"], [0, "<b>C<sub>0</sub> = 350 mg/mL</b>"], [1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = {{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|2k}}"], [3, "= {{frac:350 mg/mL|2 &times; 6 (mg/mL)/hr}} = {{frac:350|12}} = 29.17 &nbsp; <b>t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 29.2 hr</b>"], [4, "10% of 350 = 35 mg/mL"], [5, "t = {{frac:350 &minus; 35|6}} = {{frac:315|6}} &nbsp; <b>t = 52.5 hr</b>"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m1-5": {"title": "Area under the curve by the trapezoidal rule", "segs": [{"t": "Module one, objective five. The area under the curve, by the trapezoidal rule. Plot concentration against time, and the area under that curve, the A U C, tells you the extent of drug that is available for the body to use. Its units are concentration times time, for example microgram hours per milliliter. You rarely have an equation for the curve, only a table of measured points, so the trapezoidal rule estimates the area from those points.", "s": "intro:26", "c": [[2, "AUC: extent of drug available"], [3, "units: concentration &times; time (mcg&middot;hr/mL)"], [4, "table of points → trapezoidal rule"]]}, {"t": "Join each pair of neighbouring points with a straight line. Each strip underneath is a trapezoid. Its area is the average of the two concentrations times the time between them: C n minus one plus C n, over two, times t n minus t n minus one. The two concentrations bound the segment, in micrograms per milliliter, and the width is in hours, so each strip comes out in microgram hours per milliliter. A U C total is the sum of the A U C of every segment, and the rule works for any concentration time table, from any route.", "s": "intro:28", "c": [[2, "AUC = {{frac:C<sub>n&minus;1</sub> + C<sub>n</sub>|2}} &times; (t<sub>n</sub> &minus; t<sub>n&minus;1</sub>)"], [3, "mcg/mL &times; hr = mcg&middot;hr/mL"], [4, "AUC<sub>total</sub> = sum of the segments"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example. Plasma levels were thirty eight point nine, thirty point three, eighteen point four, eleven point one, six point seven seven and four point one zero micrograms per milliliter, at zero point five, one, two, three, four and five hours. What is the A U C from hours two to four? The first step is choosing the rows. Only the rows inside two to four hours count: two hours at eighteen point four, three hours at eleven point one, and four hours at six point seven seven. The zero point five hour row is before the interval and the five hour row is beyond it. That makes two segments, each one hour wide.", "s": "intro:29", "c": [[1, "t: 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 hr"], [1, "C: 38.9, 30.3, 18.4, 11.1, 6.77, 4.10 mcg/mL"], [2, "AUC from 2 to 4 hr?"], [4, "2 hr: 18.4 &nbsp; 3 hr: 11.1 &nbsp; 4 hr: 6.77"], [6, "two segments, 1 hr each"]]}, {"t": "Segment two to three hours: eighteen point four plus eleven point one, over two, times one hour, is fourteen point seven five microgram hours per milliliter. Segment three to four hours: eleven point one plus six point seven seven, over two, times one hour, is eight point nine three five microgram hours per milliliter. Each strip is micrograms per milliliter times hours, so the units carry through the sum. Add them: fourteen point seven five plus eight point nine three five is twenty three point six eight five. So the A U C from two to four hours is twenty three point seven microgram hours per milliliter.", "s": "intro:29", "c": [[0, "2 → 3 hr: {{frac:18.4 + 11.1|2}} &times; 1 hr = 14.75 mcg&middot;hr/mL"], [1, "3 → 4 hr: {{frac:11.1 + 6.77|2}} &times; 1 hr = 8.935 mcg&middot;hr/mL"], [3, "14.75 + 8.935 = 23.685"], [4, "<b>AUC = 23.7 mcg&middot;hr/mL</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her questions give a table and an interval named in hours. Use only the trapezoids between rows inside that interval, not the whole table and not an extrapolated tail. Check each width from the time column. Where sampling times are uneven, as from zero point five to one hour, a segment is half an hour wide, not one hour. She says the units aloud rather than printing them, so give them as concentration times time. A concept question asks what A U C tells you: the extent of drug available for the body to use.", "s": "intro:29", "c": [[1, "✗ whole table &nbsp; ✗ extrapolated tail"], [3, "0.5 → 1 hr: width 0.5 hr"], [4, "units: mcg&middot;hr/mL"], [5, "AUC: extent of drug available"]]}, {"t": "A U C comes back later in the course. Comparing oral and I V A U C gives bioavailability, F, the fraction of an oral dose that enters the plasma, with no units. F equals A U C oral over A U C I V. The equation sheet has it in the dose corrected form: F equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o, where p o means by mouth and the two D terms are the I V and oral doses in milligrams. Clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time, also links the dose to the A U C.", "s": "intro:27", "c": [[2, "F = {{frac:AUC<sub>oral</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}}"], [3, "F = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} &times; {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [4, "clearance: links dose to AUC"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m2-1": {"title": "The one-compartment model after an IV bolus", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective one. The one compartment model after an I V bolus. An I V bolus is a whole dose injected into a vein at once, so all of the drug is in the body at time zero, and she calls it instantaneous input. The one compartment open model treats the body as one uniform space that drug can enter and leave, which is what open means. The drug spreads through it instantly and evenly, so the plasma concentration stands for the whole body.", "s": "ivb:3", "c": [[2, "IV bolus: whole dose at once, instantaneous input"], [3, "one compartment, open: drug enters and leaves"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> stands for the whole body"]]}, {"t": "Elimination starts the moment the drug is in, and it is first order, so a fixed fraction of the drug leaves per unit time. These are simplifying assumptions that real bodies do not meet exactly, but they make the simplest model of distribution and elimination, and the rest of the course builds on it. So when a stem says the dose is an I V bolus, the expectation is first order.", "s": "ivb:3", "c": [[0, "elimination: first order, from time zero"], [1, "simplest model of distribution and elimination"], [2, "IV bolus → first order"]]}, {"t": "Two relations go with the model. C p equals D B over V D: milligrams over liters, so milligrams per liter. V D, the volume of distribution, is what links an amount to a concentration at any one time. At time zero after a bolus, D B, the amount of drug in the body, equals the dose, D zero. The overall elimination rate constant, k, equals k m plus k e, the rate constants for metabolism and excretion, all in per hour. A k with no subscript always means this overall constant.", "s": "ivb:3", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [1, "{{frac:mg|L}} = mg/L"], [3, "t = 0: D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>"], [4, "k = k<sub>m</sub> + k<sub>e</sub> (hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)"]]}, {"t": "The model itself is first order: d D B by d t equals minus k D B, and d C p by d t equals minus k C p. The rate of loss is proportional to what is present, which is what first order means, and the minus sign says the amount is falling. The integrated result is C p equals C p zero times e to the minus k t, where C p zero is the concentration at time zero and t is the time since the dose.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[0, "{{frac:dD<sub>B</sub>|dt}} = &minus;kD<sub>B</sub> &nbsp; {{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = &minus;kC<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "rate of loss proportional to what is present"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"]]}, {"t": "The natural log of C p equals minus k t plus the natural log of C p zero. Log C p equals minus k t over two point three, plus log C p zero. She prefers the natural log form, but any of the three gets the same answer. So the concentration starts high and falls along an exponential curve, which becomes a straight line on a log scale.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[0, "ln C<sub>p</sub> = &minus;kt + ln C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>"], [1, "log C<sub>p</sub> = &minus;{{frac:kt|2.3}} + log C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>"], [3, "exponential curve → straight line on a log scale"]]}, {"t": "The model is usually a condition in her stems rather than a question of its own, and it is examined inside the calculations of objective three. About half the time she states it outright, as one compartment, first order elimination, or as one compartment open model. In the data table problems she leaves it out, because working out the order from the data is the task. On a graph with a log concentration axis, a single straight line after an I V bolus means one compartment.", "s": "ivb:10", "c": [[1, "stated: one-compartment, first-order elimination"], [3, "log axis, one straight line → one compartment"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m2-2": {"title": "Volume of distribution and clearance", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective two. Two key parameters, volume of distribution and clearance. The apparent volume of distribution, V D, is the volume the dose would have to dissolve in to give the concentration measured in plasma. It is not a real body space, which is why it is called apparent. It is a proportionality constant between the amount of drug in the body and the plasma concentration. So V D equals D B, the amount in the body, divided by C p, the plasma concentration at the same time. For a bolus, the amount in the body at time zero is the dose, so V D also equals D zero over C zero, which finds V D from a dose and an initial concentration.", "s": "ivb:5", "c": [[2, "V<sub>D</sub>: apparent volume, not a real body space"], [5, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [6, "at time zero: V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "A drug that leaves the blood for the tissues has a low plasma concentration, and so a large V D. A drug held in the blood, for example by binding to plasma proteins, has a small V D. V D is given in liters, in liters per kilogram, or as a percent of body weight. For a percent, one liter is taken as equal to one kilogram. So ten percent of an eighty kilogram patient is eight liters. She said, in kinetics, our body weight is always in kilograms.", "s": "ivb:6", "c": [[0, "leaves blood for tissues → low C<sub>p</sub> → large V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "held in blood, protein bound → small V<sub>D</sub>"], [3, "percent of body weight: 1 L = 1 kg"], [4, "10% × 80 kg = <b>8 L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her lecture example puts the same one hundred milligram dose into three different volumes of distribution. The relation is the same one rearranged: C zero equals D zero divided by V D. With a V D of one liter, one hundred milligrams over one liter gives one hundred milligrams per liter. With ten liters, one hundred milligrams over ten liters gives ten milligrams per liter. With one hundred liters, one hundred milligrams over one hundred liters gives one milligram per liter. Milligrams divided by liters leaves milligrams per liter each time. The amount of drug is the same, but these would be three different drugs, and the larger the V D, the lower the plasma concentration.", "s": "ivb:5", "c": [[1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [2, "{{frac:100 mg|1 L}} = <b>100 mg/L</b>"], [3, "{{frac:100 mg|10 L}} = <b>10 mg/L</b>"], [4, "{{frac:100 mg|100 L}} = <b>1 mg/L</b>"], [6, "larger V<sub>D</sub> → lower C<sub>p</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Clearance, Cl T, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. It measures elimination without saying how the drug is removed. It is also called drug, systemic or total body clearance. Clearance equals k times V D, where k is the elimination rate constant in per hour and V D is in liters. Per hour times liters leaves liters per hour, the unit a clearance must have. This line is not on the equation sheet. She said, I want you to take this one with you to your grave along with the half-life equation.", "s": "ivb:8", "c": [[0, "Cl: volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time"], [3, "Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [4, "hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × L = L/hr"], [5, "not on the equation sheet"]]}, {"t": "The second form is Cl T equals D zero divided by A U C from zero to infinity, the area under the plasma concentration time curve. Use it when a dose and an A U C are given instead of k and V D. Milligrams divided by milligram hours per liter leaves liters per hour. On the equation sheet it is written Cl T equals F D zero over A U C, where F, the fraction absorbed, is one for I V. Clearance is useful for two reasons: it stays constant, and it links the dose directly to the area under the curve. Clearance and V D are both constants for a given drug in a given patient, so changing the dose or the concentration does not change them.", "s": "ivb:8", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|AUC<sub>0</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>}}"], [2, "{{frac:mg|mg·hr/L}} = L/hr"], [3, "sheet: Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:FD<sub>0</sub>|AUC}}, F = 1 for IV"], [5, "Cl and V<sub>D</sub>: constant in a given patient"]]}, {"t": "Her questions test that constancy. One asks which value increases when the I V bolus dose increases. Only the plasma concentration does; clearance, half-life and V D stay the same. At the end of calculation problems she asks what doubling the dose does to the half-life, the clearance and the initial concentration: no change, no change, doubled. A true or false item says that clearance increases as concentration increases. That is false, because clearance is a constant.", "s": "inf:1", "c": [[2, "dose ↑ → C<sub>p</sub> ↑; Cl, t&frac12;, V<sub>D</sub> unchanged"], [3, "dose × 2: t&frac12; same, Cl same, C<sub>0</sub> × 2"], [5, "✗ clearance rises with concentration"]]}, {"t": "A single best answer poll asks which parameter describes elimination as volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time. The answer is clearance, not biotransformation, elimination rate or excretion. In calculations the usual trap is the unit of V D. It can arrive per kilogram, such as three liters per kilogram, as a percent of body weight, as liters, or not at all, to be found as dose divided by C zero. A percent or per kilogram V D must be multiplied by the weight in kilograms, and the answer is in liters, not kilograms. From the fish tank model of clearance, the part she uses is that only free, unbound drug can be cleared, and protein bound drug cannot.", "s": "ivb:10", "c": [[1, "✓ clearance &nbsp; ✗ biotransformation ✗ elimination rate ✗ excretion"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub>: L/kg, % body weight, L, or {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}}"], [4, "L/kg or % × weight in kg → L"], [5, "only free drug is cleared"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m2-3": {"title": "Parameters from concentration versus time data", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective three. Calculating parameters from concentration versus time data. Given a dose and one or two measured plasma concentrations, you work out the patient's elimination rate constant, half-life, starting concentration, volume of distribution and clearance. Each value feeds the next, so the order matters: k first, then half-life, then C zero, then V D, then clearance and amounts. Compare like with like. A rate constant comes from two amounts or from two concentrations, never from an amount and a concentration together. V D is the converter between the two, because an amount equals V D times a concentration.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[3, "k → t&frac12; → C<sub>0</sub> → V<sub>D</sub> → Cl<sub>T</sub>, amounts"], [5, "k from two amounts or two concentrations, never mixed"], [6, "amount = V<sub>D</sub> × concentration"]]}, {"t": "The rate constant k equals l n C one minus l n C two, divided by t two minus t one. With amounts, k equals l n D zero minus l n D B, divided by t. Either way k is in per hour, and it is the slope of l n C p against time with the sign changed. Half-life equals zero point six nine three divided by k. Per hour in the bottom leaves hours. This line is not on the equation sheet, and she named it as one to memorise. It is the same at any concentration for a first order drug.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:ln C<sub>1</sub> &minus; ln C<sub>2</sub>|t<sub>2</sub> &minus; t<sub>1</sub>}}"], [1, "k = {{frac:ln D<sub>0</sub> &minus; ln D<sub>B</sub>|t}}"], [3, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [4, "{{frac:1|hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = hr"]]}, {"t": "When no sample was taken at time zero, C zero equals C t times e to the k t, where C t is a measured concentration at time t. The exponent is positive because you are going back in time, so C zero must be higher than every sampled point. For an amount rather than a concentration, D B equals D zero times e to the minus k t, or D B equals V D times C t. Both routes give the same answer, and she accepts either.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[0, "C<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>t</sub>e<sup>kt</sup>"], [1, "+kt: back in time, C<sub>0</sub> above every point"], [2, "D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &nbsp; or &nbsp; D<sub>B</sub> = V<sub>D</sub> × C<sub>t</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her deck practice. A two hundred milligram I V dose is given to an eighty kilogram man. At six hours the plasma level is fifteen milligrams per liter, and V D is ten percent of body weight. Find the amount in the body at twelve hours, and the half-life. First convert V D to liters, using one liter per kilogram: zero point one zero times eighty kilograms is eight liters. Next, turn the six hour concentration into an amount, so it can be compared with the two hundred milligram dose. D B equals eight liters times fifteen milligrams per liter, which is one hundred twenty milligrams, because liters cancel and milligrams remain.", "s": "ivb:7", "c": [[1, "D<sub>0</sub> = 200 mg; 80 kg"], [2, "C = 15 mg/L at 6 hr; V<sub>D</sub> = 10% of body weight"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.10 × 80 kg = 8 L"], [6, "D<sub>B</sub> = 8 L × 15 mg/L = 120 mg at 6 hr"]]}, {"t": "Now k comes from the two amounts. k equals l n two hundred minus l n one hundred twenty, divided by six hours, which is zero point zero eight five per hour. The amount at twelve hours is D zero times e to the minus k t. That is two hundred milligrams times e to the minus zero point zero eight five times twelve. The exponential is zero point three six, so the amount is seventy two milligrams.", "s": "ivb:7", "c": [[1, "k = {{frac:ln 200 &minus; ln 120|6 hr}} = 0.085 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [3, "= 200 mg × e<sup>&minus;(0.085)(12)</sup>"], [4, "= 200 × 0.36 = <b>72 mg</b>"]]}, {"t": "The half-life is zero point six nine three divided by zero point zero eight five per hour, which is eight point one five hours. Carrying k unrounded, at zero point zero eight five one four per hour, gives eight point one four hours. The seventy two milligrams is the same either way. Her added question asks the concentration at twelve hours. Seventy two milligrams divided by eight liters is nine milligrams per liter.", "s": "ivb:7", "c": [[0, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|0.085 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>8.15 hr</b>"], [1, "k = 0.08514 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> → 8.14 hr"], [4, "C = {{frac:72 mg|8 L}} = <b>9 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her review problem. A one hundred fifty four pound woman gets a fifteen milligram per kilogram I V bolus. Levels are thirty two point eight five micrograms per milliliter at two hours, and nine point three two micrograms per milliliter at eight hours. Both are concentrations, so k comes straight from them. l n thirty two point eight five minus l n nine point three two is one point two five nine eight. The points are eight minus two, six hours apart. One point two five nine eight divided by six hours gives k of zero point two one per hour. The half-life is zero point six nine three over zero point two one per hour, which is three point three hours.", "s": "ivb:4", "c": [[2, "32.85 mcg/mL at 2 hr; 9.32 mcg/mL at 8 hr"], [4, "k = {{frac:ln 32.85 &minus; ln 9.32|8 &minus; 2 hr}}"], [6, "= {{frac:1.2598|6 hr}} = <b>0.21 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"], [7, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|0.21 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>3.3 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "The dose needs the weight in kilograms. One kilogram is two point two pounds, so one hundred fifty four pounds divided by two point two is seventy kilograms. Fifteen milligrams per kilogram times seventy kilograms is one thousand fifty milligrams. Next, run back to time zero: C zero equals thirty two point eight five times e to the zero point two one times two, which is fifty micrograms per milliliter. That is fifty milligrams per liter, because micrograms per milliliter and milligrams per liter are the same number. Then V D is one thousand fifty milligrams over fifty milligrams per liter, which is twenty one liters.", "s": "ivb:5", "c": [[1, "{{frac:154 lb|2.2 lb/kg}} = 70 kg"], [2, "D<sub>0</sub> = 15 mg/kg × 70 kg = 1050 mg"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = 32.85 × e<sup>(0.21)(2)</sup> = <b>50 mcg/mL</b>"], [4, "50 mcg/mL = 50 mg/L"], [5, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:1050 mg|50 mg/L}} = <b>21 L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Last, clearance is k times V D. Zero point two one per hour times twenty one liters is four point four one liters per hour. Her questions run as one patient and a chain of small parts, from k and half-life through C zero, V D and clearance to an amount at a stated hour. Fifteen minutes must be converted to zero point two five hours, and ninety nine point nine percent eliminated always means ten half-lives. Weight in pounds with a milligram per kilogram dose tests the two point two conversion, and every later part depends on it. The usual trap is dividing the dose by a concentration to get k. Convert one first, so both are amounts or both are concentrations.", "s": "ivb:8", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "= 0.21 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 21 L = <b>4.41 L/hr</b>"], [3, "15 min = 0.25 hr; 99.9% eliminated = 10 half-lives"], [5, "✗ {{frac:dose|concentration}} for k"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m2-4": {"title": "One compartment versus two compartments", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective four. Single versus multiple compartment models. Both models start with an I V bolus. The difference is whether the drug spreads evenly through the body at once, or reaches some tissues before others. Even spread at once is the one compartment model. Reaching some tissues first calls for two or more compartments.", "s": "ivb:14", "c": [[2, "both: IV bolus"], [4, "one compartment: even spread at once"], [5, "two or more: some tissues reached first"]]}, {"t": "You tell them apart on a plot of plasma concentration, C p, on a log scale against time. A first order decline is a straight line on that plot, so one compartment gives a single straight line. Two compartments give a steep early segment, the distribution phase, while drug is still moving into the tissues. It is followed by a straight, shallower segment, the elimination phase. By then the drug is evenly distributed, so its fall reflects elimination. In this module, her graphs use a log scale even when the axis is not labelled log.", "s": "ivb:20", "c": [[1, "one compartment: one straight line on log C<sub>p</sub>"], [2, "two compartments: steep distribution phase"], [3, "then shallower straight elimination phase"]]}, {"t": "The equations show the same difference. For one compartment, C p equals C p zero times e to the minus k t. One exponential term means one straight line on a log plot. For two compartments, C p equals A e to the minus alpha t, plus B e to the minus beta t. Two terms mean two phases. Here k, alpha and beta are rate constants in per hour, and C p zero, A and B are concentrations in milligrams per liter. Both lines are on the equation sheet.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &nbsp; one term, one line"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> = Ae<sup>&minus;at</sup> + Be<sup>&minus;bt</sup> &nbsp; two terms, two phases"], [5, "k, a, b in hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>; C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup>, A, B in mg/L"]]}, {"t": "When data do not follow one compartment, a curve is fitted by the method of residuals, also called feathering or peeling. The extrapolated straight line is subtracted from the observed data to get the fast, alpha, phase. Feathering is conceptual only. She said, I want you to conceptually know what that is, but I'm not gonna ask you to do that. Instead she gives you A, B, alpha and beta, and asks what they represent and why they are used.", "s": "ivb:19", "c": [[0, "method of residuals: feathering, peeling"], [1, "observed &minus; extrapolated line → fast alpha phase"], [4, "given: A, B, alpha, beta"]]}, {"t": "Her questions use this in two ways. One is a log scale graph after an I V bolus with the model withheld. A single straight line is one compartment, and a line with a steeper early segment is two compartments. The other is a stem that never says two compartment but gives a biexponential equation, or A, B, alpha and beta. Recognising the model is then the first step of the calculation. Without a graph, she has to tell you which model the drug follows. She said, if I tell you that we're giving an I V bolus dose, now what do I have to tell you? I have to tell you that it follows a one compartment model or a two compartment.", "s": "ivb:13", "c": [[2, "graph: one line → one compartment; steep start → two"], [3, "stem: biexponential, or A, B, alpha, beta → two compartments"], [5, "no graph: the model is stated"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m2-5": {"title": "Why some drugs need two compartments", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective five. Why some drugs best fit a multi compartment model. Tissues receive different amounts of blood, so a drug reaches well perfused organs first and slowly perfused tissues later, before it is evenly spread through the body. The highly perfused group is the heart, brain, hepatic portal system, kidney and endocrine glands, nine percent of body weight. The slowly perfused group, bone, ligaments, tendons, cartilage, teeth and hair, is twenty two percent. The brain is listed as highly perfused, but she says it keeps drugs out and is usually not part of the distribution phase.", "s": "ivb:15", "c": [[2, "perfusion differs → well perfused first, slow tissues later"], [3, "highly perfused: heart, brain, hepatic-portal, kidney, endocrine, 9%"], [4, "slowly perfused: bone, ligaments, tendons, cartilage, teeth, hair, 22%"], [5, "brain: keeps drugs out, usually not in distribution phase"]]}, {"t": "When drug reaches tissues at different rates, the plasma level after a single I V bolus does not fall as one straight line on a log plot. So one compartment no longer describes it, and a multi compartment model is used instead. Distribution has to be faster than elimination. Otherwise the drug would be eliminated before it reached concentrations high enough to work.", "s": "ivb:26", "c": [[0, "log C<sub>p</sub> not one straight line"], [1, "→ multi-compartment model"], [2, "distribution faster than elimination"]]}, {"t": "The two compartment model splits the body in two. The central compartment is plasma and the tissues drug reaches quickly, with its own amount D p, concentration C p and volume V p. The tissue compartment holds the more slowly reached tissues, with D t, C t and V t. Drug moves from central to tissue with the transfer constant k one two, and back with k two one, both in per hour. Drug goes in and is eliminated in the central compartment, with the elimination rate constant k.", "s": "ivb:17", "c": [[1, "central: D<sub>p</sub>, C<sub>p</sub>, V<sub>p</sub>, plasma and fast tissues"], [2, "tissue: D<sub>t</sub>, C<sub>t</sub>, V<sub>t</sub>"], [3, "central → tissue: k<sub>12</sub>; tissue → central: k<sub>21</sub>"], [4, "input and elimination k: central only"]]}, {"t": "A two compartment model can be wired three ways: elimination from the central compartment, from the tissue, or from both. The course uses elimination from the central compartment only. So drug enters and leaves the body only through the central compartment.", "s": "ivb:16", "c": [[0, "eliminated from central, from tissue, or from both"], [1, "<b>course: elimination from central only</b>"]]}, {"t": "The model diagram reads as two rate equations. The change in tissue concentration over time equals k one two times C p, minus k two one times C t. The tissue gains drug from plasma and loses it back. The change in plasma concentration equals k two one times C t, minus k one two times C p, minus k times C p. Plasma gains drug from tissue and loses it to tissue, and it also loses drug by elimination, which is the extra minus k C p. She does not ask you to solve these. Objective six has the equations you calculate with.", "s": "ivb:17", "c": [[1, "{{frac:dC<sub>t</sub>|dt}} = k<sub>12</sub>C<sub>p</sub> &minus; k<sub>21</sub>C<sub>t</sub>"], [3, "{{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = k<sub>21</sub>C<sub>t</sub> &minus; k<sub>12</sub>C<sub>p</sub> &minus; kC<sub>p</sub>"], [4, "&minus;kC<sub>p</sub>: elimination from plasma"]]}, {"t": "She asks this objective as a reason in words, not a calculation: what A, B, alpha and beta represent, and why the model is used. A and B are the intercepts, in milligrams per liter, of the distribution and elimination phases of a two compartment curve. Alpha and beta are the slopes of those phases, in per hour. The curve of tissue against plasma concentrations is theoretical, because only plasma is sampled in practice.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[1, "A, B: intercepts, mg/L"], [2, "alpha, beta: slopes, hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "tissue curve: theoretical, only plasma sampled"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m2-6": {"title": "Concentrations in a two compartment model", "segs": [{"t": "Module two, objective six. Predicting drug concentration after an I V bolus in a multi compartment model. For a two compartment drug, the plasma concentration is the sum of two falling exponential terms. C p equals A e to the minus alpha t, plus B e to the minus beta t. The alpha term is the fast one, for distribution, and the beta term is the slow one, for elimination. Alpha is always the larger exponent, because distribution is faster than elimination, but the intercepts A and B can be either way round. At time zero both exponentials equal one, so C p zero equals A plus B. She gives you the four parameters, and you substitute them.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[3, "C<sub>p</sub> = Ae<sup>&minus;at</sup> + Be<sup>&minus;bt</sup>"], [4, "a: distribution, fast; b: elimination, slow"], [5, "a > b always; A and B either way"], [6, "t = 0: C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = A + B"]]}, {"t": "Her deck practice uses theophylline: C p equals twelve e to the minus five point eight t, plus eighteen e to the minus zero point one six t. What is the plasma level three hours after an I V bolus dose? Read the parameters: A is twelve milligrams per liter, alpha is five point eight per hour, B is eighteen milligrams per liter, and beta is zero point one six per hour. Alpha is much larger than beta, as it must be. At time zero, twelve plus eighteen gives thirty milligrams per liter.", "s": "ivb:22", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = 12e<sup>&minus;5.8t</sup> + 18e<sup>&minus;0.16t</sup>"], [2, "A = 12 mg/L, a = 5.8 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>; B = 18 mg/L, b = 0.16 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "5.8 ≫ 0.16 ✓"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = 12 + 18 = <b>30 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Now each term at three hours. The distribution term is twelve times e to the minus five point eight times three, which is twelve times e to the minus seventeen point four. That is effectively zero milligrams per liter. The elimination term is eighteen times e to the minus zero point one six times three, which is eighteen times e to the minus zero point four eight. That exponential is zero point six one eight eight, so the term is eleven point one four milligrams per liter. Adding the two terms, zero plus eleven point one four gives eleven point one four milligrams per liter.", "s": "ivb:22", "c": [[1, "12 × e<sup>&minus;(5.8)(3)</sup> = 12 × e<sup>&minus;17.4</sup>"], [2, "≈ 0 mg/L"], [3, "18 × e<sup>&minus;(0.16)(3)</sup> = 18 × e<sup>&minus;0.48</sup>"], [4, "= 18 × 0.6188 = 11.14 mg/L"], [5, "C<sub>p</sub> = 0 + 11.14 = <b>11.14 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "The elimination half-life of a two compartment drug is the beta half-life, and it comes from beta alone. The beta half-life equals zero point six nine three divided by beta. Do not calculate k first; divide zero point six nine three by beta directly. For theophylline, zero point six nine three over zero point one six per hour gives four point three three hours. Per hour in the bottom leaves hours. Using alpha here is the usual mistake.", "s": "ivb:23", "c": [[1, "t&frac12;<sub>&beta;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|b}}"], [2, "✗ k first &nbsp; ✗ alpha"], [3, "t&frac12;<sub>&beta;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.16 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>4.33 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "The same four numbers give the rate constants of the model, all in per hour. Each uses the cross term, A times beta plus B times alpha, in which each intercept is multiplied by the other phase's slope, so work it out once. The overall elimination constant k equals alpha times beta times A plus B, divided by the cross term. It is a different number from beta. k two one equals the cross term divided by A plus B. k one two equals A times B times the square of beta minus alpha, divided by A plus B times the cross term. All three are on the equation sheet.", "s": "ivb:24", "c": [[1, "cross term: Ab + Ba"], [2, "k = {{frac:ab(A + B)|Ab + Ba}}"], [4, "k<sub>21</sub> = {{frac:Ab + Ba|A + B}}"], [5, "k<sub>12</sub> = {{frac:AB(b &minus; a)<sup>2</sup>|(A + B)(Ab + Ba)}}"]]}, {"t": "The volumes come next. The central volume, V p, equals the dose D zero divided by A plus B. Milligrams over milligrams per liter leaves liters. When a dose and an area under the curve are given instead, V p equals D zero divided by k times A U C from zero to infinity. The tissue volume, V t, equals V p times k one two, divided by k two one.", "s": "ivb:25", "c": [[1, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|A + B}}"], [2, "{{frac:mg|mg/L}} = L"], [3, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|k × AUC<sub>0</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>}}"], [4, "V<sub>t</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>p</sub>k<sub>12</sub>|k<sub>21</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "Her in-class sheet: a two hundred fifty milligram bolus, A eight point four five and B five point three two milligrams per liter, alpha two point one eight and beta zero point one one four per hour. The beta half-life is zero point six nine three over beta, six point zero eight hours. C p zero is A plus B, thirteen point seven seven milligrams per liter. The four hour alpha term is eight point four five times e to the minus two point one eight times four, zero point zero zero one. The beta term is five point three two times e to the minus zero point one one four times four, three point three seven two milligrams per liter. Adding gives three point three seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "ivb:21", "c": [[0, "250 mg; A = 8.45, B = 5.32 mg/L; a = 2.18, b = 0.114 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [1, "t&frac12;<sub>&beta;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.114 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>6.08 hr</b>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> = 8.45 + 5.32 = <b>13.77 mg/L</b>"], [3, "8.45e<sup>&minus;(2.18)(4)</sup> = 0.001"], [4, "5.32e<sup>&minus;(0.114)(4)</sup> = 3.372 mg/L"], [5, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> = 0.001 + 3.372 = 3.37 mg/L</b> at 4 hr"]]}, {"t": "The central volume is two hundred fifty milligrams over thirteen point seven seven milligrams per liter, which is eighteen point one six liters. The cross term is eight point four five times zero point one one four, which is zero point nine six three three, plus five point three two times two point one eight, which is eleven point five nine seven six. Together that is twelve point five six zero nine. Then k is two point one eight times zero point one one four times thirteen point seven seven, which is three point four two two one, divided by twelve point five six zero nine. That gives k of zero point two seven two per hour.", "s": "ivb:24", "c": [[0, "V<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:250 mg|13.77 mg/L}} = <b>18.16 L</b>"], [1, "Ab + Ba = (8.45)(0.114) + (5.32)(2.18) = 0.9633 + 11.5976"], [2, "= 12.5609"], [3, "k = {{frac:(2.18)(0.114)(13.77)|12.5609}} = {{frac:3.4221|12.5609}}"], [4, "<b>k = 0.272 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"]]}, {"t": "k two one is twelve point five six zero nine over thirteen point seven seven, which is zero point nine one two per hour. For k one two, the top is eight point four five times five point three two times the square of zero point one one four minus two point one eight, which is one hundred ninety one point eight eight. The bottom is thirteen point seven seven times twelve point five six zero nine, which is one hundred seventy two point nine six. So k one two is one point one zero nine per hour. Her questions give only the equation or the four parameters, and ask, in order, the elimination half-life, the initial concentration, a concentration at a stated hour and the central volume.", "s": "ivb:24", "c": [[0, "k<sub>21</sub> = {{frac:12.5609|13.77}} = <b>0.912 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"], [1, "top: (8.45)(5.32)(0.114 &minus; 2.18)<sup>2</sup> = 191.88"], [2, "bottom: (13.77)(12.5609) = 172.96"], [3, "k<sub>12</sub> = {{frac:191.88|172.96}} = <b>1.109 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"], [4, "t&frac12;<sub>&beta;</sub> → C<sub>p</sub><sup>0</sup> → C<sub>p</sub> at t → V<sub>p</sub>"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m3-1": {"title": "The pharmacokinetics of an IV infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective one. The pharmacokinetics of a drug given by I V infusion. An intravenous infusion runs drug into a vein at a constant rate for a set time, instead of giving it all at once as an I V bolus. The infusion rate, R, is the amount of drug put in per unit time, most often milligrams per hour. A constant rate of input is a zero order process, because it runs at the same rate whatever the concentration.", "s": "inf:3", "c": [[2, "infusion: constant rate, set time; bolus: all at once"], [3, "R = amount per time, mg/hr"], [4, "input: zero order"]]}, {"t": "Elimination stays first order, exactly as after a bolus. A fixed fraction of the drug present leaves per unit time, set by the elimination rate constant k, in per hour. The drug has not changed, only the way it enters the body has. The plasma concentration, C p, starts at zero and rises toward a plateau called steady state. At steady state, the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out, and the plateau concentration is C s s, in milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:3", "c": [[0, "elimination: first order, k in hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub>: from zero, rising to a plateau"], [4, "steady state: rate in = rate out → C<sub>ss</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Cessation means stopping the continuous infusion. After it there is no more input, so C p falls by ordinary first order elimination, and drug only leaves the body. She said, Our input is zero order, constant in, first order out. When we stop the in, then it's just out. The words constant rate or continuous infusion signal this pattern: zero order input with first order elimination.", "s": "inf:12", "c": [[1, "after cessation: no input, first-order fall"], [2, "zero order in, first order out"], [4, "constant rate, continuous infusion → zero order in"]]}, {"t": "The point of infusing is precise control of the plasma concentration. With the basic parameters, you can predict C p at any time during or after the infusion. This objective is the frame for every calculation in the module, so it shows up inside multi part problems. Her stems read as clinical requests in the second person, such as you are asked to recommend, with a patient vignette, an indication and a body weight.", "s": "inf:19", "c": [[0, "infusion: precise control of C<sub>p</sub>"], [1, "predict C<sub>p</sub> during or after the infusion"]]}, {"t": "Her practice problem: two hundred milligrams of an analgesic are dissolved in five hundred milliliters of five percent dextrose and infused over twenty four hours. Find the rate in milligrams per hour, and in milliliters per minute. The rate is the amount infused divided by the time it runs. So R equals two hundred milligrams over twenty four hours, which is eight point three three milligrams per hour.", "s": "inf:3", "c": [[0, "200 mg in 500 mL, over 24 hr"], [2, "R = {{frac:amount|time}}"], [3, "R = {{frac:200 mg|24 hr}} = <b>8.33 mg/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "For the pump, use the volume instead of the drug amount. Five hundred milliliters over twenty four hours is twenty point eight three milliliters per hour. Then convert hours to minutes by multiplying by one hour per sixty minutes. Hours cancel, and the result is zero point three four seven milliliters per minute, which she reports as zero point three five milliliters per minute. The compounding detail, two hundred milligrams in five hundred milliliters, is in her stem for this later part.", "s": "inf:3", "c": [[1, "{{frac:500 mL|24 hr}} = 20.83 mL/hr"], [2, "{{frac:500 mL|24 hr}} × {{frac:1 hr|60 min}}"], [3, "= 0.347 mL/min → <b>0.35 mL/min</b>"]]}, {"t": "Weight may come in pounds and V D, the apparent volume of distribution, in liters per kilogram, so convert before using them. V D is the volume that relates the amount of drug in the body to the plasma concentration. In her solution, one hundred ten pounds became fifty kilograms, because one kilogram is two point two pounds. Three liters per kilogram then became one hundred fifty liters, three liters per kilogram times fifty kilograms.", "s": null, "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub>: links amount in body to C<sub>p</sub>"], [2, "{{frac:110 lb|2.2 lb/kg}} = 50 kg"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = 3 L/kg × 50 kg = <b>150 L</b>"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m3-2": {"title": "Steady state during an infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective two. Steady state, and how it relates to continuous dosing. Steady state is reached when the rate of drug going in equals the rate going out. So the plasma concentration, C p, stops rising and holds at a plateau, called C s s. Early in an infusion little drug is in the body, so little is eliminated. As C p rises, the amount eliminated per hour rises, until it matches the infusion rate, R.", "s": "inf:4", "c": [[2, "steady state: rate in = rate out"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> stops rising → plateau, C<sub>ss</sub>"], [5, "amount out per hour rises until it equals R"]]}, {"t": "The plateau has its own relation. C s s equals R divided by clearance, and clearance is k times V D, so C s s also equals R divided by k times V D. R is the drug put in per unit time, in milligrams per hour. Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time, in liters per hour, so milligrams per hour divided by liters per hour leaves milligrams per liter. This line holds at steady state only. It contains no time, so it says nothing about when steady state arrives.", "s": "inf:4", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}} = {{frac:R|kV<sub>D</sub>}}"], [3, "{{frac:mg/hr|L/hr}} = mg/L"], [5, "no t in it: steady state only"]]}, {"t": "Before steady state, C p equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t. The first factor is C s s. The bracket, one minus e to the minus k t, is the fraction of C s s reached after infusing for time t, counted in hours from the start. Early on the bracket is a small fraction, and by about five half-lives it approaches one. The curve is asymptotic, so it gets closer and closer to C s s but in theory never reaches it exactly.", "s": "inf:5", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [1, "{{frac:R|Cl}} = C<sub>ss</sub>"], [2, "(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>) = fraction of C<sub>ss</sub> reached"], [3, "early: small fraction &nbsp; ~5 half-lives: → 1"]]}, {"t": "How long the approach takes depends only on the half-life, the time for the concentration to change by half, and so on the elimination rate constant k. k is the fraction of drug removed per hour, and it fixes the half-life: k equals zero point six nine three divided by the half-life. This line is not on the equation sheet. Her short answer for the time to reach steady state after an I V infusion is three to five half-lives. The rate R sets how high the plateau is, not how soon it arrives, so a faster infusion gives a higher C s s at the same time.", "s": "inf:4", "c": [[0, "time to C<sub>ss</sub>: half-life only"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|t<sub>&frac12;</sub>}}"], [3, "3 to 5 half-lives"], [4, "R sets the height of the plateau, not the timing"]]}, {"t": "The fraction reached can be counted in half-lives: after n half-lives it is one minus zero point five to the power n. One half-life gives fifty percent, two give seventy five percent, and three give eighty seven point five percent. Four half-lives give ninety three point seven five percent, and ten give ninety nine point nine. About three point three two half-lives reach ninety percent, her own shortcut, and four point three two half-lives reach ninety five percent.", "s": "inf:5", "c": [[0, "after n half-lives: 1 &minus; 0.5<sup>n</sup>"], [1, "1 → 50% &nbsp; 2 → 75% &nbsp; 3 → 87.5%"], [2, "4 → 93.75% &nbsp; 10 → 99.9%"], [3, "3.32 → 90% &nbsp; 4.32 → 95%"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example four. The elimination rate constant is zero point one five per hour, and the question asks how long the infusion must run to reach ninety percent of steady state. Ninety percent is not a power of one half, so the bracket is solved with a natural log. Set the bracket equal to the fraction wanted: zero point nine equals one minus e to the minus zero point one five t. Isolate the exponential: e to the minus zero point one five t equals one minus zero point nine, which is zero point one.", "s": "inf:11", "c": [[1, "k = 0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>; &nbsp; 90% of C<sub>ss</sub>: t = ?"], [3, "0.9 = 1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;0.15t</sup>"], [4, "e<sup>&minus;0.15t</sup> = 1 &minus; 0.9 = 0.1"]]}, {"t": "Take the natural log of both sides: minus zero point one five t equals the natural log of zero point one, which is minus two point three zero two six. Divide both sides by minus zero point one five per hour. The minus signs cancel, and a number divided by per hour leaves hours, so t is fifteen point three five hours. She states it as about fifteen hours. Check: the half-life is zero point six nine three over zero point one five per hour, which is four point six two hours, and fifteen point three five hours is three point three two half-lives, matching her ninety percent shortcut.", "s": "inf:11", "c": [[0, "&minus;0.15t = ln(0.1) = &minus;2.3026"], [1, "t = {{frac:&minus;2.3026|&minus;0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [2, "<b>t = 15.35 hr</b>"], [3, "≈ 15 hours"], [4, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 4.62 hr; &nbsp; 15.35 hr = 3.32 half-lives ✓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Asked how long it takes to reach steady state, the answer she wants is three to five half-lives. A statement that raising the infusion rate shortens the time to steady state is false, because that time depends on the half-life alone. A stated percentage needs a calculation instead. Fifty, seventy five or eighty seven point five percent means counting half-lives, and any other percentage means solving one minus e to the minus k t with a natural log.", "s": "inf:9", "c": [[1, "time to steady state: 3 to 5 half-lives"], [2, "✗ faster R → steady state sooner"], [4, "50, 75, 87.5% → count half-lives; &nbsp; other % → ln"]]}, {"t": "A concentration can be a percentage in disguise. In her Practice four the half-life is six hours, so k is zero point six nine three over six hours, zero point one one five five per hour. A level of five milligrams per liter against a C s s of ten milligrams per liter is fifty percent, so the time is one half-life, six hours. The next part asks for eighty percent, which is not a power of one half. So e to the minus k t equals zero point two, and t is the natural log of five divided by zero point one one five five per hour, thirteen point nine three hours.", "s": "inf:9", "c": [[1, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "{{frac:5 mg/L|10 mg/L}} = 50% → <b>1 half-life = 6 hr</b>"], [3, "80%: not a power of &frac12;"], [4, "e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> = 0.2 &nbsp; t = {{frac:ln 5|0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [4, "<b>t = 13.93 hr</b>"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m3-3": {"title": "Calculating infusion parameters", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective three. Determining optimum dosing for an infused drug by calculating pharmacokinetic parameters. Most infusion problems start by turning the stem's data into the parameters the equations need: the elimination rate constant k, the apparent volume of distribution V D, and the total body clearance. With those and the infusion rate R, every later part follows. That means the steady state concentration, the plasma concentration at a time, the time to a percentage, the loading dose and the pump rate. A loading dose is an I V bolus given as the infusion starts, sized to reach the target concentration at once.", "s": "inf:7", "c": [[2, "first: k, V<sub>D</sub>, Cl"], [4, "then: C<sub>ss</sub>, C<sub>p</sub> at t, time to %, D<sub>L</sub>, pump rate"], [5, "loading dose: I V bolus at the start"]]}, {"t": "Clearance comes from k and the volume. Clearance equals k times V D, with k in per hour and V D in liters, so clearance comes out in liters per hour. This line is used whenever the stem gives a half-life or k and a volume, but the equation needs clearance. It is not on the equation sheet. When the stem gives the routes separately, total body clearance, Cl T, is renal clearance plus metabolic clearance. Her Practice four prints this sum in its stem: five plus six point five five is eleven point five five liters per hour.", "s": "inf:7", "c": [[1, "Cl = k × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × L = L/hr"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>renal</sub> + Cl<sub>metabolic</sub>"], [5, "5 + 6.55 = <b>11.55 L/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "The two infusion lines from objective two then apply. C s s equals R divided by clearance. C p equals C s s times one minus e to the minus k t, where t is the time since the infusion started, in hours. The bracket is the fraction of C s s reached after infusing for time t, so C p climbs from zero toward C s s.", "s": "inf:9", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub>: 0 → C<sub>ss</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example one. Theophylline has a half-life of six hours and a V D of thirty liters, and it is infused at fifty milligrams per hour. The question asks the expected C s s. That needs clearance, and the stem gives only a half-life and a volume, so k comes first. k is zero point six nine three over six hours, which is zero point one one five five per hour. Clearance is zero point one one five five per hour times thirty liters, which is three point four six five liters per hour.", "s": "inf:7", "c": [[1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 6 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 30 L, R = 50 mg/hr"], [2, "C<sub>ss</sub> = ?"], [4, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [5, "Cl = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 30 L = 3.465 L/hr"]]}, {"t": "Then divide the rate by clearance. C s s is fifty milligrams per hour divided by three point four six five liters per hour. The hours cancel, leaving fourteen point four three milligrams per liter, which she works with as fourteen point four. While working this example aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance. Thirty liters is V D, and k times thirty liters gives the clearance, three point four six five liters per hour.", "s": "inf:7", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}}"], [2, "<b>C<sub>ss</sub> = 14.43 mg/L</b>"], [4, "30 L = V<sub>D</sub>, not Cl"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Examples three and four use a second drug. Cl T is four point five liters per hour, k is zero point one five per hour, and the rate is fifty milligrams per hour. The questions ask C s s, then C p eight hours after the start. Here clearance is given, so C s s is fifty milligrams per hour divided by four point five liters per hour, which is eleven point one one milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:11", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 4.5 L/hr, k = 0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, R = 50 mg/hr"], [2, "C<sub>ss</sub> = ? &nbsp; C<sub>p</sub> at 8 hr = ?"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|4.5 L/hr}} = <b>11.11 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "For C p at eight hours, first find the fraction of C s s reached. k times t is zero point one five per hour times eight hours, which is one point two, with no units left. One minus e to the minus one point two is zero point six nine eight eight. So C p is eleven point one one milligrams per liter times zero point six nine eight eight, which is seven point seven six milligrams per liter. She did not say this value aloud; it is computed from her R, Cl T and k.", "s": "inf:10", "c": [[1, "kt = (0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(8 hr) = 1.2"], [2, "1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;1.2</sup> = 0.6988"], [3, "C<sub>p</sub> = 11.11 mg/L × 0.6988"], [3, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> = 7.76 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She builds one patient and chains six or seven parts from it, and later parts say at the rate you determined above, so an error in k early on carries into every later answer. Her stems begin with unit work. In her Practice one, one hundred ten pounds is fifty kilograms, since one kilogram is two point two pounds. Three liters per kilogram times fifty kilograms is a V D of one hundred fifty liters. A half-life of four hours gives k of zero point six nine three over four hours, zero point one seven three three per hour. Contrasting scenarios are set in capitals, with the loading dose against without the loading dose, so each part is checked for which one it means.", "s": null, "c": [[1, "one patient → 6 or 7 chained parts"], [3, "110 lb ÷ 2.2 = 50 kg"], [4, "3 L/kg × 50 kg = 150 L"], [5, "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.1733 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [6, "WITH vs WITHOUT THE LOADING DOSE"]]}, {"t": "A renal failure part changes only clearance. In Practice four, Cl T falls from eleven point five five to eight point five five liters per hour. So k falls to zero point zero eight five five per hour, the new clearance divided by the unchanged V D. The rate for the same target C s s falls to C s s times the new clearance, eighty five point five milligrams per hour. Some stem data are never used, such as the sixty five kilogram body weight in Practice four. She computes to more precision than she reports and rounds to a practical value, for example fourteen point eight six milligrams per hour to fifteen.", "s": "inf:8", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub>: 11.55 → 8.55 L/hr"], [2, "k = {{frac:Cl|V<sub>D</sub>}} = 0.0855 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl = <b>85.5 mg/hr</b>"], [4, "65 kg: not used"], [5, "14.86 → 15 mg/hr"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m3-4": {"title": "Loading doses with an infusion", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective four. Calculating loading doses to be used with an intravenous infusion. A loading dose, D L, is an I V bolus given at the same moment the infusion starts. Its size should equal the amount of drug the body holds at steady state. That amount is the target steady state concentration, C s s, times the apparent volume of distribution, V D.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[2, "D<sub>L</sub>: I V bolus at the start of the infusion"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> = amount in the body at steady state"], [4, "= C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"]]}, {"t": "So D L equals C s s times V D. Milligrams per liter times liters leaves milligrams, a dose. This route is used whenever a target C s s and a volume are given, and it does not depend on any rate. It is on the equation sheet, page one, directly under the R and k form. One unit to watch: one microgram per milliliter equals one milligram per liter.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[0, "D<sub>L</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> × V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "mg/L × L = mg"], [2, "no rate in it"], [4, "1 mcg/mL = 1 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "A second route divides the infusion rate R by the elimination rate constant k. D L equals R over k, and milligrams per hour divided by per hour leaves milligrams. It gives the right dose only if R was itself chosen to give the target C s s. A rate picked without that check gives a poor loading dose.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[1, "D<sub>L</sub> = {{frac:R|k}}"], [1, "{{frac:mg/hr|hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = mg"], [2, "only if R gives the target C<sub>ss</sub>"]]}, {"t": "After it is given, the bolus part of the concentration falls by first order elimination while the infusion part rises, and the two are added. The bolus part is D L over V D, times e to the minus k t. The infusion part is R over k V D, times one minus e to the minus k t. Both parts are in milligrams per liter, and t is the time since the start of therapy, in hours. The equation sheet carries the two parts separately, as the first order C line and the infusion line, not as one line.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = bolus part + infusion part"], [1, "bolus: {{frac:D<sub>L</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [2, "infusion: {{frac:R|kV<sub>D</sub>}}(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [3, "t: from the start of therapy"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example seven. A physician wants to infuse at twenty milligrams per hour. The elimination rate constant is zero point one six per hour, and V D is ten liters. The question asks what loading dose reaches twelve point five micrograms per milliliter immediately. First route, rate over k: twenty milligrams per hour divided by zero point one six per hour. The hours cancel, and D L is one hundred twenty five milligrams.", "s": "inf:17", "c": [[1, "R = 20 mg/hr"], [2, "k = 0.16 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, V<sub>D</sub> = 10 L"], [3, "target 12.5 mcg/mL: D<sub>L</sub> = ?"], [4, "D<sub>L</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/hr|0.16 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [5, "<b>D<sub>L</sub> = 125 mg</b>"]]}, {"t": "Second route: twelve point five micrograms per milliliter is twelve point five milligrams per liter. So D L is twelve point five milligrams per liter times ten liters, again one hundred twenty five milligrams. The routes agree because this rate is the one that gives twelve point five milligrams per liter. C s s is twenty milligrams per hour divided by zero point one six per hour times ten liters, which is twelve point five milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:17", "c": [[0, "12.5 mcg/mL = 12.5 mg/L"], [1, "D<sub>L</sub> = 12.5 mg/L × 10 L = <b>125 mg</b>"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/hr|0.16 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 10 L}} = 12.5 mg/L ✓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She asks for the loading dose and the infusion rate together, as one recommendation for one patient, in either order. Either route earns the answer, and she shows both, joined with or. In one of her review problems, a C s s of twenty four milligrams per liter and a V D of about twenty two liters give a D L of twenty four times twenty two, five hundred twenty eight milligrams. The answer is a dose, so it is in milligrams.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[1, "D<sub>L</sub> and R together"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> = 24 mg/L × 22 L = <b>528 mg</b>"], [4, "a dose: mg"]]}, {"t": "A renal failure part changes clearance and leaves V D alone, so R moves and D L does not. In her Practice four, R is one hundred fifteen point five, then eighty five point five milligrams per hour, and D L is one thousand milligrams both times. The trap is using D L equals R over k with a rate that was never matched to the target concentration.", "s": "inf:15", "c": [[0, "renal failure: Cl changes, V<sub>D</sub> does not"], [1, "R: 115.5 → 85.5 mg/hr; &nbsp; D<sub>L</sub>: 1000 mg both times"], [2, "✗ {{frac:R|k}} with an unmatched R"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m3-5": {"title": "The purpose of a loading dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective five. The purpose of a loading dose. An infusion alone takes three to five half-lives to approach its steady state concentration, C s s, so the patient sits below the target for that time. A loading dose, D L, puts in at once the amount of drug that would otherwise take three to five half-lives to build up. So C s s is reached almost immediately.", "s": "inf:16", "c": [[2, "infusion alone: 3 to 5 half-lives below target"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub>: that amount, at once"], [4, "C<sub>ss</sub> almost immediately"]]}, {"t": "When D L is chosen correctly, the falling bolus curve and the rising infusion curve add to a flat line at C s s throughout therapy. Adding the concentrations from the bolus and the infusion at the same time to get the total is called superposition. Too large a loading dose gives a peak above the plateau that falls back to it. Too small a loading dose gives a dip below the plateau that climbs up to it. The plateau itself is set by the infusion.", "s": "inf:16", "c": [[0, "bolus ↓ + infusion ↑ = flat at C<sub>ss</sub>"], [1, "superposition: add the two at the same time"], [2, "D<sub>L</sub> too large: peak above, falls back"], [3, "D<sub>L</sub> too small: dip below, climbs up"], [4, "plateau set by the infusion"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example eight. A loading dose of two hundred eighty eight milligrams is given with an infusion of fifty milligrams per hour. The half-life is four hours and the apparent volume of distribution, V D, is twelve liters, and the question asks C p, the plasma concentration, at two and at four hours. First the elimination rate constant, from the first order half-life relation. k is zero point six nine three over four hours, which is zero point one seven three two five per hour.", "s": "inf:18", "c": [[1, "D<sub>L</sub> = 288 mg, R = 50 mg/hr"], [2, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 12 L; &nbsp; C<sub>p</sub> at 2 and 4 hr = ?"], [3, "k = {{frac:0.693|t<sub>&frac12;</sub>}}"], [4, "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.17325 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"]]}, {"t": "The bolus part at two hours is the loading dose spread through V D, then declining by first order. Two hundred eighty eight milligrams over twelve liters is twenty four milligrams per liter. k times t is zero point one seven three two five per hour times two hours, and e to the minus that product is zero point seven zero seven two. Twenty four milligrams per liter times zero point seven zero seven two is sixteen point nine seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:18", "c": [[0, "bolus part: C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [1, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:288 mg|12 L}} = 24 mg/L"], [2, "e<sup>&minus;(0.17325)(2)</sup> = 0.7072"], [3, "24 mg/L × 0.7072 = 16.97 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "The plateau of the infusion is the rate divided by the clearance, k times V D. Fifty milligrams per hour divided by zero point one seven three two five per hour times twelve liters is twenty four point zero five milligrams per liter. The infusion part at two hours is that plateau times the fraction reached, one minus zero point seven zero seven two, which is zero point two nine two eight. Twenty four point zero five milligrams per liter times zero point two nine two eight is seven point zero four milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:18", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|kV<sub>D</sub>}}"], [1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:50 mg/hr|0.17325 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 12 L}} = 24.05 mg/L"], [2, "1 &minus; 0.7072 = 0.2928"], [3, "24.05 mg/L × 0.2928 = 7.04 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Add the parts: sixteen point nine seven plus seven point zero four is twenty four point zero one milligrams per liter, which she gives as twenty four. Four hours is exactly one half-life. The bolus part is twenty four times one half, twelve milligrams per liter, and the infusion part is twenty four point zero five times one half, twelve point zero two. The sum is again twenty four milligrams per liter, the same as the plateau.", "s": "inf:18", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub>(2 hr) = 16.97 + 7.04 = 24.01 mg/L"], [0, "<b>C<sub>p</sub>(2 hr) ≈ 24 mg/L</b>"], [1, "4 hr = 1 half-life"], [2, "24 × 0.5 = 12.00 mg/L &nbsp; 24.05 × 0.5 = 12.02 mg/L"], [3, "<b>C<sub>p</sub>(4 hr) = 24 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Why it stays flat. The amount of drug in the body at steady state is R divided by k: fifty milligrams per hour over zero point one seven three two five per hour, which is two hundred eighty eight point six milligrams. So the two hundred eighty eight milligram dose matches the amount held at steady state. She said, We want the loading dose to look like the amount of drug that's in the body at steady state. In class she reworked this example with three hundred eighty eight milligrams, and got about thirty milligrams per liter at two hours against a plateau of twenty four, the too high case.", "s": "inf:16", "c": [[1, "{{frac:R|k}} = {{frac:50 mg/hr|0.17325 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 288.6 mg"], [2, "288 mg ≈ amount at steady state ✓"], [4, "388 mg → ~30 mg/L at 2 hr: too high"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. The purpose is the reasoning attached to the loading dose calculations of objective four, not a separate item. As a one sentence answer: the loading dose puts in at once the amount that would otherwise take three to five half-lives to accumulate. A perturbation part asks how long a new steady state takes after renal failure. The answer is in half-lives of the new, longer half-life, four point three two of them for ninety five percent in her Practice three.", "s": "inf:16", "c": [[2, "D<sub>L</sub>: at once, what takes 3 to 5 half-lives"], [3, "renal failure: new, longer t<sub>&frac12;</sub>"], [4, "95%: 4.32 new half-lives"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m3-6": {"title": "Choosing an infusion rate", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective six. Choosing an infusion rate to reach a desired steady state concentration. To hit a target C s s, the steady state equation is rearranged for the infusion rate, R. C s s equals R over clearance, so R equals C s s times clearance. If only the half-life and volume are given, clearance is built first from the elimination rate constant k and the apparent volume of distribution, V D. So R also equals C s s times k times V D.", "s": "inf:4", "c": [[3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [3, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × Cl"], [5, "R = C<sub>ss</sub> × k × V<sub>D</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Milligrams per liter times liters per hour leaves milligrams per hour, an amount per time. If the stem gives a solution concentration, R is converted into milliliters per hour or milliliters per minute for the pump. Raising R raises C s s in proportion and shifts the whole curve upward, but the plateau still arrives at the same time. She said, changing the rate changes our steady state concentration. She said she repeated it five times on purpose.", "s": "inf:4", "c": [[0, "mg/L × L/hr = mg/hr"], [1, "pump setting: mL/hr or mL/min"], [2, "R ↑ → C<sub>ss</sub> ↑, same time to plateau"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example two uses the drug from Example one: a half-life of six hours and a V D of thirty liters. The question asks what rate gives a C s s of twenty milligrams per liter. The relation needs clearance, so k comes first. k is zero point six nine three over six hours, which is zero point one one five five per hour. Clearance is zero point one one five five per hour times thirty liters, which is three point four six five liters per hour.", "s": "inf:8", "c": [[0, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 6 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 30 L"], [1, "target C<sub>ss</sub> = 20 mg/L: R = ?"], [3, "k = {{frac:0.693|6 hr}} = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [4, "Cl = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 30 L = 3.465 L/hr"]]}, {"t": "Then the rate is twenty milligrams per liter times three point four six five liters per hour. The liters cancel, so R is sixty nine point three milligrams per hour. Check: sixty nine point three milligrams per hour divided by three point four six five liters per hour gives back twenty milligrams per liter. That is up from the fourteen point four three milligrams per liter that fifty milligrams per hour gives by the same division. While working this aloud she called the thirty liters the clearance; thirty liters is V D, and k times thirty liters gives the clearance.", "s": "inf:8", "c": [[0, "R = 20 mg/L × 3.465 L/hr"], [1, "<b>R = 69.3 mg/hr</b>"], [2, "{{frac:69.3 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = 20 mg/L ✓"], [3, "{{frac:50 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = 14.43 mg/L"], [4, "30 L = V<sub>D</sub>, not Cl"]]}, {"t": "In one of her review problems the target is twenty four milligrams per liter, the half-life is five hours, and V D is about twenty two liters. k is zero point six nine three over five hours, zero point one three eight six per hour. So R is twenty four times zero point one three eight six times twenty two, which is seventy three point one eight milligrams per hour, and she gives it as about seventy three. The word recommend means round to a number a pump could take.", "s": "inf:8", "c": [[0, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 24 mg/L, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 5 hr, V<sub>D</sub> ≈ 22 L"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "R = 24 × 0.1386 × 22 = <b>73.18 mg/hr</b> ≈ 73 mg/hr"], [3, "recommend → a number a pump could take"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She gives a target C s s and two of the parameters, half-life or k, and V D or clearance, and asks what rate to recommend. With a solution concentration in the stem, a parallel part asks the rate in volume: zero point three five milliliters per minute in her Practice one, and one point three two milliliters per hour from one hundred twenty five milligram per milliliter vials in Practice two. The distractor she flags is that a different rate reaches steady state sooner. It does not, because that time depends only on the half-life.", "s": "inf:8", "c": [[1, "target C<sub>ss</sub> + two parameters → R"], [2, "volume rate: 0.35 mL/min; &nbsp; 1.32 mL/hr from 125 mg/mL"], [3, "✗ new R → steady state sooner"], [4, "time to steady state: t<sub>&frac12;</sub> only"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m3-7": {"title": "Plasma concentration at any time", "segs": [{"t": "Module three, objective seven. The plasma concentration at any time, during or after an infusion. C p follows a different equation in each phase: while the infusion runs it rises, and after it stops it falls. During the infusion, C p equals R over clearance, times one minus e to the minus k t, with t the time since the infusion started. Putting t equal to the infusion length gives the concentration at the moment the infusion stopped, called C peak.", "s": "inf:9", "c": [[2, "during: rises &nbsp; after: falls"], [3, "during: C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [4, "t = infusion length → C<sub>peak</sub>"]]}, {"t": "After cessation, which means stopping the infusion, no more drug goes in. It is only elimination, so C p decays by first order from C peak. C p equals C peak times e to the minus k t, where t is now the time since cessation. C peak plays the part of C zero, the starting concentration, in the I V bolus equation. Plotted as the natural log of C p against time, it is a straight falling line from the peak. She said, Think of that C peak as your C zero as your starting point.", "s": "inf:12", "c": [[1, "after: first-order decay from C<sub>peak</sub>"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>peak</sub> e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [3, "C<sub>peak</sub> plays C<sub>0</sub>"], [4, "ln C<sub>p</sub> vs time: straight line down"]]}, {"t": "C peak equals C s s only if the infusion ran long enough to reach steady state. Otherwise, C peak is computed first from the during infusion equation. That starting point is the key decision, and two adjacent parts of one practice set often differ only in that respect. When a time is a whole number of half-lives, halves can be counted instead of evaluating the exponential, and she shows both routes.", "s": "inf:12", "c": [[0, "steady state reached: C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>"], [1, "not reached: C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>(1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [3, "whole half-lives: count halves"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example six. One hundred fifty milligrams is infused over six hours, k is zero point two three one per hour, and the apparent volume of distribution, V D, is fifteen liters. The question asks C p three hours after cessation. The rate is one hundred fifty milligrams over six hours, twenty five milligrams per hour. Clearance is zero point two three one per hour times fifteen liters, three point four six five liters per hour. So C s s is twenty five milligrams per hour over three point four six five liters per hour, which is seven point two one five milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:14", "c": [[1, "150 mg over 6 hr; k = 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>; V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> 3 hr after cessation = ?"], [3, "R = {{frac:150 mg|6 hr}} = 25 mg/hr"], [4, "Cl = 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> × 15 L = 3.465 L/hr"], [5, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:25 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}} = 7.215 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Did the infusion reach steady state? The half-life is zero point six nine three over zero point two three one per hour, which is three hours. So six hours of infusion is two half-lives, and steady state was not reached. C peak is seven point two one five times one minus e to the minus zero point two three one times six. That bracket is zero point seven four nine nine, so C peak is five point four one milligrams per liter, which is seventy five percent of C s s.", "s": "inf:14", "c": [[1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 3 hr"], [2, "6 hr = 2 half-lives: not at steady state"], [3, "C<sub>peak</sub> = 7.215 mg/L × (1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;(0.231)(6)</sup>)"], [4, "= 7.215 × 0.7499 = 5.41 mg/L, 75% of C<sub>ss</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Then the concentration decays for three hours, which is one half-life. C p is five point four one milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point two three one times three. That exponential is zero point five, so C p is two point seven one milligrams per liter. Her stated answer is two point seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:14", "c": [[0, "3 hr = 1 half-life"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 5.41 mg/L × e<sup>&minus;(0.231)(3)</sup>"], [2, "= 5.41 × 0.5 = 2.71 mg/L"], [3, "<b>C<sub>p</sub> ≈ 2.7 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example five. An infusion reached a C s s of fifteen milligrams per liter, the half-life is five hours, and the question asks C p twelve hours after cessation. The infusion reached steady state, so C peak is fifteen milligrams per liter. k is zero point six nine three over five hours, which is zero point one three eight six per hour. C p is fifteen milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point one three eight six times twelve. That exponential is zero point one eight nine five, so C p is two point eight four milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:13", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 15 mg/L, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 5 hr; &nbsp; C<sub>p</sub> 12 hr after = ?"], [2, "C<sub>peak</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub> = 15 mg/L"], [3, "k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> = 15 mg/L × e<sup>&minus;(0.1386)(12)</sup>"], [5, "= 15 × 0.1895 = <b>2.84 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "In class she changed twelve hours to ten, exactly two half-lives, and took one quarter of fifteen milligrams per liter, which is three point seven five milligrams per liter. That written answer belongs to ten hours, and for the twelve hours asked the answer is two point eight four. Counting halves also works in her Practice four, with a six hour half-life, k of zero point one one five five per hour, and a C s s of ten milligrams per liter. One hour after cessation C p is ten times e to the minus zero point one one five five, which is eight point nine one milligrams per liter. At six and twelve hours, one and two half-lives, it is five and two point five milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:13", "c": [[0, "10 hr = 2 half-lives: 0.25 × 15 mg/L = 3.75 mg/L"], [1, "12 hr, as asked: 2.84 mg/L"], [2, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 6 hr, k = 0.1155 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, C<sub>ss</sub> = 10 mg/L"], [3, "1 hr: 10 × e<sup>&minus;0.1155</sup> = 8.91 mg/L"], [4, "6 hr: 5 mg/L &nbsp; 12 hr: 2.5 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Her stems ask for the concentration a stated number of hours after the cessation of the infusion, and say whether a loading dose was given. In one review problem an infusion aimed at a C s s of twenty four milligrams per liter is stopped after five hours, with a five hour half-life, so k is zero point one three eight six per hour. One half-life of infusion reaches fifty percent of C s s, not seventy five, so C peak is twelve milligrams per liter, not the eighteen a student started from. Three hours later it is twelve times e to the minus zero point one three eight six times three, which is seven point nine milligrams per liter.", "s": "inf:12", "c": [[2, "C<sub>ss</sub> = 24 mg/L; stopped at 5 hr; k = 0.1386 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "1 half-life → 50%: C<sub>peak</sub> = 12 mg/L &nbsp; ✗ 18 mg/L"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> = 12 mg/L × e<sup>&minus;(0.1386)(3)</sup> = <b>7.9 mg/L</b>"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m4-1": {"title": "Renal and hepatic routes of elimination", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective one. The main routes of drug elimination, renal and hepatic. Elimination is the irreversible removal of drug from the body, by every route at once. Drug that moves into tissue and later comes back has only been distributed, not eliminated. There are two ways out. Excretion removes the drug intact, without changing it chemically. Biotransformation, also called drug metabolism, first converts the drug chemically into a metabolite, most often a compound the body can eliminate more readily than the parent drug.", "s": "cl:3", "c": [[2, "elimination: irreversible, all routes"], [3, "distribution: drug comes back, not eliminated"], [5, "excretion: drug removed intact"], [6, "biotransformation: drug → metabolite"]]}, {"t": "The kidney and the liver are the two major elimination organs. The kidney excretes drug into the urine, and the liver biotransforms it. Each route has its own clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. The two add up to the total body clearance. Cl T equals Cl R plus Cl H, where Cl R is renal clearance and Cl H is hepatic clearance, also called metabolic clearance, all in liters per hour. A Cl written with no subscript means total body clearance. That sum is how the two routes appear in every calculation in this module.", "s": "cl:8", "c": [[1, "kidney: excretion &nbsp; liver: biotransformation"], [4, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>R</sub> + Cl<sub>H</sub>"], [5, "Cl with no subscript = Cl<sub>T</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Hepatic clearance is found by difference. The rate and extent of metabolism can rarely be measured directly, and the liver is not sampled, so hepatic clearance is what is left once renal clearance is taken from the total. Cl H equals Cl T minus Cl R. The second form uses f e, the fraction of the dose recovered unchanged in the urine, which has no units. One minus f e is the share of clearance that is not renal, so Cl H equals one minus f e, times Cl T. The two forms give the same answer, and both are on the equation sheet.", "s": "cl:11", "c": [[2, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> &minus; Cl<sub>R</sub>"], [3, "f<sub>e</sub>: fraction excreted unchanged, no units"], [4, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (1 &minus; f<sub>e</sub>) &times; Cl<sub>T</sub>"]]}, {"t": "A worked example. A drug has a total body clearance of one point three liters per hour, and zero point six of the dose is excreted unchanged in the urine. Its hepatic clearance is asked, using the fraction excreted. First, the share that is not renal is one minus zero point six, which is zero point four. Then that share is applied to total clearance: zero point four times one point three liters per hour gives zero point five two liters per hour. Check by subtraction. Renal clearance is zero point six times one point three liters per hour, which is zero point seven eight liters per hour, and one point three minus zero point seven eight is again zero point five two liters per hour.", "s": "cl:11", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 1.3 L/hr, &nbsp; f<sub>e</sub> = 0.6"], [3, "1 &minus; f<sub>e</sub> = 1 &minus; 0.6 = 0.4"], [4, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (0.4)(1.3 L/hr) = <b>0.52 L/hr</b>"], [6, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = (0.6)(1.3 L/hr) = 0.78 L/hr"], [6, "1.3 L/hr &minus; 0.78 L/hr = 0.52 L/hr ✓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. The routes are asked as vocabulary: a definition in the stem, and related words as options, such as elimination, excretion, biotransformation and distribution. Two features decide it: whether removal is irreversible, which distribution is not, and whether the drug is chemically changed, which is biotransformation, or removed intact, which is excretion. A usual trap swaps the organs, putting excretion in the liver and biotransformation in the kidney. Numerically, the two routes return in objective five, where hepatic clearance is whatever is left after renal clearance is subtracted.", "s": "cl:3", "c": [[1, "elimination, excretion, biotransformation, distribution"], [2, "irreversible? &nbsp; chemically changed or intact?"], [3, "✗ excretion in the liver, biotransformation in the kidney"], [4, "objective 5: Cl<sub>H</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> &minus; Cl<sub>R</sub>"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m4-2": {"title": "Filtration, secretion and reabsorption", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective two. Glomerular filtration, tubular secretion and tubular reabsorption. The amount of drug that ends up in the urine is the net result of three processes in the kidney's nephron: filtration, secretion and reabsorption. The first two add drug to the tubular lumen, the fluid space inside the kidney tubule. Reabsorption moves drug from the lumen back into the bloodstream.", "s": "cl:14", "c": [[2, "urine: net of filtration, secretion, reabsorption"], [3, "filtration, secretion → add drug to the lumen"], [4, "reabsorption → lumen back to blood"]]}, {"t": "Glomerular filtration is passive diffusion of drug from the blood across the glomerulus into the tubule, so it needs no energy. In normal kidneys it averages one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. Only unbound drug is filtered, so drug bound to plasma protein stays in the blood. Active tubular secretion uses a transporter and energy to move drug from the blood into the urine, so it removes drug on top of what filtration removes. Tubular reabsorption moves some drug from the urine in the tubule back into the blood, so it takes away from what was removed.", "s": "cl:15", "c": [[0, "filtration: passive, no energy"], [1, "GFR: average 120 mL/min"], [2, "only unbound drug is filtered"], [3, "secretion: active, transporter, adds"], [4, "reabsorption: back to blood, subtracts"]]}, {"t": "Because of this, a drug's renal clearance, Cl R, compared with one hundred twenty milliliters per minute tells you which processes are at work. Cl R equals filtration plus secretion minus reabsorption, with each term in milliliters per minute. Secretion is apparent when renal clearance is greater than the filtration rate, because secretion is then adding to filtration. This is a reasoning rule, not a line on the equation sheet.", "s": "cl:20", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = filtration + secretion &minus; reabsorption"], [2, "Cl<sub>R</sub> &gt; 120 mL/min → secretion"]]}, {"t": "Reabsorption occurs when renal clearance is less than the clearance by filtration. So a value below one hundred twenty milliliters per minute means some filtered drug is returning to the blood, and a value of about one hundred twenty means filtration alone. She allows a margin. She said, I'm saying one hundred twenty. If it's one hundred nineteen, one hundred twenty one let's call that filtration. If it's two hundred fifty, then let's assume that we've got some active secretion going on.", "s": "cl:21", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>R</sub> &lt; 120 mL/min → reabsorption"], [1, "Cl<sub>R</sub> about 120 mL/min → filtration alone"], [4, "119 or 121 → filtration"], [5, "250 → secretion"]]}, {"t": "A worked example. A drug has a total body clearance of fifty two point nine five milliliters per minute per seventy kilograms and a renal clearance of thirty four point seven two milliliters per minute per seventy kilograms, and the probable mechanism of its renal clearance is asked. The mechanism is read from renal clearance only, so total clearance is set aside. Both values are already in milliliters per minute, so no conversion is needed. Thirty four point seven two is less than one hundred twenty milliliters per minute. The kidney removes less drug than filtration alone would, so some filtered drug must be returning to the blood. The mechanism is glomerular filtration with tubular reabsorption.", "s": "cl:21", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 52.95, &nbsp; Cl<sub>R</sub> = 34.72 mL/min per 70 kg"], [2, "use Cl<sub>R</sub> only"], [3, "mL/min against mL/min: no conversion"], [4, "34.72 mL/min &lt; 120 mL/min"], [6, "<b>filtration with reabsorption</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her own example goes the other way: a renal clearance of three hundred fifty milliliters per minute means filtration plus active secretion. In her questions the stem gives a renal clearance and the answer is the process, with one hundred twenty milliliters per minute as the reference on both sides, so the item tests direction, not arithmetic. A usual trap reads a value below one hundred twenty as reduced filtration; within this rule, a value below the filtration rate points to drug returning to the blood. A second trap compares a renal clearance in liters per hour with one hundred twenty milliliters per minute without converting.", "s": "cl:25", "c": [[0, "350 mL/min → filtration + secretion"], [1, "renal clearance → process, against 120 mL/min"], [2, "✗ below 120 read as reduced filtration"], [3, "✗ L/hr compared with mL/min"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m4-3": {"title": "Creatinine clearance and its significance", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective three. Creatinine clearance and what it means. The glomerular filtration rate, G F R, tells you how well a patient's kidneys are working. It is measured with a substance eliminated primarily by filtration only, one that is neither reabsorbed nor secreted. Inulin is almost completely filtered, so it is the better marker, but it is not native to the body: it has to be given, and the testing is more involved. Creatinine comes from the breakdown of muscle and is already in the blood, so creatinine clearance, C r C l, is the common clinical estimate of G F R. Creatinine is also secreted, which is why C r C l estimates G F R rather than measuring it.", "s": "cl:16", "c": [[3, "GFR marker: filtered only, not reabsorbed, not secreted"], [4, "inulin: almost completely filtered, must be given"], [5, "creatinine: from muscle, already in the blood"], [6, "CrCl estimates GFR: creatinine is also secreted"]]}, {"t": "C r C l is calculated with the Cockcroft-Gault equation, and one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty milliliters per minute is considered normal. C r C l equals one hundred forty minus age, times I B W, divided by seventy two times S C r, times zero point eight five if female. Age is in years, I B W is ideal body weight in kilograms, and S C r is serum creatinine in milligrams per deciliter. An older patient gets a lower estimate, because one hundred forty minus age gets smaller. A higher serum creatinine sits in the bottom of the fraction, so it also lowers the estimate. The zero point eight five factor rests on the assumption that women are smaller and less muscular than men.", "s": "cl:17", "c": [[0, "normal: 120&ndash;130 mL/min"], [1, "CrCl = {{frac:(140 &minus; age) &times; IBW|72 &times; S<sub>Cr</sub>}} &nbsp; (&times; 0.85 if female)"], [2, "age in years, IBW in kg, S<sub>Cr</sub> in mg/dL"], [3, "older → lower CrCl"], [4, "higher S<sub>Cr</sub> → lower CrCl"], [5, "0.85: women smaller, less muscular"]]}, {"t": "Ideal body weight, in kilograms, is calculated from sex and height. For a male, I B W equals fifty plus two point three times the inches over five feet. For a female, I B W equals forty five point five plus two point three times the inches over five feet. The inches over five feet are the height in inches minus sixty. Neither the I B W lines nor Cockcroft-Gault are on the equation sheet. The units do not cancel, since the algebra gives kilograms divided by milligrams per deciliter. She said, I also want you to recognize that the units of creatinine clearance should be in milliliters per minute So don't give me kilograms per milligram per deciliter.", "s": "cl:18", "c": [[1, "IBW (male) = 50 + 2.3 &times; (inches over 5 ft)"], [2, "IBW (female) = 45.5 + 2.3 &times; (inches over 5 ft)"], [3, "inches over 5 ft = height in inches &minus; 60"], [4, "{{frac:kg|mg/dL}}: units do not cancel"], [5, "reported in mL/min"]]}, {"t": "When the height is given in centimeters, it is converted first. Height in inches equals height in centimeters divided by two point five four, because one inch is two point five four centimeters. A decimal height is read as written: sixty four point five inches is sixty four point five inches, not sixty four inches plus another five.", "s": "cl:18", "c": [[1, "height in inches = {{frac:height in cm|2.54}}"], [2, "64.5 inches = 64.5 inches"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example. Estimate, using the ideal body weight, the C r C l of a forty five year old female who weighs sixty one kilograms, has a serum creatinine of one point one milligrams per deciliter, and is one hundred sixty five centimeters tall. First the height: one hundred sixty five centimeters divided by two point five four is sixty four point nine six inches, which she rounds to sixty five inches. The inches over five feet are sixty five minus sixty, which is five inches. She is female, so I B W is forty five point five plus two point three times five. That is forty five point five plus eleven point five, which is fifty seven kilograms. The sixty one kilogram actual weight is not used.", "s": "cl:19", "c": [[1, "female, 45 yr, 61 kg, S<sub>Cr</sub> = 1.1 mg/dL, 165 cm"], [2, "{{frac:165 cm|2.54}} = 64.96 → 65 inches"], [3, "65 &minus; 60 = 5 inches"], [4, "IBW = 45.5 + 2.3 &times; 5"], [5, "= 45.5 + 11.5 = <b>57 kg</b>"], [6, "61 kg actual weight: not used"]]}, {"t": "Now Cockcroft-Gault. The top of the fraction is one hundred forty minus forty five, which is ninety five, times fifty seven kilograms, giving five thousand four hundred fifteen. The bottom is seventy two times one point one milligrams per deciliter, which is seventy nine point two. Five thousand four hundred fifteen divided by seventy nine point two is sixty eight point three seven. She is female, so that is multiplied by zero point eight five, which gives fifty eight point one milliliters per minute.", "s": "cl:19", "c": [[1, "top: (140 &minus; 45) &times; 57 kg = 95 &times; 57 = 5415"], [2, "bottom: 72 &times; 1.1 mg/dL = 79.2"], [3, "{{frac:5415|79.2}} = 68.37"], [4, "68.37 &times; 0.85 = <b>58.1 mL/min</b>"]]}, {"t": "What the number means. Fifty eight point one milliliters per minute is below the normal one hundred twenty to one hundred thirty, so her renal function is reduced. A low C r C l means a drug eliminated by the kidney is excreted more slowly and may accumulate, so depending on the drug, its dose may need adjusting. In the same way, a C r C l of thirty milliliters per minute means a renally eliminated drug will be excreted more slowly and may accumulate. C r C l describes the patient's kidneys, not a particular drug. A drug's renal clearance, worked out in objective five from the fraction of the dose excreted unchanged, is a different number, and confusing the two is a trap she names herself.", "s": "cl:26", "c": [[1, "58.1 &lt; 120&ndash;130 mL/min → reduced renal function"], [2, "low CrCl → slower excretion, accumulation, dose adjustment"], [4, "CrCl: the patient's kidneys"], [5, "Cl<sub>R</sub>: one drug, from f<sub>e</sub> &nbsp; ✗ confusing the two"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. A one patient vignette gives age, sex, height, actual body weight and serum creatinine, with the answer in milliliters per minute. The height usually arrives in centimeters, so the conversion is part of the question, and the actual weight is there to be left alone. Sex decides two things: which I B W constant is used, fifty or forty five point five, and whether the zero point eight five factor applies. When she worked this example, answers of one hundred four and fifty four milliliters per minute came back, and she said fifty four meant something had been done wrong.", "s": "cl:19", "c": [[1, "given: age, sex, height, actual weight, S<sub>Cr</sub>"], [2, "cm → inches; actual weight not used"], [3, "sex: 50 or 45.5; &times; 0.85 or not"], [4, "✗ 104 mL/min &nbsp; ✗ 54 mL/min"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m4-4": {"title": "Ionization and renal excretion", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective four. How the degree of ionization affects renal excretion. After drug is filtered into the tubule, some of it can cross back into the blood, which is tubular reabsorption. For weak acids and weak bases, how much crosses back depends on two things: the p H of the urine, which is the fluid in the renal tubule, and the drug's p K a. The p K a is a property of the drug that, together with the p H, fixes how much of it is ionized.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[2, "tubular reabsorption: tubule → blood"], [3, "depends on: urine pH, drug pK<sub>a</sub>"], [4, "pK<sub>a</sub> + pH → how much is ionized"]]}, {"t": "A weak acid or weak base exists in two forms at once, ionized and nonionized. Only the nonionized form crosses membranes, so only that form is reabsorbed. The ionized form does not cross, so it stays in the urine. How the drug is split between the two forms at a given p H is the degree of ionization, and the urine p H compared with the drug's p K a sets that split.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[1, "nonionized: crosses membranes, reabsorbed"], [2, "ionized: stays in the urine"], [3, "degree of ionization: the split at a given pH"]]}, {"t": "The relation is written once for each kind of drug. For a weak acid, p H equals p K a plus the log of ionized over nonionized. For a weak base, p H equals p K a plus the log of nonionized over ionized. The two differ only in which way up the ratio is written: ionized on top for a weak acid, nonionized on top for a weak base. The ratio is an amount over an amount, so it has no units. Neither form is on the equation sheet.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[1, "Weak acid: &nbsp; pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log {{frac:ionized|nonionized}}"], [2, "Weak base: &nbsp; pH = pK<sub>a</sub> + log {{frac:nonionized|ionized}}"], [4, "ratio: no units"]]}, {"t": "A weak acid is more ionized in alkaline urine, and a weak base is more ionized in acidic urine. More ionized drug stays in the tubule, so less is reabsorbed and renal clearance rises. Filtration itself is passive and does not depend on urine p H. Weak acids have p K a values of three to eight, and weak bases have p K a values of seven point five to ten point five. Those ranges overlap between seven point five and eight, so a p K a alone does not always settle whether the drug is treated as an acid or a base.", "s": "cl:22", "c": [[0, "weak acid: more ionized in alkaline urine &nbsp; weak base: more ionized in acidic urine"], [1, "more ionized → less reabsorbed → Cl<sub>R</sub> rises"], [2, "filtration: passive, not pH dependent"], [3, "pK<sub>a</sub>: weak acids 3&ndash;8, weak bases 7.5&ndash;10.5"], [4, "overlap: 7.5&ndash;8"]]}, {"t": "Questions on this objective. It is asked as recall of the two factors that govern reabsorption, which are urine p H and the drug's p K a. Trap options swap in plasma p H or the filtration rate. The p K a ranges are asked too, and the usual trap swaps them, putting weak bases at three to eight.", "s": "cl:26", "c": [[1, "urine pH + drug pK<sub>a</sub>"], [2, "✗ plasma pH &nbsp; ✗ filtration rate"], [3, "✗ weak bases at 3&ndash;8"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m4-5": {"title": "Total, renal and hepatic clearance", "segs": [{"t": "Module four, objective five. Calculating total, renal and hepatic clearance. Clearance is the volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time, such as liters per hour or milliliters per minute, for the whole body or for one organ. For a first order drug it is a constant. The rate of elimination, an amount per time, equals clearance times the plasma concentration, C p, and the volumes cancel. As the concentration falls the rate falls with it, but clearance stays the same. So a claim that clearance rises as concentration rises is false, because a change in concentration changes the elimination rate, not the clearance.", "s": "cl:5", "c": [[2, "Cl: volume cleared of drug per time"], [3, "first order: Cl constant"], [4, "Rate of elimination = Cl &times; C<sub>p</sub>"], [5, "C<sub>p</sub> falls → rate falls, Cl constant"], [6, "✗ Cl rises as C<sub>p</sub> rises"]]}, {"t": "Her example. A drug has a total clearance of fifteen milliliters per minute, and the plasma concentration is five micrograms per milliliter. The elimination rate is fifteen milliliters per minute times five micrograms per milliliter. The milliliters cancel, giving seventy five micrograms per minute. The units can also be changed first. Fifteen milliliters per minute is zero point nine liters per hour, and five micrograms per milliliter is five milligrams per liter, since one milligram per liter equals one microgram per milliliter. Then zero point nine liters per hour times five milligrams per liter is four point five milligrams per hour.", "s": "cl:6", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = 15 mL/min, &nbsp; C<sub>p</sub> = 5 mcg/mL"], [2, "rate = (15 mL/min)(5 mcg/mL)"], [3, "= <b>75 mcg/min</b>"], [5, "15 mL/min = 0.9 L/hr; &nbsp; 5 mcg/mL = 5 mg/L"], [6, "(0.9 L/hr)(5 mg/L) = <b>4.5 mg/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Total clearance comes from either of two relations. From dose and exposure, Cl T equals F times D zero, divided by A U C, the area under the plasma concentration time curve. F is the bioavailability factor, with no units, and is taken as one for an I V dose; D zero is in milligrams and A U C in milligram hours per liter, so Cl T comes out in liters per hour. From the rate constant and the volume, Cl T equals k times V D, with k per hour and V D in liters, so again liters per hour.", "s": "cl:7", "c": [[1, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:F &times; D<sub>0</sub>|AUC}}"], [2, "{{frac:mg|mg&middot;hr/L}} = L/hr &nbsp; (F = 1 for IV)"], [3, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = k &times; V<sub>D</sub> &nbsp; (hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(L) = L/hr"]]}, {"t": "Collecting urine gives the fraction of the dose excreted unchanged, f e. It equals the cumulative amount of unchanged drug in the urine, D u infinity, divided by F times D zero, and it also equals k e over k, so k e equals f e times k. Here k e is the excretion rate constant, the part of k due to urinary excretion of unchanged drug. She said, lowercase f e is our fraction excreted, capital F is our bioavailability factor. Renal clearance is that fraction of total clearance, so Cl R equals f e times Cl T, and hepatic clearance is the rest, one minus f e times Cl T, or Cl T minus Cl R.", "s": "cl:10", "c": [[1, "f<sub>e</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>u</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>|F &times; D<sub>0</sub>}} = {{frac:k<sub>e</sub>|k}}"], [1, "k<sub>e</sub> = f<sub>e</sub> &times; k"], [3, "f<sub>e</sub>: fraction excreted &nbsp; F: bioavailability"], [4, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = f<sub>e</sub> &times; Cl<sub>T</sub>"], [4, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = (1 &minus; f<sub>e</sub>) &times; Cl<sub>T</sub> = Cl<sub>T</sub> &minus; Cl<sub>R</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example. Five hundred milligrams of a drug was given by rapid I V injection. The V D is fifteen liters and the elimination half-life is eight hours. Urine was collected for forty eight hours, and three hundred milligrams of unchanged drug was recovered. Asked are f e, k, k e, Cl T, Cl R and Cl H. The fraction f e comes first, because every later term uses it. With F equal to one for an I V dose, f e is three hundred milligrams over five hundred milligrams. The milligrams cancel, so f e is zero point six, with no units.", "s": "cl:12", "c": [[1, "D<sub>0</sub> = 500 mg IV, &nbsp; F = 1"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L, &nbsp; t&frac12; = 8 hr"], [3, "D<sub>u</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = 300 mg"], [6, "f<sub>e</sub> = {{frac:300 mg|500 mg}}"], [7, "<b>f<sub>e</sub> = 0.6</b>, no units"]]}, {"t": "Next, k comes from the half-life, because the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k. Zero point six nine three divided by eight hours is zero point zero eight six six per hour. The excretion rate constant k e is f e times k: zero point six times zero point zero eight six six per hour is zero point zero five two per hour. Total clearance is k times V D: zero point zero eight six six per hour times fifteen liters is one point three liters per hour.", "s": "cl:12", "c": [[0, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [1, "k = {{frac:0.693|8 hr}} = <b>0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"], [2, "k<sub>e</sub> = (0.6)(0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>) = <b>0.052 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup></b>"], [3, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = (0.0866 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(15 L) = <b>1.3 L/hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Renal clearance is f e times Cl T: zero point six times one point three liters per hour is zero point seven eight liters per hour. Hepatic clearance is found by difference: one point three minus zero point seven eight liters per hour is zero point five two liters per hour. The forty eight hour collection time is not used; only the cumulative amount and the dose are. The traps are giving f e units, or putting the urinary fraction in place of capital F. On an I V problem F is one whatever the urine shows.", "s": "cl:12", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>R</sub> = (0.6)(1.3 L/hr) = <b>0.78 L/hr</b>"], [1, "Cl<sub>H</sub> = 1.3 L/hr &minus; 0.78 L/hr = <b>0.52 L/hr</b>"], [2, "48 hr collection: not used"], [3, "✗ units on f<sub>e</sub> &nbsp; ✗ f<sub>e</sub> in place of F"]]}, {"t": "Clearance, half-life and volume are linked. Putting Cl T equals k times V D into the half-life equals zero point six nine three over k gives a second form: the half-life equals zero point six nine three times V D, divided by Cl T. V D and Cl T must share a volume unit, and the half-life then takes the time unit of Cl T. If the volume stays the same and clearance falls, as in renal failure, the half-life gets longer.", "s": "cl:23", "c": [[1, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|k}}, &nbsp; Cl<sub>T</sub> = k &times; V<sub>D</sub>"], [1, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693 &times; V<sub>D</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>}}"], [2, "V<sub>D</sub> and Cl<sub>T</sub>: same volume unit"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> same, Cl<sub>T</sub> falls → t&frac12; longer"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example. A new antibiotic is actively secreted by the kidney, and its V D is twenty five liters. Its clearance is seven hundred fifty milliliters per minute, falling to one hundred fifty milliliters per minute in partial renal failure. To match V D in liters, seven hundred fifty milliliters per minute is zero point seven five liters per minute. The usual half-life is zero point six nine three times twenty five liters, divided by zero point seven five liters per minute, which is twenty three point one minutes. In renal failure V D stays twenty five liters, and with zero point one five liters per minute the half-life is one hundred fifteen point five minutes. Clearance fell to one fifth, so the half-life became five times longer.", "s": "cl:24", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub> = 25 L"], [2, "Cl<sub>T</sub>: 750 mL/min → 150 mL/min"], [3, "750 mL/min = 0.75 L/min"], [4, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693 &times; 25 L|0.75 L/min}} = <b>23.1 min</b>"], [5, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693 &times; 25 L|0.15 L/min}} = <b>115.5 min</b>"], [6, "Cl<sub>T</sub> to one fifth → t&frac12; &times; 5"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m5-1": {"title": "Kinetics after an oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective one. The kinetics of a drug after extravascular administration. Extravascular means the dose is given outside the blood vessels, and in this course that means by mouth, so the drug has to be absorbed before it reaches the blood. An I V bolus puts all the drug in at once, and an I V infusion puts it in at a constant, zero order rate. An oral dose goes in by a first order process instead: first order in, first order out.", "s": "oral:4", "c": [[2, "extravascular: oral, absorbed first"], [3, "IV bolus: all at once &nbsp; IV infusion: zero order in"], [4, "oral: first order in, first order out"]]}, {"t": "Three amounts, all in milligrams, follow the dose. D G I is the drug still in the gut waiting to be absorbed, D B is the drug in the body, spread through the volume of distribution V D, and D E is the drug already eliminated. The change in the amount in the body is the rate in minus the rate out. So d D B over d t equals d D G I over d t, the rate of absorption from the gut, minus d D E over d t, the rate of elimination. This line is not on the equation sheet.", "s": "oral:5", "c": [[1, "D<sub>GI</sub> → D<sub>B</sub> (in V<sub>D</sub>) → D<sub>E</sub>"], [2, "change in body = rate in &minus; rate out"], [3, "{{frac:dD<sub>B</sub>|dt}} = {{frac:dD<sub>GI</sub>|dt}} &minus; {{frac:dD<sub>E</sub>|dt}}"]]}, {"t": "In first order absorption, the rate of absorption is proportional to the amount of drug still in the gut, so a fixed fraction is absorbed per unit time. Before it reaches the body, the tablet has to disintegrate, dissolve, cross membranes and pass through the liver. All of those steps are rolled into one absorption rate constant, k a, with units of per hour. Usually only part of the dose arrives, because some of it is eliminated before it becomes available. The fraction that arrives is the bioavailability, F, between zero and one with no units, and for an I V dose F is taken as one. She said, when you see a capital F, You should think oral.", "s": "oral:7", "c": [[0, "rate in proportional to D<sub>GI</sub>: fixed fraction per time"], [2, "k<sub>a</sub>: all absorption steps, hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [4, "F: fraction that arrives, 0 to 1; IV: F = 1"], [5, "capital F → oral"]]}, {"t": "The absorption half-life is the time for half of the drug still to be absorbed to be absorbed, in hours. It follows the first order half-life relation: the absorption half-life equals zero point six nine three over k a. Stems usually give the absorption half-life, so it is turned into k a before any oral equation is used. This line is not on the equation sheet either.", "s": "oral:6", "c": [[1, "t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k<sub>a</sub>}}"], [2, "given t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> → k<sub>a</sub> first"]]}, {"t": "Because the drug enters gradually while it is also being eliminated, the plasma curve starts at zero, rises to a clear peak and then falls. In the absorption phase, the rising part, more drug is going in than coming out. At the peak, C max at t max, the rate in equals the rate out. Just after the peak is the post absorption phase: elimination is now faster than absorption, although some drug is still being absorbed. Once all the drug has been absorbed, only elimination is left, and that is the elimination phase.", "s": "oral:8", "c": [[0, "starts at zero → peak → falls"], [1, "absorption phase: rate in &gt; rate out"], [2, "peak (C<sub>max</sub> at t<sub>max</sub>): rate in = rate out"], [3, "post-absorption: rate out &gt; rate in, still absorbing"], [4, "elimination phase: rate out only"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She shows a curve and asks for the route. A clear peak, up and back down, means oral input, first order in and first order out. An I V bolus profile starts at its highest point and falls, and an I V infusion profile starts at zero and levels off to a plateau. Oral input is usually first order, not always: a modified release product that releases drug at a zero order rate gives a curve shaped like an I V infusion.", "s": "oral:22", "c": [[2, "peak → oral"], [3, "highest at start → IV bolus &nbsp; plateau → IV infusion"], [4, "modified release, zero order in → infusion shape"]]}], "mins": 4}, "gobj-m5-2": {"title": "Plasma concentration after a single oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective two. Plasma concentration after a single oral dose. One equation gives the plasma concentration, C p, at any time after a single oral dose, as long as you know F, the dose, V D, k a and k. F is the fraction of the dose absorbed, V D the volume of distribution, k a the absorption rate constant, and k the elimination rate constant. C p comes out in milligrams per liter, the dose is in milligrams, V D is in liters, both rate constants are per hour, and t is the hours since the dose. It applies to a single oral dose in one compartment, with first order absorption and first order elimination, and it is on the equation sheet, page one.", "s": "oral:10", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}} &times; (e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"], [3, "F: fraction absorbed &nbsp; V<sub>D</sub>: volume of distribution"], [3, "k<sub>a</sub>: absorption &nbsp; k: elimination"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> mg/L, D<sub>0</sub> mg, V<sub>D</sub> L, k<sub>a</sub> and k hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, t hr"]]}, {"t": "Inside the bracket, e to the minus k t is drug going out, and the bracket as a whole is drug in minus drug out. At time zero the bracket is e to the zero minus e to the zero, which is zero, so the oral curve starts at zero concentration and has to rise. The fraction in front is not C zero, a starting concentration. It lumps F, the dose, k a, V D and the difference of the rate constants into one number. She said, this big portion right here does not represent C zero.", "s": "oral:10", "c": [[0, "bracket = drug in &minus; drug out"], [1, "t = 0: e<sup>0</sup> &minus; e<sup>0</sup> = 0, so the curve starts at zero"], [2, "{{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}} is not C<sub>0</sub>"]]}, {"t": "In her problems the equation often arrives with that fraction already worked out as one leading number, P, in milligrams per liter. Her slide Example two gives C p equals seventy five e to the minus zero point two two t, minus seventy five e to the minus two point seven five t, so P is seventy five. The larger exponent is k a, two point seven five per hour, and the smaller is k, zero point two two per hour. To find V D, set P equal to the fraction and rearrange. V D equals F times k a times the dose, divided by P times k a minus k. That line is the page one equation rearranged, and V D comes out in liters.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 75e<sup>&minus;0.22t</sup> &minus; 75e<sup>&minus;2.75t</sup> &nbsp; P = 75"], [2, "larger: k<sub>a</sub> = 2.75 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &nbsp; smaller: k = 0.22 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "P = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}}"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|P (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}}"]]}, {"t": "Her in class practice two. A seven hundred fifty milligram oral dose, eighty four percent bioavailable, gives C p equals twenty three point two, times e to the minus zero point one eight two t minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, in milligrams per liter with t in hours. Find V D and the half-life of elimination. First read the rate constants off the exponents. The larger is k a, zero point eight seven two per hour, and the smaller is k, zero point one eight two per hour. The eighty four percent is used as a decimal, so F is zero point eight four.", "s": "oral:10", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 23.2(e<sup>&minus;0.182t</sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;0.872t</sup>) mg/L &nbsp; D<sub>0</sub> = 750 mg"], [2, "find: V<sub>D</sub> and t&frac12;"], [4, "k<sub>a</sub> = 0.872 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &nbsp; k = 0.182 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [5, "F = 84% = 0.84"]]}, {"t": "Set the leading number equal to the fraction. Twenty three point two milligrams per liter equals zero point eight four, times seven hundred fifty milligrams, times zero point eight seven two per hour, all divided by V D times zero point eight seven two minus zero point one eight two per hour. That difference is zero point six nine zero per hour. Rearrange so V D stands alone, with twenty three point two moving to the bottom.", "s": "oral:10", "c": [[1, "23.2 mg/L = {{frac:(0.84)(750 mg)(0.872 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)|V<sub>D</sub> (0.872 &minus; 0.182) hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [2, "0.872 &minus; 0.182 = 0.690 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:(0.84)(750 mg)(0.872 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)|(23.2 mg/L)(0.690 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)}}"]]}, {"t": "The top is zero point eight four times seven hundred fifty milligrams times zero point eight seven two per hour, which is five hundred forty nine point three six. The bottom is twenty three point two milligrams per liter times zero point six nine zero per hour, which is sixteen point zero zero eight. The per hour cancels, and milligrams divided by milligrams per liter leaves liters. Five hundred forty nine point three six divided by sixteen point zero zero eight is thirty four point three two liters, which she reports as thirty four point three liters.", "s": "oral:10", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:549.36|16.008}} L"], [2, "{{frac:mg|mg/L}} = L"], [3, "= 34.32 L &rarr; <b>V<sub>D</sub> = 34.3 L</b>"]]}, {"t": "The second answer is the half-life. With no qualifier, half-life means the elimination half-life, so it uses k, the smaller exponent. t half equals zero point six nine three divided by k. She says this one is never on the equation sheet. Zero point six nine three divided by zero point one eight two per hour is three point eight hours, because one over per hour leaves hours.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[1, "no qualifier: elimination half-life, use k"], [2, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|k}}"], [4, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|0.182 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>3.8 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She gives the two exponential equation with the fraction already worked out and asks for something inside it, most often V D, with F and the dose supplied. She expects you to pick the larger exponent as k a without being told. Units are fixed in a sentence of the stem, such as assume units of micrograms per milliliter for C p and hours for time, and milligrams per liter is numerically the same. In her slide Example two, five hundred milligrams at eighty seven percent bioavailability gives a V D of six point three liters. The usual traps are reading the leading number as C zero, leaving out V D, and losing the k a minus k term.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[2, "larger exponent = k<sub>a</sub>"], [3, "mg/L = mcg/mL"], [4, "Example 2: D<sub>0</sub> = 500 mg, F = 0.87 &rarr; V<sub>D</sub> = 6.3 L"], [5, "✗ P read as C<sub>0</sub> &nbsp; ✗ V<sub>D</sub> left out &nbsp; ✗ (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k) lost"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m5-3": {"title": "Peak concentration and time to peak after one oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective three. The peak concentration and the time to peak after a single oral dose. t max is the time after the dose at which the plasma concentration is highest, and C max is that highest concentration. At the peak, the rate in equals the rate out. t max depends only on the two rate constants, k a and k. The dose, F and V D do not appear, so changing the dose does not move t max.", "s": "oral:12", "c": [[2, "t<sub>max</sub>: time of the peak (hr) &nbsp; C<sub>max</sub>: the peak (mg/L)"], [3, "at the peak: rate in = rate out"], [5, "t<sub>max</sub>: k<sub>a</sub> and k only, no dose"]]}, {"t": "t max equals the natural log of k a divided by k, all divided by k a minus k, and it is on the equation sheet, page one. With both constants per hour, t max comes out in hours. There is no separate C max equation for a single oral dose. You find t max, then put it in for t in the C p equation, which is why t max always comes first, even when only C max is asked. The sheet's other C max lines contain tau, the dosing interval, and belong to multiple dosing. Both equations need rate constants, not half-lives, each one zero point six nine three divided by the half-life of its own process.", "s": "oral:12", "c": [[0, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(k<sub>a</sub> &divide; k)|k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k}}"], [3, "C<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}} &times; (e<sup>&minus;k t<sub>max</sub></sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub> t<sub>max</sub></sup>)"], [4, "C<sub>max</sub> lines with &tau;: multiple dosing"], [5, "k<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|t&frac12;<sub>a</sub>}} &nbsp; k = {{frac:0.693|t&frac12;}}"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example one. V D is twenty two liters, the absorption half-life is forty five minutes, the elimination half-life is three hours, and the drug is eighty five percent bioavailable in one compartment. What is the peak after a single five hundred milligram dose, and when does it occur? First put both half-lives in hours, so forty five minutes is zero point seven five hours. k a is zero point six nine three divided by zero point seven five hours, which is zero point nine two four per hour. k is zero point six nine three divided by three hours, which is zero point two three one per hour.", "s": "oral:13", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub> = 22 L, t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> = 45 min, t&frac12; = 3 hr, F = 0.85"], [2, "D<sub>0</sub> = 500 mg; find t<sub>max</sub>, C<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "45 min = 0.75 hr"], [4, "k<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|0.75 hr}} = 0.924 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [5, "k = {{frac:0.693|3 hr}} = 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"]]}, {"t": "t max comes first. k a divided by k is zero point nine two four over zero point two three one, which is four, and the natural log of four is one point three eight six. The bottom is zero point nine two four minus zero point two three one, which is zero point six nine three per hour. One point three eight six divided by zero point six nine three per hour gives a t max of two hours. The ratio inside the log has no unit, so one over per hour leaves hours.", "s": "oral:12", "c": [[1, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(0.924 &divide; 0.231)|(0.924 &minus; 0.231) hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [2, "= {{frac:ln 4|0.693 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = {{frac:1.386|0.693}} hr"], [3, "<b>t<sub>max</sub> = 2 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Next the fraction in front, which needs V D. She said, Do not forget the volume of distribution. The top is zero point eight five times five hundred milligrams times zero point nine two four per hour, which is three hundred ninety two point seven. The bottom is twenty two liters times zero point six nine three per hour, which is fifteen point two four six. The per hour cancels, leaving milligrams per liter. Three hundred ninety two point seven divided by fifteen point two four six is twenty five point seven six milligrams per liter.", "s": "oral:13", "c": [[0, "{{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub> (k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}}"], [2, "{{frac:(0.85)(500 mg)(0.924 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)|(22 L)(0.693 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)}}"], [3, "= {{frac:392.7|15.246}}"], [5, "= 25.76 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Then the bracket, with t equal to t max, two hours. e to the minus zero point two three one times two is zero point six three zero zero. e to the minus zero point nine two four times two is zero point one five seven six. The difference is zero point four seven two four, with no unit. C max is twenty five point seven six milligrams per liter times zero point four seven two four, which is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter, two hours after the dose.", "s": "oral:13", "c": [[1, "e<sup>&minus;(0.231)(2)</sup> = 0.6300"], [2, "e<sup>&minus;(0.924)(2)</sup> = 0.1576"], [3, "0.6300 &minus; 0.1576 = 0.4724"], [4, "C<sub>max</sub> = (25.76 mg/L)(0.4724) = <b>12.17 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "When the parameters come as a finished equation, she wants the same two answers, and the exponents give the constants. In her slide Example two, C p equals seventy five e to the minus zero point two two t minus seventy five e to the minus two point seven five t. So k a is two point seven five and k is zero point two two per hour, which gives a t max of one hour. Put t max into the given equation, and C max is fifty five point four milligrams per liter.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 75e<sup>&minus;0.22t</sup> &minus; 75e<sup>&minus;2.75t</sup>"], [2, "k<sub>a</sub> = 2.75, k = 0.22 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &rarr; t<sub>max</sub> = 1 hr"], [3, "C<sub>max</sub> = 75(e<sup>&minus;0.22</sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;2.75</sup>) = <b>55.4 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her in class practice two works the same way. Twenty three point two, times e to the minus zero point one eight two t minus e to the minus zero point eight seven two t, gives k a as zero point eight seven two and k as zero point one eight two per hour. Its t max is two point two seven hours, and its C max is twelve point one four milligrams per liter.", "s": "oral:12", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = 23.2(e<sup>&minus;0.182t</sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;0.872t</sup>) mg/L"], [1, "k<sub>a</sub> = 0.872, k = 0.182 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "t<sub>max</sub> = 2.27 hr &nbsp; C<sub>max</sub> = <b>12.14 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "The traps. The first is using a half-life where a rate constant belongs. In her practice one, the one point five hours is the absorption half-life, so k a is zero point six nine three divided by one point five hours, which is zero point four six two per hour. The absorption half-life is often given in minutes and the elimination half-life in hours, so the first step is converting to hours. The second trap is leaving V D out of the fraction in front. Practice one, with a V D of twenty liters, gives a t max of three point seven hours and a C max of about thirteen milligrams per liter.", "s": "oral:12", "c": [[1, "✗ half-life used as a rate constant"], [2, "k<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|1.5 hr}} = 0.462 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "minutes &rarr; hours first"], [4, "✗ V<sub>D</sub> left out of the fraction"], [5, "practice 1: t<sub>max</sub> = 3.7 hr, C<sub>max</sub> about 13 mg/L"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m5-4": {"title": "Changing parameters after an oral dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module five, objective four. Changing one parameter after an oral dose. The question is what happens to C max, t max and A U C, the area under the curve, when one parameter changes, and the answers are directions, not numbers. Two relations give every direction. A U C equals F times the dose divided by clearance, and clearance equals k times V D. k a appears in neither line, which is why changing k a leaves A U C about the same. t max equals the natural log of k a over k, divided by k a minus k. Only k a and k appear, with no dose, F or V D, so a dose change cannot move t max.", "s": "oral:18", "c": [[2, "one change &rarr; C<sub>max</sub>, t<sub>max</sub>, AUC: up, down or unchanged"], [4, "AUC = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl}} &nbsp; Cl = k &times; V<sub>D</sub>"], [5, "no k<sub>a</sub> in AUC"], [6, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:ln(k<sub>a</sub> &divide; k)|k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k}}"], [7, "no D<sub>0</sub>, F or V<sub>D</sub> in t<sub>max</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Raise the dose, and C max and A U C rise in proportion, while t max stays put. The rates rise because there is more drug, but the rate constants k and k a do not change. Her own question is, if the dose is increased, what happens to t max? Her answer is nothing, because t max is independent of dose.", "s": "oral:18", "c": [[0, "dose up: C<sub>max</sub> and AUC up in proportion, t<sub>max</sub> unchanged"], [1, "rates up; k and k<sub>a</sub> unchanged"], [3, "t<sub>max</sub> independent of dose"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example one puts numbers on it. V D is twenty two liters, the absorption half-life forty five minutes, the elimination half-life three hours, and F is eighty five percent. For one five hundred milligram dose, t max is two hours and C max is twelve point one seven milligrams per liter, from objective three. Double the dose to one thousand milligrams. t max uses only k a and k, so it stays at two hours. The dose sits only in the fraction in front, so C max doubles. Twelve point one seven milligrams per liter, times one thousand over five hundred milligrams, is twenty four point three four milligrams per liter. A U C doubles as well.", "s": "oral:13", "c": [[2, "500 mg: t<sub>max</sub> = 2 hr, C<sub>max</sub> = 12.17 mg/L"], [3, "D<sub>0</sub>: 500 &rarr; 1000 mg"], [4, "t<sub>max</sub> = <b>2 hr</b>, unchanged"], [6, "C<sub>max</sub> = (12.17 mg/L)({{frac:1000 mg|500 mg}}) = <b>24.34 mg/L</b>"], [7, "AUC doubles"]]}, {"t": "Raise k a with k unchanged. The drug gets in faster, so C max is higher and t max earlier. A U C is relatively unchanged, because clearance has not changed. Her textbook table holds the dose at one hundred milligrams, V at ten liters, F at one, and k at zero point one per hour. Raising k a from zero point two to zero point six per hour moves t max from six point nine three to three point five eight hours. C max goes from five point zero zero to six point nine nine micrograms per milliliter, and A U C stays one hundred microgram hours per milliliter.", "s": "oral:19", "c": [[0, "k<sub>a</sub> up, k fixed"], [1, "C<sub>max</sub> up, t<sub>max</sub> earlier"], [2, "AUC about unchanged: Cl unchanged"], [4, "k<sub>a</sub> 0.2 &rarr; 0.6 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 6.93 &rarr; 3.58 hr"], [5, "C<sub>max</sub> 5.00 &rarr; 6.99 mcg/mL; AUC 100 mcg&middot;hr/mL both"]]}, {"t": "That A U C result cannot be read off the curves. She said, looking at the curve, you might not be able to really ascertain, but this comes from your textbook, and this is tabulated, and the A U C is relatively unchanged. So the trap is to read a change in A U C from the figure, when the table says it holds.", "s": "oral:19", "c": [[1, "k<sub>a</sub> change: AUC from the table, not the figure"], [2, "✗ AUC changes with k<sub>a</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Raise k with k a unchanged. The drug leaves faster, so C max is lower and t max earlier. A U C falls, because changing k changes the clearance. In the textbook table, with k a held at zero point three per hour, raising k from zero point one to zero point five per hour moves t max from five point four nine to two point five five hours. C max falls from five point seven seven to two point seven nine micrograms per milliliter, and A U C from one hundred to twenty microgram hours per milliliter.", "s": "oral:19", "c": [[0, "k up, k<sub>a</sub> fixed"], [1, "C<sub>max</sub> down, t<sub>max</sub> earlier"], [2, "AUC down: Cl = k &times; V<sub>D</sub> up"], [3, "k 0.1 &rarr; 0.5 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>: t<sub>max</sub> 5.49 &rarr; 2.55 hr"], [4, "C<sub>max</sub> 5.77 &rarr; 2.79 mcg/mL; AUC 100 &rarr; 20 mcg&middot;hr/mL"]]}, {"t": "Two terms say which process is slower. Disposition rate limiting, the usual case, means the absorption half-life is much shorter than the elimination half-life, so k a is much larger than k. Absorption rate limiting means the absorption half-life is much longer than the elimination half-life. She once said distribution limited for the usual case, but the term to use is disposition rate limiting. From the textbook, in an extended release product absorption is slower than elimination, so the tail of the curve shows absorption, not elimination, which is called flip flop.", "s": "oral:20", "c": [[1, "disposition rate limiting: t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> much shorter, k<sub>a</sub> much larger than k"], [2, "absorption rate limiting: t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> much longer"], [3, "✗ distribution limited"], [4, "flip-flop: the tail shows absorption"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Example two asks which case an antibiotic is. From its exponents, k a is two point seven five per hour and k is zero point two two per hour. The absorption half-life is zero point six nine three over two point seven five per hour, which is zero point two five two hours. The elimination half-life is zero point six nine three over zero point two two per hour, which is three point one five hours. Three point one five hours over zero point two five two hours is twelve point five, so absorption is twelve point five times faster. The drug is disposition rate limiting, the usual case.", "s": "oral:15", "c": [[1, "k<sub>a</sub> = 2.75 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &nbsp; k = 0.22 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "t&frac12;<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:0.693|2.75 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 0.252 hr"], [3, "t&frac12; = {{frac:0.693|0.22 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = 3.15 hr"], [4, "{{frac:3.15 hr|0.252 hr}} = 12.5"], [5, "<b>disposition rate limiting</b>"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. One parameter changes, and she asks about C max, t max and A U C, in that order. The rate limiting question gives two exponents to compare rather than two half-lives, and with e to the minus zero point one eight against e to the minus zero point eight seven, absorption is much faster than elimination. k a, t max and C max matter for designing multiple dose regimens, for bioequivalence, for choosing the route and dosage form, for the onset of action, and for correlating with the effect.", "s": "oral:21", "c": [[1, "order: C<sub>max</sub>, t<sub>max</sub>, AUC"], [2, "e<sup>&minus;0.18</sup> vs e<sup>&minus;0.87</sup>: absorption faster"], [3, "uses: regimens, bioequivalence, route and form, onset, effect"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m6-1": {"title": "Superposition and its assumptions", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective one. Superposition and its assumptions in multiple dose regimens. When the same dose is given at a fixed interval, tau, such as eight hours for three times a day, each new dose is added to what is left of the earlier doses, so the peaks and troughs climb. The peak is the highest concentration in an interval, and the trough is the lowest, just before the next dose. The climb stops at a plateau called steady state. Every dose then rises to the same peak and falls to the same trough, because the drug eliminated over one interval equals one dose.", "s": "rep:3", "c": [[2, "&tau;: time between doses; TID &rarr; &tau; = 8 hr"], [3, "peak = maximum &nbsp; trough = minimum, just before the next dose"], [5, "steady state: eliminated per &tau; = one dose"]]}, {"t": "Steady state is reached in three to five half-lives. That time depends on the half-life only, not on the dose, and the dose sets how high the plateau is. After the last dose, most of the drug is gone in another three to five half-lives. She said, not three to five doses, three to five half-lives. An infinity sign on a symbol means at steady state.", "s": "rep:3", "c": [[0, "steady state: 3 to 5 t&frac12;"], [1, "independent of dose; dose sets the plateau height"], [2, "after the last dose: most gone in 3 to 5 t&frac12;"], [4, "&infin; = at steady state"]]}, {"t": "Superposition says the concentration at any time is the sum of what is left of every dose given so far, each dose following its own single dose curve. It holds under two assumptions. First, elimination is first order, at a rate proportional to the amount present, with a constant k. Second, the pharmacokinetics after one dose are not altered by later doses, so the half-life and the clearance stay the same. Clearance is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time. She said, First order kinetics equals linear pharmacokinetics.", "s": "rep:4", "c": [[0, "C = sum of what is left of each dose"], [2, "1. first-order elimination"], [3, "2. t&frac12; and Cl unchanged by later doses"], [5, "first order = linear"]]}, {"t": "So the single dose decline, C equals C zero e to the minus k t, applies after every dose, at steady state as well as after the first, and only the starting value is higher. C zero is the concentration just after a dose, k the elimination rate constant, and t the time since that dose. The average concentration at steady state is the area under the curve over one dosing interval at steady state, divided by tau. The area is in milligram hours per liter and tau in hours, so the average is in milligrams per liter. That area form is not on the equation sheet.", "s": "rep:4", "c": [[0, "C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> after every dose"], [2, "C<sub>av</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:[AUC]<sub>t1</sub><sup>t2</sup>|&tau;}}"], [3, "{{frac:mg&middot;hr/L|hr}} = mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Her Example one regimen. The half-life is four hours, the volume of distribution, V D, is zero point two five liters per kilogram, and ten milligrams per kilogram is given by I V bolus every eight hours. What is the peak just after the second dose, and when is steady state reached? First, the peak after the first dose is the dose divided by V D. Ten milligrams per kilogram over zero point two five liters per kilogram is forty milligrams per liter, because the kilograms cancel.", "s": "rep:9", "c": [[1, "t&frac12; = 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 0.25 L/kg, 10 mg/kg every 8 hr"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:dose|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [4, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:10 mg/kg|0.25 L/kg}} = 40 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Now let the first dose fall for one interval. Eight hours is two half-lives, so it halves twice, from forty to twenty to ten milligrams per liter. The second dose adds its own forty milligrams per liter on top of what is left of the first, which is superposition. Forty plus ten gives fifty milligrams per liter just after the second dose. Time to steady state is three to five half-lives. Three times four hours is twelve hours, and five times four hours is twenty hours, so steady state is reached within twelve to twenty hours.", "s": "rep:9", "c": [[1, "8 hr = 2 t&frac12;: 40 &rarr; 20 &rarr; 10 mg/L"], [3, "40 + 10 = <b>50 mg/L</b> after dose 2"], [5, "3 &times; 4 hr = 12 hr to 5 &times; 4 hr = 20 hr"], [5, "<b>steady state within 12 to 20 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "The same reasoning gives the steady state peak. Call it P, the new dose's forty milligrams per liter plus the trough left from earlier doses. That trough is one quarter of the previous peak, because one interval is two half-lives of decline. So P equals forty plus zero point two five P. Solving, P is forty milligrams per liter divided by zero point seven five, which is fifty three point three milligrams per liter. The steady state trough is fifty three point three times zero point two five, which is thirteen point three milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:11", "c": [[1, "P = 40 mg/L + trough"], [2, "trough = 0.25 P"], [3, "P = 40 + 0.25P"], [4, "P = {{frac:40 mg/L|0.75}} = <b>53.3 mg/L</b>"], [5, "trough = 53.3 &times; 0.25 = <b>13.3 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "From the textbook, the accumulation factor R equals one divided by one minus e to the minus k tau. It depends only on k and tau, not on the dose. R is two when tau equals one half-life, and there is little accumulation when tau is three half-lives or more. Doubling the dose doubles every level but leaves R unchanged. Accumulation happens because the next dose arrives before the last one is completely eliminated, and a longer interval than that gives none.", "s": "rep:3", "c": [[0, "R = {{frac:1|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}}"], [1, "k and &tau; only, no dose"], [2, "&tau; = t&frac12; &rarr; R = 2; &tau; of 3 t&frac12; or more &rarr; little"], [3, "dose &times; 2: levels &times; 2, R unchanged"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. She asks for the two assumptions, first order elimination and pharmacokinetics unchanged by later doses, and the second is the one that names half-life and clearance. She asks how long steady state takes, and the answer is three to five half-lives, while three to five doses, or it depends on the dose, are the two wrong answers she argues against. First order and linear mean the same thing in her questions. The textbook lists what breaks superposition: changing pathophysiology in the patient, saturation of a drug carrier system, enzyme induction or inhibition, and nonlinear pharmacokinetics in general.", "s": "rep:4", "c": [[1, "first order; t&frac12; and Cl unchanged"], [2, "✓ 3 to 5 t&frac12; &nbsp; ✗ 3 to 5 doses &nbsp; ✗ depends on dose"], [4, "breaks it: pathophysiology, carrier saturation, induction or inhibition, nonlinear PK"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m6-2": {"title": "Concentrations after multiple IV bolus doses", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective two. Concentrations at any time after multiple I V bolus doses. After one I V bolus, the drug falls by first order elimination. The fraction still in the body when the next dose is due is e to the minus k tau, with k the elimination rate constant and tau the dosing interval, the time between doses. So the amount left one interval after a dose, D B, is the dose, D zero, times e to the minus k tau. That leftover is why drug accumulates. Tau is the interval, not the frequency, so three times a day, T I D, is a tau of eight hours.", "s": "rep:6", "c": [[3, "fraction left after &tau;: e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>"], [4, "D<sub>B</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>"], [6, "TID &rarr; &tau; = 8 hr"]]}, {"t": "At steady state, every peak equals the first dose peak times an accumulation factor, r, that holds only k and tau. r equals one divided by one minus e to the minus k tau, and k tau has no units. A shorter interval makes it larger, and the dose does not change it. The first dose peak, C zero, is the dose divided by V D. So C max at steady state is C zero over one minus e to the minus k tau, and it must come out higher than C zero. The trough at steady state is one interval of decline later, so C min equals C max times e to the minus k tau.", "s": "rep:8", "c": [[1, "r = {{frac:1|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}}"], [3, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [4, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}}, higher than C<sub>0</sub>"], [5, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>"]]}, {"t": "The average at steady state is not the midpoint of peak and trough, because the decline is exponential. She said, I want you to realize that the average is not the max plus the min divided by two. C average at steady state equals F times the dose, divided by V D times k times tau. Since total clearance, Cl T, is V D times k, that is also F times the dose over Cl T times tau. F is one for I V dosing, and no exponential or accumulation factor is needed. The amount forms, D max, D min and D average, divided by V D give the matching concentrations.", "s": "rep:8", "c": [[1, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> is not {{frac:max + min|2}}"], [2, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:FD<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>k&tau;}}"], [3, "= {{frac:FD<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>&tau;}}"], [4, "IV: F = 1"], [5, "C = {{frac:D|V<sub>D</sub>}} for max, min and avg"]]}, {"t": "Her Example one. An antibiotic has a half-life of about four hours and a V D of twenty five percent of body weight. Ten milligrams per kilogram is given every eight hours by I V bolus to a sixty five kilogram female. Find the maximum, minimum and average at steady state. With one liter taken as one kilogram of body weight, V D is zero point two five liters per kilogram. C zero is ten milligrams per kilogram over zero point two five liters per kilogram, forty milligrams per liter, because the kilograms cancel.", "s": "rep:9", "c": [[1, "t&frac12; = 4 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 25% of body weight"], [2, "10 mg/kg every 8 hr, 65 kg"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.25 L/kg"], [5, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:10 mg/kg|0.25 L/kg}} = 40 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Next, k is zero point six nine three over four hours, which is zero point one seven three three per hour. k tau is zero point one seven three three per hour times eight hours, which is one point three eight six, with no units. e to the minus one point three eight six is zero point two five, so a quarter of each dose is left after one interval. The steady state peak is forty milligrams per liter over one minus zero point two five. That is forty over zero point seven five, fifty three point three milligrams per liter, above the first dose peak of forty, as her check requires.", "s": "rep:11", "c": [[0, "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.1733 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [1, "k&tau; = (0.1733 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(8 hr) = 1.386"], [2, "e<sup>&minus;1.386</sup> = 0.25"], [3, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:40 mg/L|1 &minus; 0.25}}"], [4, "= {{frac:40 mg/L|0.75}} = <b>53.3 mg/L</b> ✓ above 40"]]}, {"t": "The steady state trough is one interval later. Fifty three point three milligrams per liter times zero point two five is thirteen point three milligrams per liter. For the average, F is one, the dose over V D is forty milligrams per liter, and k tau is one point three eight six. Forty divided by one point three eight six is twenty eight point nine milligrams per liter. That is below the midpoint of peak and trough, thirty three point three milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:13", "c": [[1, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = (53.3 mg/L)(0.25) = <b>13.3 mg/L</b>"], [2, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:(1)(40 mg/L)|1.386}}"], [3, "<b>C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = 28.9 mg/L</b>"], [4, "midpoint 33.3 mg/L, not the average"]]}, {"t": "Before steady state, a dose number, n, tracks how far the climb has gone. The level after n doses is the dose over V D, times the build up after n doses, times the decay since dose n. The build up is one minus e to the minus n k tau, over one minus e to the minus k tau. The decay is e to the minus k t, with t the time since dose n, not since the first dose. With f the fraction left after one interval, the body just after dose n holds one plus f plus f squared, up to f to the n minus one doses, and that sum is the build up. As n grows, the build up becomes r.", "s": "rep:14", "c": [[1, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} &times; build-up &times; decay"], [2, "build-up = {{frac:1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;nk&tau;</sup>|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}}"], [3, "decay = e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>, t since dose n"], [4, "1 + f + f<sup>2</sup> + &hellip; + f<sup>n&minus;1</sup> = {{frac:1 &minus; f<sup>n</sup>|1 &minus; f}}"], [5, "n large: build-up &rarr; r"]]}, {"t": "Her Example two asks the level three hours after the second dose. Two doses is not steady state, so n is two, t is three hours, and the first part is forty milligrams per liter. The build up is one minus e to the minus two times one point three eight six, over one minus zero point two five. That is one minus zero point zero six two five, over zero point seven five, which is one point two five. The decay is e to the minus zero point one seven three three times three, or zero point five nine four five. Forty times one point two five times zero point five nine four five is twenty nine point seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:16", "c": [[1, "n = 2, t = 3 hr, {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} = 40 mg/L"], [2, "{{frac:1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;2(1.386)</sup>|1 &minus; 0.25}}"], [3, "= {{frac:1 &minus; 0.0625|0.75}} = 1.25"], [4, "e<sup>&minus;(0.1733)(3)</sup> = e<sup>&minus;0.520</sup> = 0.5945"], [5, "C<sub>p</sub> = (40 mg/L)(1.25)(0.5945) = <b>29.7 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Within a steady state interval, and after the last dose, the level just falls from the steady state peak, so C p equals C max at steady state times e to the minus k t. She prefers this route, C zero e to the minus k t started from the steady state peak, to searching the sheet for a matching line. Her vignettes run from the first dose to steady state, a time before it and a time after the last dose. Dose and volume may come per kilogram or scaled from a per seventy kilogram value, so put both on the same basis first.", "s": "rep:17", "c": [[0, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [1, "start from C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>, then C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>"], [2, "first dose &rarr; steady state &rarr; n doses &rarr; after the last dose"], [3, "dose and V<sub>D</sub> on the same basis"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m6-3": {"title": "Concentrations after repeated IV infusions", "segs": [{"t": "Module six, objective three. Concentrations after repeated intermittent I V infusions. An intermittent I V infusion gives each dose slowly over a set time instead of all at once, which prevents high concentrations and the side effects that go with them. No new equation is needed. During each infusion the level rises by the single infusion equation from Module three. When it stops, the level falls by first order elimination, where a fixed fraction of the drug present is removed per unit time.", "s": "rep:21", "c": [[2, "slow infusion: no high concentrations, fewer side effects"], [4, "during: single-infusion equation, Module 3"], [5, "after: first-order fall"]]}, {"t": "The infusion rate, R, is the dose divided by the infusion time, in milligrams per hour. It always comes first, because the stem gives a dose and a duration while the equation needs a rate. During an infusion, C p equals R over V D times k, times one minus e to the minus k t. V D times k is the clearance in liters per hour, so R over V D k is in milligrams per liter. That is C s s, the plateau a continuous infusion at rate R would reach, and short infusions stop well below it. Here t is the time the pump has run, never the spacing between infusions.", "s": "rep:22", "c": [[0, "R = {{frac:dose|infusion time}} (mg/hr)"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = {{frac:R|V<sub>D</sub>k}} (1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>)"], [3, "{{frac:mg/hr|L/hr}} = mg/L"], [4, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"], [5, "t = infusion time only"]]}, {"t": "The same drug at the same rate for the same duration reaches the same end of infusion concentration, C end, every time, and that value is the starting point for the fall. She said, So that C zero is gonna be the concentration at the end of the infusion, right? After an infusion stops, C equals C end times e to the minus k t, with t the time since that infusion ended, not since it started. Because elimination is first order, the contributions of separate infusions add, which is superposition. For two infusions, C equals C end e to the minus k t one, plus C end e to the minus k t two.", "s": "rep:22", "c": [[0, "C<sub>end</sub> = the C<sub>0</sub> of the fall"], [2, "C = C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>, t since that infusion ended"], [3, "first order &rarr; contributions add"], [4, "C = C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt<sub>1</sub></sup> + C<sub>end</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt<sub>2</sub></sup>"]]}, {"t": "Her Example four. Three hundred milligrams is infused over two hours, and a second three hundred milligrams starts six hours after the first started, again over two hours. k is zero point one five per hour and V D is fifteen liters. Find the level at the end of the first infusion, and four hours after the second infusion stops. First, R is three hundred milligrams over two hours, one hundred fifty milligrams per hour. The clearance, V D k, is fifteen liters times zero point one five per hour, two point two five liters per hour. One hundred fifty over two point two five is sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:23", "c": [[1, "300 mg over 2 hr; second 300 mg starts at 6 hr"], [2, "k = 0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, V<sub>D</sub> = 15 L"], [4, "R = {{frac:300 mg|2 hr}} = 150 mg/hr"], [5, "V<sub>D</sub>k = (15 L)(0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>) = 2.25 L/hr"], [6, "{{frac:150 mg/hr|2.25 L/hr}} = 66.67 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Next, the fraction of that plateau reached in two hours, with t equal to the infusion time. One minus e to the minus zero point one five times two is one minus zero point seven four zero eight, which is zero point two five nine two. So at the end of the first infusion, C p is sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter times zero point two five nine two, which is seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter. That answers part a. The second infusion is identical, so it also ends at seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter.", "s": "rep:24", "c": [[1, "1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;(0.15)(2)</sup> = 1 &minus; 0.7408 = 0.2592"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> = (66.67 mg/L)(0.2592) = <b>17.28 mg/L</b>"], [4, "infusion 2 also ends at 17.28 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "For part b, draw a number line of start and stop times. Infusion one runs from zero to two hours, and infusion two runs from six to eight hours. The time asked for is eight plus four, which is twelve hours. Give each infusion its own t, counted from the end of that infusion. For the first, twelve minus two is ten hours, and for the second, twelve minus eight is four hours.", "s": "rep:25", "c": [[1, "infusion 1: 0 to 2 hr &nbsp; infusion 2: 6 to 8 hr"], [2, "asked: 8 + 4 = 12 hr"], [4, "t<sub>1</sub> = 12 &minus; 2 = 10 hr &nbsp; t<sub>2</sub> = 12 &minus; 8 = 4 hr"]]}, {"t": "The first infusion leaves seventeen point two eight milligrams per liter times e to the minus zero point one five times ten. That is seventeen point two eight times zero point two two three one, or three point eight six milligrams per liter. The second leaves seventeen point two eight times e to the minus zero point one five times four. That is seventeen point two eight times zero point five four eight eight, or nine point four eight milligrams per liter. Adding them, three point eight six plus nine point four eight is thirteen point three four milligrams per liter. Her stated answer is thirteen point three three, and the difference is rounding.", "s": "rep:25", "c": [[0, "17.28 e<sup>&minus;(0.15)(10)</sup>"], [1, "= 17.28 &times; 0.2231 = 3.86 mg/L"], [2, "17.28 e<sup>&minus;(0.15)(4)</sup>"], [3, "= 17.28 &times; 0.5488 = 9.48 mg/L"], [4, "C = 3.86 + 9.48 = <b>13.34 mg/L</b>"], [5, "her answer: 13.33 mg/L, rounding"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Her stems give two infusions of the same dose and duration, the second starting a stated number of hours after the first starts. The elimination parameter arrives as k, as a half-life, or as a clearance with a volume, so convert to k and to R before anything else. The usual trap is using the same t for both infusions, when each t runs from the end of its own infusion. The second trap is putting the six hour spacing into the infusion equation, whose t is the infusion time only. A closing part can ask what a continuous infusion at the same rate would reach, which is R over Cl.", "s": "rep:26", "c": [[2, "k, t&frac12;, or Cl with V<sub>D</sub> &rarr; k and R first"], [3, "✗ same t for both infusions"], [4, "✗ 6 hr spacing in the infusion equation"], [5, "continuous: C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:R|Cl}}"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m6a-1": {"title": "Concentrations after multiple oral doses", "segs": [{"t": "Module six A, objective one. Concentrations after multiple oral doses. Each oral dose has to be absorbed first, so the level rises to a peak and then falls. When the next dose arrives before the last is gone, drug accumulates until every interval repeats, which is steady state. The single dose line from Module five is C p equals P, times e to the minus k t minus e to the minus k a t. The prefactor P is F times k a times D zero, over V D times k a minus k. The oral peak is not at the moment of dosing, so t max is found first. t max equals one over k a minus k, times the natural log of k a over k.", "s": "mo:3", "c": [[2, "oral: absorbed first → rise to a peak, then fall"], [3, "accumulation → steady state (&infin;)"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> = P (e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &minus; e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"], [5, "P = {{frac:F k<sub>a</sub> D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>(k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k)}} &nbsp; (mg/L)"], [6, "peak not at the moment of dosing → t<sub>max</sub> first"], [7, "t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:1|k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k}} ln({{frac:k<sub>a</sub>|k}})"]]}, {"t": "Repeating the dose adds the dose number n and the dosing interval tau. With n equal to one, the extra fractions equal one and the single dose line is left. As n grows, they become two accumulation factors. r k equals one over one minus e to the minus k tau, and r a is the same with k a. Both are at least one, because e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose still in the body one interval later. At steady state, C p equals P, times r k e to the minus k t, minus r a e to the minus k a t, with t the time after the latest dose.", "s": "mo:5", "c": [[0, "new: n (dose number), &tau; (interval, hr)"], [1, "n = 1 → single-dose line"], [3, "r<sub>k</sub> = {{frac:1|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}} &nbsp; r<sub>a</sub> = {{frac:1|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub>&tau;</sup>}}"], [4, "e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>: fraction left after one interval; r ≥ 1"], [5, "C<sub>p</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = P (r<sub>k</sub> e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> &minus; r<sub>a</sub> e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub>t</sup>)"]]}, {"t": "The steady state peak needs its own time to peak. t max at steady state is the single dose t max with one more fraction inside the log: one minus e to the minus k tau, over one minus e to the minus k a tau. That correction is less than one, so t max at steady state is shorter than the first dose t max. It holds tau, so a new dosing interval means a new t max at steady state. Putting the first dose t max into the steady state peak is a trap she uses.", "s": "mo:7", "c": [[1, "t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:1|k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k}} ln[ {{frac:k<sub>a</sub>|k}} × {{frac:1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k<sub>a</sub>&tau;</sup>}} ]"], [2, "correction &lt; 1 → t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> &lt; t<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "depends on k, k<sub>a</sub> and &tau;"], [4, "✗ first-dose t<sub>max</sub> in C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>"]]}, {"t": "The peak at steady state, C max, equals F D zero over V D, times r k, times e to the minus k t max at steady state. F D zero over V D is the absorbed dose spread through V D, and r k adds drug left from earlier doses. F and t max mark this as the oral line, not the repeated bolus peak. The trough, C min, is P times r k times e to the minus k tau, and it starts from P, not from F D zero over V D. The average, C avg, equals F D zero over Cl T times tau, with no t max and no accumulation factor.", "s": "mo:6", "c": [[0, "C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}} × r<sub>k</sub> × e<sup>&minus;kt<sub>max</sub>&infin;</sup>"], [2, "F and t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> mark the oral line"], [3, "C<sub>min</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = P × r<sub>k</sub> × e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup> &nbsp; (P, not {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}})"], [4, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>&tau;}} = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>k&tau;}}"]]}, {"t": "Her Example one. A seventy five kilogram man takes two hundred fifty milligrams of tetracycline hydrochloride by mouth every eight hours. F is about seventy five percent, V D one point five liters per kilogram, the half life about ten hours, and k a zero point nine per hour. She asks the first dose peak, then the steady state peak, trough and average. Convert first. k is zero point six nine three over ten hours, zero point zero six nine three per hour. V D is one point five liters per kilogram times seventy five kilograms, one hundred twelve point five liters, because the kilograms cancel. F is zero point seven five and tau is eight hours.", "s": "mo:8", "c": [[1, "75 kg; 250 mg by mouth every 8 hr"], [2, "F 75%, V<sub>D</sub> 1.5 L/kg, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> 10 hr, k<sub>a</sub> 0.9 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [5, "k = {{frac:0.693|10 hr}} = 0.0693 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [6, "V<sub>D</sub> = 1.5 L/kg × 75 kg = 112.5 L"], [7, "F = 0.75, &tau; = 8 hr"]]}, {"t": "First dose t max. k a minus k is zero point eight three zero seven per hour. k a over k is twelve point nine nine, and its natural log is two point five six four. Divided by zero point eight three zero seven, t max is three point zero nine hours, which she writes as three point one. The top of P is zero point seven five times zero point nine times two hundred fifty milligrams, one hundred sixty eight point seven five. The bottom is one hundred twelve point five liters times zero point eight three zero seven, ninety three point four five. The per hour units cancel, and P is one point eight zero six milligrams per liter.", "s": "mo:8", "c": [[1, "k<sub>a</sub> &minus; k = 0.9 &minus; 0.0693 = 0.8307 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [2, "ln({{frac:0.9|0.0693}}) = ln(12.99) = 2.564"], [3, "<b>t<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:2.564|0.8307}} = 3.09 hr</b>"], [4, "P = {{frac:(0.75)(0.9)(250 mg)|(112.5 L)(0.8307)}}"], [5, "= {{frac:168.75|93.45}}"], [6, "<b>P = 1.806 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "At three point zero nine hours, e to the minus k t is zero point eight zero seven, and e to the minus k a t is zero point zero six two. One point eight zero six times their difference, zero point seven four five, is one point three five milligrams per liter. For steady state, e to the minus k tau is zero point five seven four, so one minus it is zero point four two six and r k is two point three five. That is more than double, because less than one half life passes between doses. e to the minus k a tau is zero point zero zero zero seven, leaving zero point nine nine nine, so r a is one.", "s": "mo:8", "c": [[0, "e<sup>&minus;0.0693 × 3.09</sup> = 0.807; &nbsp; e<sup>&minus;0.9 × 3.09</sup> = 0.062"], [1, "<b>C<sub>max</sub> = 1.806 × (0.807 &minus; 0.062) = 1.806 × 0.745 = 1.35 mg/L</b>"], [2, "e<sup>&minus;0.0693 × 8</sup> = 0.574"], [2, "1 &minus; 0.574 = 0.426 → r<sub>k</sub> = {{frac:1|0.426}} = 2.35"], [4, "e<sup>&minus;0.9 × 8</sup> = 0.0007 → 1 &minus; 0.0007 = 0.999 → r<sub>a</sub> = 1.00"]]}, {"t": "t max at steady state is the natural log of twelve point nine nine times zero point four two six over zero point nine nine nine, all over zero point eight three zero seven. That log is of five point five three nine, one point seven one two, so t max at steady state is two point zero six hours, about an hour earlier than the first dose. For C max at steady state, F D zero over V D is one point six six seven milligrams per liter. Times r k, two point three five, times e to the minus k times two point zero six, zero point eight six seven, that gives three point four zero milligrams per liter.", "s": "mo:8", "c": [[0, "t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:1|0.8307}} ln[12.99 × {{frac:0.426|0.999}}]"], [1, "ln(5.539) = 1.712"], [1, "<b>t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:1.712|0.8307}} = 2.06 hr</b>"], [2, "{{frac:(0.75)(250 mg)|112.5 L}} = 1.667 mg/L"], [3, "<b>C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = 1.667 × 2.35 × e<sup>&minus;0.0693 × 2.06</sup> = 1.667 × 2.35 × 0.867 = 3.40 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "C min at steady state is one point eight zero six times two point three five times zero point five seven four, two point four four milligrams per liter. For C avg, the top is one hundred eighty seven point five milligrams. The bottom is one hundred twelve point five liters times zero point zero six nine three per hour times eight hours, sixty two point three seven liters, because the hours cancel. So C avg is three point zero one milligrams per liter. Check: the steady state peak is higher and earlier than the first dose peak, and even the trough is above it. The average is a time average, not the midpoint, and here sits above the midpoint, two point nine two milligrams per liter.", "s": "mo:8", "c": [[0, "<b>C<sub>min</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = 1.806 × 2.35 × 0.574 = 2.44 mg/L</b>"], [1, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:(0.75)(250 mg)|(112.5 L)(0.0693 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(8 hr)}}"], [2, "= {{frac:187.5 mg|62.37 L}}"], [3, "<b>C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = 3.01 mg/L</b>"], [4, "3.40 &gt; 1.35 mg/L, 2.06 &lt; 3.09 hr; trough 2.44 &gt; 1.35 ✓"], [5, "time average: 3.01 &gt; midpoint {{frac:3.40 + 2.44|2}} = 2.92 mg/L"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m6a-2": {"title": "Changing the dose or the dosing interval", "segs": [{"t": "Module six A, objective two. Changing the dose or the dosing interval. A dosage regimen is the dose size, D zero, plus the dosing interval, tau, the time between doses. These are the only two things a prescriber adjusts. Clearance, half life, the elimination rate constant and the volume of distribution belong to the drug and the patient, so a regimen does not change them. Changing the dose or the interval moves the steady state levels and the swing between peak and trough. Neither change alters how long steady state takes to reach, because the half life alone sets that.", "s": "mo:11", "c": [[2, "regimen = D<sub>0</sub> + &tau;"], [4, "fixed by drug and patient: Cl<sub>T</sub>, t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, k, V<sub>D</sub>"], [6, "time to steady state: t<sub>&frac12;</sub> alone"]]}, {"t": "The average at steady state shows which way each change moves the level. C avg at steady state equals F D zero over Cl T times tau. D zero is on top, so a larger dose at the same interval raises the steady state level. Tau is underneath, so a longer interval at the same dose lowers it. The units are milligrams over liters per hour times hours, which leaves milligrams per liter.", "s": "mo:12", "c": [[1, "C<sub>avg</sub><sup>&infin;</sup> = {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>&tau;}}"], [2, "D<sub>0</sub> ↑ → level ↑"], [3, "&tau; ↑ → level ↓"], [4, "{{frac:mg|(L/hr)(hr)}} = mg/L"]]}, {"t": "The interval also sits in the accumulation factor, r k, which equals one over one minus e to the minus k tau. e to the minus k tau is the fraction of a dose still in the body one interval later. A longer tau makes that fraction smaller, because more of each dose is eliminated before the next, so r k is smaller and less accumulates. The trough is the peak level carried down through the rest of the interval, so a longer tau lets it fall further and the swing gets larger. Tau is also inside t max at steady state, so a new interval means recalculating the steady state t max and peak. A new dose changes neither the first dose t max nor the steady state one.", "s": "mo:15", "c": [[0, "r<sub>k</sub> = {{frac:1|1 &minus; e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup>}}"], [2, "&tau; ↑ → e<sup>&minus;k&tau;</sup> ↓ → r<sub>k</sub> ↓"], [3, "&tau; ↑ → trough falls further → swing ↑"], [4, "new &tau; → new t<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>, C<sub>max</sub><sup>&infin;</sup>"], [5, "new D<sub>0</sub> → neither t<sub>max</sub> changes"]]}, {"t": "Her question to the room. Five hundred milligrams every four hours is changed to five hundred milligrams every eight hours. What is the expectation at steady state? The dose stays the same, and the interval doubles. Tau is in the denominator of the average, so doubling tau halves the average, and the level goes down. Each dose now has eight hours instead of four to be eliminated, so the trough falls further below the peak. Every eight hours is three doses a day instead of six, which is easier to keep to. So the answer is a lower steady state concentration, a larger fluctuation, and better patient compliance.", "s": "mo:15", "c": [[1, "500 mg every 4 hr → 500 mg every 8 hr"], [4, "2&tau; in {{frac:F D<sub>0</sub>|Cl<sub>T</sub>&tau;}} → average halved"], [5, "8 hr to eliminate, not 4 → swing ↑"], [6, "3 doses a day, not 6 → compliance ↑"], [7, "<b>lower level, larger fluctuation, better compliance</b>"]]}, {"t": "The same three questions apply to the dose. A larger dose at the same interval gives higher concentrations and a larger fluctuation, with usually no change in compliance. A smaller dose gives the reverse, and compliance again is unchanged. The trap is pairing a higher level with a smaller swing for a larger dose, because a larger dose raises both the level and the swing.", "s": "mo:14", "c": [[1, "dose ↑: level ↑, fluctuation ↑, compliance usually unchanged"], [2, "dose ↓: the reverse"], [3, "✗ larger dose with a smaller swing"]]}, {"t": "For the interval, a longer one gives lower concentrations, a larger fluctuation and better compliance. A shorter one gives higher concentrations, a smaller fluctuation and worse compliance. Smaller fluctuation does not mean lower peaks or troughs, because with a shorter interval the peak, the trough and the average all rise, and the trough sits closer to the peak. Another trap is expecting a longer interval to raise the level because each dose has more time to be absorbed. Absorption is complete either way, and the extra time goes to elimination.", "s": "mo:18", "c": [[0, "&tau; ↑: level ↓, fluctuation ↑, compliance ↑"], [1, "&tau; ↓: level ↑, fluctuation ↓, compliance ↓"], [2, "&tau; ↓: C<sub>max</sub>, C<sub>min</sub>, C<sub>avg</sub> all ↑"], [4, "✗ longer &tau; → more absorbed; extra time → elimination"]]}, {"t": "Her figure gives equal doses every six hours and every eight hours, with k a and k the same. The six hour curve has the higher plateau, and both reach steady state in the same time, because the half life alone sets that. The six hour curve also swings less, because the next dose arrives before the three to five half lives the level would need to reach the bottom of the curve.", "s": "mo:17", "c": [[0, "equal doses: every 6 hr vs every 8 hr; same k<sub>a</sub>, k"], [1, "6 hr: higher plateau; same time to steady state"], [2, "next dose before 3 to 5 t<sub>&frac12;</sub> → smaller swing"]]}, {"t": "The peak and the trough, not the average, are the values that must sit inside the therapeutic range. The interval and the tablet strength must also be ones a patient can use. So a calculated seventeen point two nine milligrams every three point seven two hours could be written as twenty milligrams every four hours. She said, Don't tell me seventeen point two nine milligrams for an oral dose.", "s": "mo:19", "c": [[0, "peak and trough inside the therapeutic range"], [1, "usable interval and tablet strength"], [2, "17.29 mg every 3.72 hr → e.g. <b>20 mg every 4 hr</b>"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m7-1": {"title": "Product performance, bioavailability and bioequivalence", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective one. Drug product performance, bioavailability, and bioequivalence. A tablet or capsule only works if the drug leaves the dosage form. Drug product performance is that release step: the drug substance coming out of the product so that it can be absorbed. That release is what leads to the bioavailability of the drug substance.", "s": "ba:3", "c": [[3, "drug product performance: release of the drug substance from the product"], [4, "release → absorption → bioavailability"]]}, {"t": "Bioavailability is the rate and extent to which the active ingredient is absorbed from the product and becomes available at the site of action. Extent is measured by the area under the curve, A U C. Rate is measured by the time of the peak, t max. Most of this course uses bioavailability as a fraction, F, the share of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation. F has no units, and F is one for an intravenous dose. That fraction is the extent half of the definition.", "s": "ba:4", "c": [[0, "bioavailability = rate + extent"], [1, "extent: AUC, in (mg/L)hr"], [2, "rate: t<sub>max</sub>, in hr"], [3, "F = fraction of the dose absorbed, no units; F<sub>IV</sub> = 1"]]}, {"t": "Bioequivalence compares the same active ingredient from two products. The test product is the one under study, and the reference product is the standard or originator product it is compared with. They are bioequivalent when there is no significant difference in the rate and extent to which the active ingredient becomes available at the site of action, at the same molar dose, under similar conditions. The words that carry the meaning are absence of a significant difference, same molar dose, and rate and extent. She said, It's the absence of a significant difference. So they don't have to be exactly the same, but they just can't be too different.", "s": "ba:12", "c": [[1, "test against reference: same active ingredient"], [2, "no significant difference in rate and extent"], [2, "same molar dose, similar conditions"], [4, "similar, not identical"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example puts three formulations of one drug, A, B and C, on one plasma level against time plot. A and B have the same A U C, and C has half the A U C of A. The question is which pairs are bioequivalent. Compare extent first: A and B enclose the same area, and C encloses half the area of A. Then compare rate: A and C peak at the same time, and B peaks later than A.", "s": "ba:13", "c": [[1, "AUC<sub>A</sub> = AUC<sub>B</sub>"], [1, "AUC<sub>C</sub> = 0.5 AUC<sub>A</sub>"], [3, "extent: A = B; C = &frac12; A"], [4, "rate: t<sub>max</sub> of A = C; B later"]]}, {"t": "A against B: same extent, different rate, so they are not bioequivalent. A against C: same rate, different extent, so not bioequivalent either. So none of A, B and C is bioequivalent to another. A fourth curve drawn close to A, peaking at about the same time with about the same area, is the one she called bioequivalent, because it is similar in both, not identical in either. The area answers extent, the peak time answers rate, and bioequivalence needs both to match.", "s": "ba:13", "c": [[0, "A against B: same AUC, different t<sub>max</sub> ✗"], [1, "A against C: same t<sub>max</sub>, half the AUC ✗"], [2, "<b>none of A, B and C is bioequivalent to another</b>"], [3, "curve close to A in AUC and t<sub>max</sub> ✓"]]}, {"t": "Because bioequivalence needs rate as well as extent, two products with the same A U C are not shown to be bioequivalent until their peak times are also shown to be similar. She said, these two have similar F's, but you don't know if they're bioequivalent because you don't know about the, the rate. A relative bioavailability study compares two formulations of the same drug by their A U Cs, which is extent. A bioequivalence study is a specialized relative bioavailability study that compares rate as well.", "s": "ba:12", "c": [[0, "same AUC alone: bioequivalence not shown"], [2, "relative bioavailability study: extent, by AUC"], [3, "bioequivalence study: extent + rate"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m7-2": {"title": "Absolute and relative bioavailability", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective two. Relative against absolute bioavailability. F, the bioavailability factor, is the fraction of the dose that reaches the systemic circulation. Absolute bioavailability compares the drug given by an extravascular route, oral in this lecture, with the same drug given intravenously, I V. Extravascular means any route other than directly into the blood. The I V dose is all in the blood, so its F is one and it is the standard. Relative bioavailability compares two formulations of the same drug, such as a tablet against an oral solution, or a new product against the originator. Neither is I V, so the reference product is the standard.", "s": "ba:5", "c": [[3, "absolute: oral against IV"], [5, "IV is the standard: F = 1"], [6, "relative: two formulations, neither IV"], [7, "reference product is the standard"]]}, {"t": "Both are a ratio of areas under the curve, A U C, corrected for dose. For absolute bioavailability, F abs equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o, where p o means oral. The areas are in the same units, so they cancel, and the doses are in milligrams, so they cancel too. F abs has no units. The product being asked about goes on top, and the I V area goes in the denominator, so the I V dose goes on top of the dose ratio. She said, I want you to remember that the I V A U C is going to be in the denominator, right? That is what you are comparing it to.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[1, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} &times; {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [2, "{{frac:(mcg/mL)hr|(mcg/mL)hr}} &times; {{frac:mg|mg}}: no units"], [4, "IV AUC underneath, IV dose on top"]]}, {"t": "For relative bioavailability, F rel equals A U C A over A U C B, times D B over D A. A is the product being compared, the test. B is the reference or comparator, and its A U C goes in the denominator. A stem that says compared to the oral solution puts the oral solution in the denominator as B. An absolute F cannot be above one, because an oral dose cannot deliver more than the same dose given I V. A relative F can be above one when the test product does better than the reference, as with an old standard such as Bayer aspirin that a newer product can beat.", "s": "ba:9", "c": [[0, "F<sub>rel</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>A</sub>|AUC<sub>B</sub>}} &times; {{frac:D<sub>B</sub>|D<sub>A</sub>}}"], [1, "A = test"], [2, "B = reference, in the denominator"], [4, "F<sub>abs</sub> cannot be above 1"], [5, "F<sub>rel</sub> can be above 1"]]}, {"t": "Her first practice problem used six volunteers. A two hundred fifty milligram oral tablet gave an average A U C of one hundred one point five microgram hours per milliliter. A one hundred milligram I V bolus gave an average A U C of seventy three point eight microgram hours per milliliter. The question asks the absolute bioavailability of the tablet. It is absolute because the comparator is I V. So the I V area goes underneath and the I V dose goes on top: one hundred one point five over seventy three point eight, times one hundred milligrams over two hundred fifty milligrams.", "s": "ba:7", "c": [[1, "tablet: D<sub>po</sub> = 250 mg, AUC<sub>po</sub> = 101.5 (mcg/mL)hr"], [2, "IV: D<sub>IV</sub> = 100 mg, AUC<sub>IV</sub> = 73.8 (mcg/mL)hr"], [4, "comparator is IV → F<sub>abs</sub>"], [5, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:101.5 (mcg/mL)hr|73.8 (mcg/mL)hr}} &times; {{frac:100 mg|250 mg}}"]]}, {"t": "The area ratio is one point three seven five, and the dose ratio is zero point four. One point three seven five times zero point four is zero point five five zero one, which she rounds to zero point five five, or fifty five percent. The units cancel, so F has none. Inverting the ratios gives one point eight two instead of zero point five five, and an absolute F above one is not possible.", "s": "ba:7", "c": [[0, "= 1.375 &times; 0.4"], [1, "= 0.5501"], [1, "<b>F<sub>abs</sub> = 0.55, or 55%</b>"], [3, "✗ inverted: 1.82"]]}, {"t": "Her second practice problem compares the same two hundred fifty milligram tablet with five milliliters of an oral solution holding two hundred fifty milligrams, with an average A U C of ninety eight point seven six microgram hours per milliliter. The question asks the relative bioavailability of the tablet compared to the oral solution. It is relative because both products are oral. The solution is the comparator, so it is B, and its area goes underneath. F rel equals one hundred one point five over ninety eight point seven six, times two hundred fifty milligrams over two hundred fifty milligrams. The doses are equal, so the dose ratio is one. F rel is one point zero two seven seven, rounded to one point zero three.", "s": "ba:10", "c": [[0, "solution, B: D<sub>B</sub> = 250 mg, AUC<sub>B</sub> = 98.76 (mcg/mL)hr"], [2, "both oral → F<sub>rel</sub>"], [4, "F<sub>rel</sub> = {{frac:101.5 (mcg/mL)hr|98.76 (mcg/mL)hr}} &times; {{frac:250 mg|250 mg}}"], [5, "= 1.0277 &times; 1"], [6, "<b>F<sub>rel</sub> = 1.03</b>"]]}, {"t": "Read the two answers together. The tablet delivers fifty five percent of what the I V dose delivers, and about three percent more than the solution. A relative F above one is allowed, and an absolute F above one is not. What the one point zero three does not say: the tablet and the solution have similar extent, but nothing here gives their peak times, so bioequivalence is not established. The stem names the comparison. The word absolute, or an I V dose, means absolute, and compared to the oral solution, or two oral products, means relative.", "s": "ba:9", "c": [[1, "tablet against IV: 55%; against solution: about 3% more"], [3, "F<sub>rel</sub> = 1.03: extent similar, rate unknown, bioequivalence not shown"], [5, "absolute or an IV dose → F<sub>abs</sub>"], [5, "compared to the oral solution, two oral products → F<sub>rel</sub>"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m7-3": {"title": "Factors that influence bioavailability", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective three. Factors that can influence bioavailability. F is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation, with no units, and it is one for an I V dose. A low F is the sign that one of these factors is at work. Bioavailability can be low for reasons in the product, reasons on the way into the circulation, and reasons in the patient. Her list has nine entries.", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[2, "F = fraction reaching the systemic circulation; F<sub>IV</sub> = 1"], [3, "low F → a factor at work"], [4, "the product; on the way in; the patient"]]}, {"t": "First, the product: the physicochemical properties of the drug and the formulation, drug stability and p H effects, and prodrugs. A prodrug is a drug given in an inactive form that the body must convert to the active drug. She singled out the ones that come to mind most often: the physical and chemical properties of the drug itself, what p H does to it once it is in the body, first pass metabolism, and stability.", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[0, "physicochemical properties, formulation"], [0, "stability, pH effects"], [1, "prodrug: inactive form → converted to active drug"], [2, "most often: properties, pH, first-pass, stability"]]}, {"t": "Next, on the way in: pre systemic and first pass metabolism. Pre systemic metabolism is any metabolism that happens before the drug reaches the systemic circulation. First pass metabolism is metabolism of an oral dose, mainly by the liver, before the drug reaches the systemic circulation, so it lowers F. Also on the way in are food effects, drug drug interactions, and efflux transporters, which are membrane proteins that pump absorbed drug back out of the cell.", "s": "ba:14", "c": [[1, "pre-systemic: before the systemic circulation"], [2, "first-pass: oral dose, mainly the liver → F lower"], [3, "food effects; drug-drug interactions; efflux transporters"]]}, {"t": "Last, the patient: age and disease state. She said, Yeah, because our bodies change as we age, and yes, everything slows down. Her summary ties the list together. Low bioavailability has a variety of causes, including formulation factors and the first pass effect, and it bears on the drug's safety and efficacy.", "s": "ba:15", "c": [[0, "the patient: age, disease state"], [3, "low F: formulation factors, first-pass effect"], [3, "→ safety and efficacy"]]}, {"t": "Her reading of the first practice problem from objective two shows what a low F means. A two hundred fifty milligram oral tablet gave an A U C of one hundred one point five microgram hours per milliliter, against a one hundred milligram I V bolus with an A U C of seventy three point eight microgram hours per milliliter. So the tablet's F is one hundred one point five over seventy three point eight, times one hundred milligrams over two hundred fifty milligrams, which is zero point five five.", "s": "ba:7", "c": [[1, "tablet: 250 mg, AUC 101.5 (mcg/mL)hr; IV: 100 mg, AUC 73.8 (mcg/mL)hr"], [2, "F = {{frac:101.5|73.8}} &times; {{frac:100 mg|250 mg}} = 0.55"]]}, {"t": "What does that number mean for a one thousand milligram tablet? The amount available from the tablet is zero point five five times one thousand milligrams, five hundred fifty milligrams. The same one thousand milligrams given I V has an F of one, so all one thousand milligrams is available for the body to use. In the oral problems so far in the course, F was about zero point seven, zero point eight or zero point nine. At zero point five five almost half the dose is lost, so whether oral is the best way to give this drug is open to question. A low F is read as drug lost to one of these factors, and it raises the oral dose needed to match an I V dose.", "s": "ba:7", "c": [[1, "oral: 0.55 &times; 1000 mg = <b>550 mg</b>"], [2, "IV: F = 1 → all 1000 mg available"], [3, "earlier oral problems: F about 0.7, 0.8, 0.9"], [4, "0.55: almost half the dose lost"], [5, "low F → larger oral dose to match IV"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m7-4": {"title": "Estimating the bioavailability of a dose", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven A, objective four. Estimating the bioavailability of a dose. The calculation is a ratio of areas under the curve, A U C, corrected for dose. F abs equals A U C p o over A U C I V, times D I V over D p o, where p o means oral and I V means intravenous. The areas are in matching units and the doses in milligrams, so F has no units. With equal doses the dose ratio is one, and only the area ratio is left.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[3, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}} &times; {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [4, "AUC in (mg/L)hr, D in mg → F has no units"], [5, "D<sub>IV</sub> = D<sub>po</sub> → F = {{frac:AUC<sub>po</sub>|AUC<sub>IV</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "When the stem gives only the oral area, the I V area is built from clearance. Clearance, Cl, is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per hour. An I V dose equals clearance times its A U C, so A U C I V equals D I V over Cl. For a one compartment drug, clearance is k times V D, with k equal to zero point six nine three over the half-life. So a half-life and a volume of distribution are enough. Milligrams divided by liters per hour gives milligram hours per liter. This is the Module four line, Cl T equals F D zero over A U C, with F equal to one.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[2, "D<sub>IV</sub> = Cl &times; AUC<sub>IV</sub>"], [2, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|Cl}} = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|k &times; V<sub>D</sub>}}"], [3, "k = {{frac:0.693|t<sub>&frac12;</sub>}}"], [5, "{{frac:mg|L/hr}} = (mg/L)hr"], [6, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:FD<sub>0</sub>|AUC}} with F = 1"]]}, {"t": "The same relationship written for the oral dose is F abs times D p o equals Cl times A U C p o. The oral dose times F is the amount that actually entered the circulation. So F abs equals Cl times A U C p o, over D p o, and every unit cancels. If an oral dose is to match an I V dose in A U C, the two areas are set equal, and F becomes D I V over D p o. So the oral dose is the I V dose divided by F, which is larger than the I V dose.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[0, "F<sub>abs</sub> &times; D<sub>po</sub> = Cl &times; AUC<sub>po</sub>"], [2, "F<sub>abs</sub> = {{frac:Cl &times; AUC<sub>po</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [3, "AUC<sub>po</sub> = AUC<sub>IV</sub> → F = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|D<sub>po</sub>}}"], [4, "D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|F}}"]]}, {"t": "Her in class activity, question one. A single five hundred milligram oral tablet of an antihypertensive drug gave an A U C of seventy milligram hours per liter. A five hundred milligram I V bolus of the same drug in the same volunteer had a half-life of three hours and a V D of twenty five liters. The question asks the absolute bioavailability of the tablet, and the expected A U C after a two hundred fifty milligram I V bolus. There is no I V area, so it is built first from clearance. k is zero point six nine three over three hours, which is zero point two three one per hour.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[1, "tablet: D<sub>po</sub> = 500 mg, AUC<sub>po</sub> = 70 (mg/L)hr"], [2, "IV: D<sub>IV</sub> = 500 mg, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 3 hr, V<sub>D</sub> = 25 L"], [5, "k = {{frac:0.693|3 hr}} = 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"]]}, {"t": "The I V area is the five hundred milligram dose divided by twenty five liters times zero point two three one per hour. That gives eighty six point five eight milligram hours per liter. The doses are equal, so the dose ratio is one, and F is seventy over eighty six point five eight. That is zero point eight zero eight five, about eighty one percent. The units cancel, so F has none.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[0, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|25 L &times; 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}}"], [1, "= 86.58 (mg/L)hr"], [2, "F = {{frac:70 (mg/L)hr|86.58 (mg/L)hr}} &times; 1"], [3, "= 0.8085"], [3, "<b>F = 0.8085, about 81%</b>"]]}, {"t": "The other route gives the same F. Twenty five liters, times zero point two three one per hour, times seventy milligram hours per liter, divided by five hundred milligrams, is zero point eight zero eight five again. For the second part, kinetics are linear, so the A U C is proportional to dose. Two hundred fifty milligrams divided by twenty five liters times zero point two three one per hour gives forty three point two nine milligram hours per liter. That is half of eighty six point five eight, because half the dose gives half the area.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[1, "F = {{frac:(25 L)(0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>)(70 (mg/L)hr)|500 mg}} = 0.8085 ✓"], [2, "linear: AUC proportional to dose"], [3, "AUC = {{frac:250 mg|25 L &times; 0.231 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = <b>43.29 (mg/L)hr</b>"], [4, "&frac12; dose → &frac12; AUC: 86.58 → 43.29"]]}, {"t": "Question two. Ciprofloxacin has an oral immediate release bioavailability of seventy percent. What oral dose gives the same extent of absorption as a four hundred milligram I V bolus? Same extent means equal areas, so F equals D I V over D p o, and D p o equals D I V over F. Four hundred milligrams divided by zero point seven zero is five hundred seventy one point four three milligrams. That is not a real tablet, since the marketed strengths she looked up were two hundred fifty and five hundred milligrams. Rounded to a strength a tablet could have, she accepted five hundred seventy five or six hundred milligrams.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[1, "F = 0.70; D<sub>IV</sub> = 400 mg"], [3, "equal AUCs → D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>IV</sub>|F}}"], [4, "D<sub>po</sub> = {{frac:400 mg|0.70}} = 571.43 mg"], [5, "marketed: 250 and 500 mg"], [6, "<b>D<sub>po</sub> = 575 mg or 600 mg</b>"]]}, {"t": "Question three. After a five hundred milligram I V bolus, C p equals fifty e to the minus zero point two t. Matching the I V bolus equation, C zero is fifty milligrams per liter, and k is zero point two per hour. V D is the dose over C zero, five hundred milligrams over fifty milligrams per liter, ten liters. Clearance is zero point two per hour times ten liters, two liters per hour. The I V area is five hundred milligrams over two liters per hour, two hundred fifty milligram hours per liter. The tablet, at the same dose, gave one hundred eighty eight milligram hours per liter, so F is one hundred eighty eight over two hundred fifty, zero point seven five two, seventy five percent.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[2, "C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>: C<sub>p</sub> = 50e<sup>&minus;0.2t</sup> → C<sub>0</sub> = 50 mg/L, k = 0.2 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>0</sub>}} = {{frac:500 mg|50 mg/L}} = 10 L"], [4, "Cl = 0.2 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> &times; 10 L = <b>2 L/hr</b>"], [5, "AUC<sub>IV</sub> = {{frac:500 mg|2 L/hr}} = <b>250 (mg/L)hr</b>"], [6, "F = {{frac:188|250}} = 0.752 = <b>75%</b>"]]}, {"t": "Her poll: a five hundred milligram tablet with an A U C of one hundred fifteen milligram hours per liter, against a four hundred milligram I V bolus with an A U C of one hundred thirty two milligram hours per liter. F is one hundred fifteen over one hundred thirty two, times four hundred over five hundred, which is zero point six nine seven, so seventy percent. The I V area goes in the denominator and the I V dose in the numerator, and the inverted ratio is the usual wrong answer.", "s": "ba:6", "c": [[0, "tablet: 500 mg, 115 (mg/L)hr; IV: 400 mg, 132 (mg/L)hr"], [1, "F = {{frac:115|132}} &times; {{frac:400|500}} = 0.697"], [1, "<b>F = 70%</b>"], [2, "✗ inverted ratio"]]}], "mins": 6}, "gobj-m7b-1": {"title": "Response rises with dose, up to a maximum", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven B, objective one. How the response rises with dose, up to a maximum. Pharmacodynamics, or P D, is the relationship between the drug concentration at the site of action, the receptor, and the pharmacologic response that is observed. The link between pharmacokinetics, or P K, and P D has other names: the exposure response, concentration response or concentration effect relationship. Exposure response information is used in drug approval to judge safety, efficacy and benefit risk, and to derive dosing information.", "s": "pd:3", "c": [[2, "PD: concentration at the receptor → observed response"], [3, "PK–PD = exposure-response = concentration-response = concentration-effect"], [4, "drug approval: safety, efficacy, benefit-risk, dosing"]]}, {"t": "As the dose increases, the concentration at the receptor site increases, and the response increases, but only up to a maximum effect, E max. Plotted against dose on an ordinary axis, the response rises steeply at low doses and then flattens. So the same dose change gives a large increase in response at low doses, and the smallest gain near the maximum.", "s": "pd:4", "c": [[0, "dose ↑ → concentration at receptor ↑ → response ↑, up to E<sub>max</sub>"], [1, "ordinary dose axis: steep, then flat"], [2, "smallest gain near the maximum"]]}, {"t": "Plotted against log dose, the response is a sigmoid, an S shaped curve. The maximum is reached when all receptors are saturated. So a higher dose then raises the concentration without raising the response. The middle of the S is close to a straight line. Plotted as effect against log concentration, it is a straight line with slope m.", "s": "pd:5", "c": [[0, "log dose axis: sigmoid (S) curve"], [1, "top: all receptors saturated"], [4, "middle: effect vs log C, straight, slope m"]]}, {"t": "After an I V bolus, in that straight range, the effect falls in a straight line with time. E equals E zero minus k times m times t, divided by two point three. E is the effect at time t in hours, E zero the effect at time zero, k the first order elimination rate constant per hour, and m the slope of effect against log concentration. The two point three is there because the effect line uses base ten logarithms, and it is combined with the base ten I V bolus line, log C equals log C zero minus k t over two point three. A larger k or a larger m makes the effect fall faster. Here m is a slope, not a mass or a concentration.", "s": "pd:5", "c": [[1, "E = E<sub>0</sub> &minus; {{frac:kmt|2.3}}"], [2, "k in hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, t in hr, m = slope of E vs log C"], [3, "E = m log C + e &nbsp; with &nbsp; log C = log C<sub>0</sub> &minus; {{frac:kt|2.3}}"], [4, "k ↑ or m ↑ → effect falls faster"], [5, "m: a slope, not a mass or a concentration"]]}, {"t": "On a plasma level against time curve, three features of the effect are read against the minimum effective concentration, M E C, also written C eff. Onset is the time at which the rising level first reaches the M E C. Duration is how long the level stays at or above it, the time the drug is in its effective range. Intensity is how far the peak rises above the M E C. So intensity is a height above the M E C, and duration a length of time above it. Above the minimum toxic concentration, M T C, toxicity appears, and the range between the M E C and the M T C is the therapeutic window.", "s": "pd:4", "c": [[0, "read against the MEC (C<sub>eff</sub>)"], [1, "onset: level first reaches the MEC"], [2, "duration (t<sub>eff</sub>): time at or above the MEC"], [3, "intensity: height of the peak above the MEC"], [5, "therapeutic window: MEC to MTC"]]}, {"t": "Her questions on this objective are concepts, not numbers. They ask the definition of pharmacodynamics and its other names, and the uses of exposure response information in drug approval. They ask how to read the plots: flattening toward the maximum on an ordinary dose axis, an S on a log dose axis, and onset, duration and intensity each measured against the M E C.", "s": "pd:11", "c": [[0, "concept items, no numbers"], [1, "definitions; uses in drug approval"], [2, "ordinary axis: flattens; log axis: S; onset, duration, intensity vs MEC"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m7b-2": {"title": "Dose, half-life and duration of activity", "segs": [{"t": "Module seven B, objective two. How dose and half life change the duration of the response. The duration of activity, t eff, is how long a single I V bolus keeps the plasma level at or above C eff, the minimum effective concentration. Every one of her problems is a one compartment open model with first order elimination. So t eff comes from the I V bolus line, C equals C zero e to the minus k t: set C to C eff and solve for t. C zero is the level just after the bolus, the dose D zero divided by the volume of distribution V D.", "s": "pd:6", "c": [[2, "t<sub>eff</sub>: time with C ≥ C<sub>eff</sub> after one IV bolus"], [4, "C = C<sub>0</sub>e<sup>&minus;kt</sup>, set C = C<sub>eff</sub>, solve for t"], [5, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>}}"]]}, {"t": "The sheet prints t eff three ways, and they give the same answer. In natural logs, t eff equals the natural log of C zero minus the natural log of C eff, divided by k. That difference of logs is the natural log of the ratio C zero over C eff, so the ratio has no units, and dividing by k per hour gives hours. In base ten logs, t eff is two point three times log C zero minus log C eff, over k. Because two point three rounds two point three zero three, it gives a slightly smaller number: twenty six point three two hours instead of twenty six point three five for her slide problem.", "s": "pd:6", "c": [[1, "t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:ln C<sub>0</sub> &minus; ln C<sub>eff</sub>|k}}"], [2, "= {{frac:ln(C<sub>0</sub>/C<sub>eff</sub>)|k}} &nbsp; {{frac:mg/L|mg/L}} ÷ hr<sup>&minus;1</sup> = hr"], [3, "t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:2.3(log C<sub>0</sub> &minus; log C<sub>eff</sub>)|k}}"], [4, "2.3 vs 2.303: 26.32 hr vs 26.35 hr"]]}, {"t": "The third form uses the half life. t eff equals one point four four times t half times the natural log of D zero over V D times C eff. One point four four is one over zero point six nine three, which turns one over k into one point four four half lives. The dose sits inside the logarithm, so doubling the dose adds the natural log of two over k, zero point six nine three over k, which is one half life. It does not double t eff, and four times the dose adds two half lives. The half life multiplies the whole expression, so doubling the half life at the same dose doubles t eff.", "s": "pd:9", "c": [[1, "t<sub>eff</sub> = 1.44 t<sub>&frac12;</sub> ln({{frac:D<sub>0</sub>|V<sub>D</sub>C<sub>eff</sub>}})"], [2, "1.44 = {{frac:1|0.693}}"], [3, "2 × D<sub>0</sub> → + {{frac:ln 2|k}} = + {{frac:0.693|k}} = + 1 t<sub>&frac12;</sub>"], [4, "4 × D<sub>0</sub> → + 2 t<sub>&frac12;</sub>"], [5, "2 × t<sub>&frac12;</sub> → 2 × t<sub>eff</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Liver or kidney disease that lowers elimination prolongs the half life. The drug is retained longer, so the duration of activity increases, and so does the possibility of toxicity. With the dose, the gain shrinks: plotted against dose, t eff rises steeply at low doses and then flattens, because each further increase in dose adds less duration.", "s": "pd:10", "c": [[0, "liver or kidney disease → t<sub>&frac12;</sub> ↑"], [1, "→ t<sub>eff</sub> ↑, toxicity risk ↑"], [2, "t<sub>eff</sub> vs dose: steep, then flat"]]}, {"t": "Her slide Practice Problem. An antibiotic has an M E C of zero point two micrograms per milliliter, a V D of twenty four liters and a k of zero point one five per hour. What is t eff after a single two hundred fifty milligram I V dose? Zero point two micrograms per milliliter is zero point two milligrams per liter. C zero is two hundred fifty milligrams over twenty four liters, ten point four two milligrams per liter. k is given, so use the natural log form. The ratio, ten point four two over zero point two, is fifty two point zero eight. Its natural log, three point nine five three, over zero point one five per hour gives twenty six point three five hours.", "s": "pd:7", "c": [[1, "MEC 0.2 mcg/mL, V<sub>D</sub> 24 L, k 0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>, D<sub>0</sub> 250 mg"], [3, "0.2 mcg/mL = 0.2 mg/L"], [4, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:250 mg|24 L}} = 10.42 mg/L"], [6, "{{frac:C<sub>0</sub>|C<sub>eff</sub>}} = {{frac:10.42|0.2}} = 52.08"], [7, "<b>t<sub>eff</sub> = {{frac:ln 52.08|0.15 hr<sup>&minus;1</sup>}} = {{frac:3.953|0.15}} = 26.35 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Now double the dose to five hundred milligrams. C zero is twenty point eight three milligrams per liter, and t eff is thirty point nine seven hours. The gain is thirty point nine seven minus twenty six point three five, four point six two hours. That is zero point six nine three over zero point one five, one half life, not a doubling. Four times the dose, one thousand milligrams, gives thirty five point six zero hours, two half lives, or nine point two four hours, longer than at two hundred fifty milligrams.", "s": "pd:7", "c": [[1, "500 mg: C<sub>0</sub> = 20.83 mg/L → t<sub>eff</sub> = 30.97 hr"], [2, "30.97 &minus; 26.35 = 4.62 hr"], [3, "{{frac:0.693|0.15}} = 4.62 hr = 1 t<sub>&frac12;</sub>"], [4, "<b>1000 mg: t<sub>eff</sub> = 35.60 hr</b> (+ 2 t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = 9.24 hr)"]]}, {"t": "Her follow up asks what dose doubles t eff, to two times twenty six point three five, fifty two point seven hours. Turned round, the dose for a target duration is D zero equals C eff times V D times e to the plus k t eff. C eff times V D is zero point two milligrams per liter times twenty four liters, four point eight milligrams, because the liters cancel. e to the zero point one five times fifty two point seven is two thousand seven hundred ten point eight. Four point eight times that is thirteen thousand twelve milligrams, about thirteen grams. That is about fifty two times the dose for twice the duration, because the target time is in an exponent.", "s": "pd:7", "c": [[0, "target t<sub>eff</sub> = 2 × 26.35 = 52.7 hr"], [1, "D<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>eff</sub> V<sub>D</sub> e<sup>kt<sub>eff</sub></sup>"], [2, "(0.2 mg/L)(24 L) = 4.8 mg"], [3, "e<sup>(0.15)(52.7)</sup> = 2710.8"], [4, "<b>D<sub>0</sub> = 4.8 × 2710.8 = 13,012 mg ≈ 13 g</b>"], [5, "≈ 52 × the dose for 2 × the duration"]]}, {"t": "Her Practice three uses the half life form. A seventy five kilogram patient gets a twenty milligram per kilogram I V bolus, with a V D of zero point three liters per kilogram, a half life of three point six hours, and an M E C of seven point two micrograms per milliliter, which is seven point two milligrams per liter. The kilograms cancel, so C zero is twenty over zero point three, sixty six point six seven milligrams per liter. The natural log of sixty six point six seven over seven point two is two point two two six. Three point six hours over zero point six nine three is five point one nine five hours. Their product, t eff, is eleven point five six hours.", "s": "pd:9", "c": [[1, "20 mg/kg, V<sub>D</sub> 0.3 L/kg, t<sub>&frac12;</sub> 3.6 hr, MEC 7.2 mcg/mL = 7.2 mg/L"], [2, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:20 mg/kg|0.3 L/kg}} = 66.67 mg/L"], [3, "ln({{frac:66.67|7.2}}) = 2.226"], [4, "{{frac:3.6 hr|0.693}} = 5.195 hr"], [5, "<b>t<sub>eff</sub> = 2.226 × 5.195 = 11.56 hr</b>"]]}, {"t": "Doubling that dose gives a C zero of one hundred thirty three point three milligrams per liter and a t eff of fifteen point one six hours, an increase of three point six hours, one half life. Her poll asked, true or false, whether doubling the I V bolus dose of a linear, first order drug gives a two fold increase in the duration of activity. Her key is false, because doubling adds one half life. Other wrong answers she sees: converting the M E C as if the number changed, e to the minus k t in the dose line, and a base ten log in the natural log form.", "s": "pd:12", "c": [[0, "C<sub>0</sub> = {{frac:40|0.3}} = 133.3 mg/L → <b>t<sub>eff</sub> = 15.16 hr</b> (+ 3.6 hr = 1 t<sub>&frac12;</sub>)"], [1, "poll: 2 × dose → 2 × t<sub>eff</sub>?"], [2, "<b>False</b>: + 1 t<sub>&frac12;</sub>"], [3, "✗ MEC number changed &nbsp; ✗ e<sup>&minus;kt</sup> in D<sub>0</sub> line &nbsp; ✗ log in ln form"]]}], "mins": 7}, "gobj-m8-1": {"title": "Linear versus nonlinear pharmacokinetics", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective one. Linear versus nonlinear pharmacokinetics. Most drugs are eliminated by a first order process, where the rate of elimination is faster when there is more drug in the body. That is linear pharmacokinetics, and it is what the course has used until now. In linear pharmacokinetics the parameters, such as the half life and the clearance, do not change when different or multiple doses are given. That is why a first dose can be carried forward to steady state by superposition.", "s": "nonlin:3", "c": [[2, "first order: more drug → faster elimination"], [4, "linear: parameters (t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, Cl) unchanged with dose"], [5, "→ superposition to steady state"]]}, {"t": "In nonlinear pharmacokinetics, increased doses or chronic administration cause deviations from the profile seen with single doses. The half life and the clearance change, and a small change in dose can give a large change in concentration. The cause is saturation: an enzyme or carrier system that can only work so fast. Nonlinear processes can occur in absorption, distribution, metabolism or excretion, and this module deals with elimination. Phenytoin is the prototype nonlinear drug in this course.", "s": "nonlin:3", "c": [[0, "nonlinear: higher or repeated doses → deviation from single-dose profile"], [1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> and Cl change; small dose change → large concentration change"], [2, "cause: saturable enzyme or carrier"], [4, "Ex. phenytoin"]]}, {"t": "The signs of saturation kinetics. Elimination is nonlinear, and the half life changes as the dose increases. The A U C, the area under the concentration against time curve, is not proportional to the bioavailable dose. Other drugs needing the same enzyme or carrier system can affect the saturation. And the composition or ratio of the metabolites can change with the dose.", "s": "nonlin:6", "c": [[1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> changes with dose"], [2, "AUC not proportional to dose"], [3, "competing drugs, same enzyme or carrier"], [4, "metabolite ratio changes"]]}, {"t": "Her first figure is a log plot of plasma level against time. Curve A is a high dose and curve B a low dose of the saturable drug, and curve C is a first order drug. Curve A bends before it becomes straight, while curve B is straight, like curve C. The terminal slopes of A and B are the same.", "s": "nonlin:4", "c": [[1, "A: high dose; B: low dose; C: first order"], [2, "A bends, then straight; B straight like C"], [3, "terminal slopes A = B"]]}, {"t": "Her second figure plots A U C against dose. For a linear drug the A U C is proportional to the dose, a straight line. The saturable drug follows that line up to a certain dose and then rises above it, so the A U C is no longer directly proportional to the dose. The same thing shows at steady state in her slide example: a one third rise in dose rate, from six to eight milligrams per kilogram per day, doubles the steady state level from ten to twenty milligrams per liter. A linear drug would have gone only to thirteen point three milligrams per liter.", "s": "nonlin:5", "c": [[1, "linear: AUC ∝ dose"], [2, "saturable: above the line past a certain dose"], [3, "6 → 8 mg/kg/day: C<sub>ss</sub> 10 → 20 mg/L"], [4, "linear: 10 × {{frac:8|6}} = 13.3 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Her written question asks the difference between linear and nonlinear pharmacokinetics. The answer is the parameters: unchanged with dose in linear, changed by increased doses or chronic administration in nonlinear. The trap is to swap the words. Linear is not zero order, and nonlinear is not simply first order. A saturable drug behaves as first order at low concentrations and as zero order at high ones. The summary statement that clearance and half life are usually not constant within nonlinear pharmacokinetic processes is true.", "s": "nonlin:19", "c": [[1, "linear: parameters fixed; nonlinear: parameters change"], [3, "✗ linear = zero order &nbsp; ✗ nonlinear = first order"], [4, "saturable: first order at low C, zero order at high C"], [5, "Cl and t<sub>&frac12;</sub> usually not constant: true"]]}], "mins": 3}, "gobj-m8-2": {"title": "K M, C p and the order of elimination", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective two. How K M and C p decide the order of saturable elimination. A saturable elimination follows Michaelis Menten kinetics: the rate depends on the plasma concentration, C p, and on two constants, V max and K M. V max is the maximum elimination rate, the fastest the body can eliminate the drug, so it is a rate, in concentration or amount per time. K M is the Michaelis constant, which reflects the capacity of the enzyme system. Its units are concentration, milligrams per liter, so it can be compared directly with C p. For a given drug in a given patient, V max and K M are constants, and they differ from one patient to another.", "s": "nonlin:7", "c": [[3, "V<sub>max</sub>: maximum elimination rate (mg/day, mg/kg/day)"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub>: Michaelis constant, enzyme capacity"], [5, "K<sub>M</sub> in mg/L → compare with C<sub>p</sub>"], [6, "constant in one patient; differ between patients"]]}, {"t": "The rate of elimination, the fall in C p with time, equals V max times C p, divided by K M plus C p. When C p is much less than K M, C p is negligible in the denominator. The rate is then V max over K M, a constant called k prime, times C p, so it depends on concentration and elimination looks first order. When C p is much greater than K M, K M is negligible and the concentrations cancel. The rate then equals V max, independent of concentration, so elimination is zero order.", "s": "nonlin:8", "c": [[0, "&minus;{{frac:dC<sub>p</sub>|dt}} = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>C<sub>p</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [2, "C<sub>p</sub> ≪ K<sub>M</sub>: {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub>}}C<sub>p</sub> = k&prime;C<sub>p</sub> → first order"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> ≫ K<sub>M</sub>: rate = V<sub>max</sub> → zero order"]]}, {"t": "So as the dose is increased, elimination starts to lose its first order behaviour, and the drug accumulates because the body cannot go faster than V max. A higher V max, with K M the same, removes the drug faster. Her poll asked, true or false, whether drugs with Michaelis Menten saturation kinetics will often exhibit zero order kinetics at low concentrations and first order kinetics at high ones. She said, Should have been false, right? Low concentrations looks like first order, high concentrations looks like zero order, OK?", "s": "nonlin:9", "c": [[0, "dose ↑ → capped at V<sub>max</sub> → accumulation"], [1, "V<sub>max</sub> ↑ (same K<sub>M</sub>) → faster removal"], [2, "poll: zero order at low C, first order at high C?"], [3, "<b>False</b>: low C first order, high C zero order"]]}, {"t": "Because C p is added to K M, the half life rises as C p rises. The half life equals V D, times zero point six nine three over V max, times K M plus C p. V D is in liters, V max in milligrams per day, and K M and C p in milligrams per liter, so the half life comes out in days. For a first order drug the half life is zero point six nine three over k, which does not depend on concentration.", "s": "nonlin:17", "c": [[1, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = V<sub>D</sub>({{frac:0.693|V<sub>max</sub>}})(K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>)"], [2, "L × {{frac:1|mg/day}} × mg/L = day"], [3, "first order: t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = {{frac:0.693|k}}, no C<sub>p</sub>"]]}, {"t": "The total body clearance, Cl T, equals V max divided by K M plus C p. C p is in the denominator, so clearance falls as the concentration rises. Milligrams per day divided by milligrams per liter gives liters per day. For a first order drug the clearance is k times V D, again with no concentration in it.", "s": "nonlin:18", "c": [[0, "Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub>}}"], [1, "C<sub>p</sub> ↑ → Cl<sub>T</sub> ↓"], [2, "{{frac:mg/day|mg/L}} = L/day"], [3, "first order: Cl = kV<sub>D</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Her Solutions two. A seventy five kilogram patient has a V D of zero point seven five liters per kilogram, a K M of nine milligrams per liter and a V max of five hundred milligrams per day. She asks the half life and the total body clearance at fifteen and at twenty one milligrams per liter. First, V D in liters: zero point seven five liters per kilogram times seventy five kilograms is fifty six point two five liters. At fifteen milligrams per liter, K M plus C p is twenty four milligrams per liter. The half life is fifty six point two five liters, times zero point six nine three over five hundred milligrams per day, times twenty four, which gives one point eight seven days.", "s": "nonlin:17", "c": [[1, "V<sub>D</sub> 0.75 L/kg, K<sub>M</sub> 9 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 500 mg/day"], [3, "V<sub>D</sub> = 0.75 L/kg × 75 kg = 56.25 L"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>p</sub> = 9 + 15 = 24 mg/L"], [5, "<b>t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = (56.25 L)({{frac:0.693|500 mg/day}})(24 mg/L) = 1.87 days</b>"]]}, {"t": "The clearance at fifteen is five hundred milligrams per day over twenty four milligrams per liter, twenty point eight liters per day. At twenty one milligrams per liter, K M plus C p is thirty milligrams per liter. The half life is fifty six point two five times zero point six nine three over five hundred, times thirty, which is two point three four days. The clearance is five hundred over thirty, sixteen point seven liters per day. So the higher concentration has the longer half life and the lower clearance, which answers her written question three.", "s": "nonlin:18", "c": [[0, "<b>Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:500 mg/day|24 mg/L}} = 20.8 L/day</b>"], [1, "9 + 21 = 30 mg/L"], [2, "<b>t<sub>&frac12;</sub> = (56.25)({{frac:0.693|500}})(30) = 2.34 days</b>"], [3, "<b>Cl<sub>T</sub> = {{frac:500|30}} = 16.7 L/day</b>"], [4, "C<sub>p</sub> ↑: t<sub>&frac12;</sub> ↑, Cl<sub>T</sub> ↓"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Definitions ask which term is the maximum elimination rate, V max, and which is the constant in concentration units that reflects enzyme capacity, K M. K M is not a rate. Every sheet asks the half life and the clearance when the plasma concentration is fifteen milligrams per liter, and either measured steady state level could be asked instead. The unit checks are V D converted from liters per kilogram to liters first, the half life in days and the clearance in liters per day when V max is in milligrams per day.", "s": "nonlin:7", "c": [[1, "V<sub>max</sub>: rate; K<sub>M</sub>: concentration"], [2, "✗ K<sub>M</sub> as a rate"], [3, "t<sub>&frac12;</sub>, Cl<sub>T</sub> at a stated C<sub>p</sub> (15 mg/L)"], [4, "V<sub>D</sub> in L first; t<sub>&frac12;</sub> in days, Cl<sub>T</sub> in L/day"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m8-3": {"title": "Calculating V max and K M from two doses", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective three. Calculating V max and K M after multiple dosing in a patient. V max and K M can be found in test tubes, in vitro, by plotting enzyme experiments. That is not something a clinician does. In a patient, in vivo, they are found from the patient's own drug levels.", "s": "nonlin:10", "c": [[2, "in vitro: test tubes, enzyme experiments"], [4, "in vivo: the patient's own steady-state levels"]]}, {"t": "The relation is the dose rate line. R, the daily dose rate, equals V max times C s s, divided by K M plus C s s. To use it in a patient, the drug is given at one dose rate until steady state, and the level is measured. Then a second dose rate is given, and its steady state is measured. Both pairs come from one patient, so they share one V max and one K M. Each level has to be a steady state level, and for this kind of drug steady state takes much longer than for a linear drug: depending on the drug, two to three weeks, not three to five half-lives.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[1, "R = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>ss</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [2, "pair 1: R<sub>1</sub> with C<sub>1</sub>"], [3, "pair 2: R<sub>2</sub> with C<sub>2</sub>"], [4, "one patient: one V<sub>max</sub>, one K<sub>M</sub>"], [5, "steady state: 2 to 3 weeks"]]}, {"t": "Writing the dose rate line once for each pair, and combining the two, gives K M directly. This is the direct method, and K M always comes first. K M equals R two minus R one, divided by R one over C one, minus R two over C two. Each of those ratios is a dose rate divided by its own concentration. The top is in milligrams per day, and the bottom is in milligrams per day per milligram per liter, so the rate units cancel and K M comes out in milligrams per liter. This line is on the equation sheet, page two, right column.", "s": "nonlin:13", "c": [[0, "R<sub>1</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>1</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>1</sub>}} &nbsp; R<sub>2</sub> = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>2</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>2</sub>}}"], [2, "K<sub>M</sub> = {{frac:R<sub>2</sub> − R<sub>1</sub>|(R<sub>1</sub>/C<sub>1</sub>) − (R<sub>2</sub>/C<sub>2</sub>)}}"], [4, "{{frac:mg/day|(mg/day) per (mg/L)}} = mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Her slide example. A seventy two kilogram patient on six milligrams per kilogram per day reached a steady state of ten milligrams per liter, lower than desired. On eight milligrams per kilogram per day, the steady state was twenty milligrams per liter, higher than desired. K M first. The first ratio is six over ten, which is zero point six. The second ratio is eight over twenty, which is zero point four. The top of the K M line is eight minus six, two milligrams per kilogram per day. The bottom is zero point six minus zero point four, which is zero point two. Two divided by zero point two gives a K M of ten milligrams per liter.", "s": "nonlin:14", "c": [[0, "R<sub>1</sub> = 6 mg/kg/day → C<sub>1</sub> = 10 mg/L"], [2, "R<sub>2</sub> = 8 mg/kg/day → C<sub>2</sub> = 20 mg/L"], [4, "{{frac:R<sub>1</sub>|C<sub>1</sub>}} = {{frac:6|10}} = 0.6"], [5, "{{frac:R<sub>2</sub>|C<sub>2</sub>}} = {{frac:8|20}} = 0.4"], [6, "K<sub>M</sub> = {{frac:8 − 6 mg/kg/day|0.6 − 0.4}}"], [7, "= {{frac:2|0.2}}"], [8, "<b>K<sub>M</sub> = 10 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "With K M known, V max comes from either pair. V max equals R times K M plus C s s, divided by C s s. This line is not on the equation sheet. It is the sheet's dose rate line multiplied by K M plus C s s and divided by C s s.", "s": "nonlin:15", "c": [[1, "V<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:R (K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>)|C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [3, "from R = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub> C<sub>ss</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}}: × (K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>), ÷ C<sub>ss</sub>"]]}, {"t": "From the second pair: eight, times ten plus twenty, is two hundred forty, and two hundred forty divided by twenty gives twelve milligrams per kilogram per day. Check with the first pair: six, times ten plus ten, divided by ten, is also twelve, as it must be. She said: I wrote this twice because you should get the same answer regardless of which way you use it. The doses were per kilogram, so V max is per kilogram. The seventy two kilograms are used only when a dose for this patient is asked, in objective four.", "s": "nonlin:15", "c": [[0, "pair 2: {{frac:(8 mg/kg/day)(10 + 20 mg/L)|20 mg/L}} = {{frac:240|20}} = <b>12 mg/kg/day</b>"], [1, "pair 1: {{frac:(6)(10 + 10)|10}} = 12 mg/kg/day ✓"], [3, "per kilogram: V<sub>max</sub> in mg/kg/day"]]}, {"t": "Her Solutions one gives the same calculation in milligrams per day. Three hundred milligrams per day gave five point one milligrams per liter, and five hundred milligrams per day gave twenty milligrams per liter. The first ratio is three hundred over five point one, fifty eight point eight two. The second is five hundred over twenty, twenty five. The top is five hundred minus three hundred, two hundred, and the bottom is fifty eight point eight two minus twenty five, thirty three point eight two. So K M is five point nine one milligrams per liter.", "s": "nonlin:13", "c": [[1, "300 mg/day → 5.1 mg/L; &nbsp; 500 mg/day → 20 mg/L"], [2, "{{frac:300|5.1}} = 58.82"], [3, "{{frac:500|20}} = 25"], [4, "K<sub>M</sub> = {{frac:500 − 300|58.82 − 25}} = {{frac:200|33.82}}"], [5, "<b>K<sub>M</sub> = 5.91 mg/L</b>"]]}, {"t": "Then V max is three hundred, times five point nine one three plus five point one, divided by five point one, which is six hundred forty seven point eight milligrams per day, with K M carried unrounded. Her sheet writes six hundred forty seven point eight three.", "s": "nonlin:15", "c": [[0, "V<sub>max</sub> = {{frac:(300 mg/day)(5.913 + 5.1)|5.1}} = <b>647.8 mg/day</b>"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Her stem gives dose one and its steady state, then dose two and its steady state, and asks K M, then V max, as separate parts or as one part asking both. The doses can be per kilogram, as in the slide example, or per day, as on her sheets, where the weight serves only the volume of distribution. The usual errors: putting a rate unit on K M, subtracting the ratios the wrong way round, which makes K M negative, and pairing one dose rate with the other dose's concentration in the V max line. A concept question can ask what the direct method needs: two different dose rates in the same patient, and the steady state concentration each produced.", "s": "nonlin:13", "c": [[1, "dose 1 + C<sub>ss</sub> → dose 2 + C<sub>ss</sub> → K<sub>M</sub> → V<sub>max</sub>"], [3, "✗ K<sub>M</sub> in mg/day &nbsp; ✗ ratios reversed (K<sub>M</sub> < 0) &nbsp; ✗ R<sub>1</sub> with C<sub>2</sub>"], [4, "needs: two dose rates, same patient, each steady state"]]}], "mins": 5}, "gobj-m8-4": {"title": "Dosing a nonlinear drug such as phenytoin", "segs": [{"t": "Module eight, objective four. Estimating the dose of a nonlinear drug such as phenytoin. At steady state the dose rate R equals the rate of saturable elimination at that level. So R equals V max times C s s, divided by K M plus C s s, and with V max and K M known, a target C s s gives the daily dose rate. R is a dose rate, an amount per unit time such as milligrams per day, so an answer of one thousand milligrams with no interval is not a regimen. K M and C s s are both in milligrams per liter and cancel, so R comes out in the units of V max.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[2, "at steady state: dose rate = elimination rate"], [3, "R = {{frac:V<sub>max</sub>C<sub>ss</sub>|K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>}}"], [4, "R: amount per time (mg/day), not a single dose"], [5, "{{frac:mg/L|mg/L}} cancel → R in units of V<sub>max</sub>"]]}, {"t": "When V max is given per kilogram, R comes out per kilogram, and the weight then gives the patient's dose. Steady state takes two to three weeks for such a drug, so the C s s in these lines is a level reached much later than three to five half lives. Phenytoin is dosed carefully because it is nonlinear and has a narrow therapeutic window, about ten to twenty milligrams per liter. Levels are measured and the dose adjusted for the individual patient.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[0, "V<sub>max</sub> per kg → R per kg → × weight"], [1, "steady state: 2 to 3 weeks"], [2, "phenytoin window: about 10 to 20 mg/L"]]}, {"t": "Her first slide example. K M is five milligrams per liter and V max is eight milligrams per kilogram per day. She asks the daily dose rate for an average plasma concentration of twenty four milligrams per liter in a sixty five kilogram patient. The top is eight milligrams per kilogram per day times twenty four milligrams per liter, one hundred ninety two. The bottom is five plus twenty four, twenty nine milligrams per liter. The milligrams per liter cancel, so R is one hundred ninety two over twenty nine, six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day. For the patient, six point six two times sixty five kilograms gives four hundred thirty milligrams per day.", "s": "nonlin:12", "c": [[1, "K<sub>M</sub> 5 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 8 mg/kg/day"], [2, "target C<sub>ss</sub> 24 mg/L, 65 kg"], [3, "R = {{frac:(8 mg/kg/day)(24 mg/L)|(5 + 24) mg/L}}"], [4, "= {{frac:192|29}}"], [5, "= 6.62 mg/kg/day"], [6, "<b>6.62 mg/kg/day × 65 kg = 430 mg/day</b>"]]}, {"t": "The per kilogram rate is not the answer for a patient. She said, And if I'm your patient, please don't tell me six point six two milligrams per kilogram per day. Tell me how many, tell me what I need. A per kilogram V max needs the weight at the end. A per day V max does not, and the weight then serves only the volume of distribution.", "s": "nonlin:12", "c": [[0, "✗ per-kg rate left for a patient"], [3, "V<sub>max</sub> per kg: × weight at the end"], [4, "V<sub>max</sub> per day: weight only for V<sub>D</sub>"]]}, {"t": "Turned round, a new dose rate gives the steady state level it will produce. Multiply out: R times K M plus C s s equals V max times C s s. Gather the C s s terms: R times K M equals C s s times V max minus R. Divide, and C s s equals R times K M, over V max minus R. This line is not on the equation sheet. As R approaches V max the denominator shrinks, so a small increase in dose gives a large rise in C s s.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[1, "R(K<sub>M</sub> + C<sub>ss</sub>) = V<sub>max</sub>C<sub>ss</sub>"], [2, "RK<sub>M</sub> = C<sub>ss</sub>(V<sub>max</sub> &minus; R)"], [3, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:RK<sub>M</sub>|V<sub>max</sub> &minus; R}}"], [4, "not on the sheet"], [5, "R → V<sub>max</sub>: small dose ↑ → large C<sub>ss</sub> ↑"]]}, {"t": "Her Solutions one uses the K M of five point nine one milligrams per liter and the V max of six hundred forty seven point eight three milligrams per day from objective three. First, the dose for fifteen milligrams per liter. R is six hundred forty seven point eight three times fifteen, over five point nine one plus fifteen. That gives four hundred sixty four point seven three milligrams per day, which she gave as four hundred sixty five milligrams per day. The weight is not needed, because V max is per day.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[0, "K<sub>M</sub> 5.91 mg/L, V<sub>max</sub> 647.83 mg/day"], [2, "R = {{frac:(647.83 mg/day)(15 mg/L)|(5.91 + 15) mg/L}}"], [3, "<b>R = 464.73 ≈ 465 mg/day</b>"]]}, {"t": "Then the steady state on four hundred fifty milligrams daily. C s s is four hundred fifty times five point nine one, over six hundred forty seven point eight three minus four hundred fifty. The top is two thousand six hundred fifty nine point five, and the bottom is one hundred ninety seven point eight three. The milligrams per day cancel, leaving thirteen point four four milligrams per liter, written thirteen point five on her sheet, or thirteen point four five with K M and V max unrounded. So four hundred fifty milligrams per day is about ninety seven percent of four hundred sixty five, but the level is thirteen point five instead of fifteen.", "s": "nonlin:11", "c": [[1, "C<sub>ss</sub> = {{frac:(450 mg/day)(5.91 mg/L)|(647.83 &minus; 450) mg/day}}"], [2, "= {{frac:2659.5|197.83}}"], [3, "<b>C<sub>ss</sub> = 13.44 ≈ 13.5 mg/L</b>"], [4, "450 ≈ 97% of 465 mg/day, but 13.5 mg/L, not 15"]]}, {"t": "How she tests it. Every sheet asks a daily dose for fifteen milligrams per liter at steady state, then the steady state at another dose, such as eleven point five milligrams per liter on four hundred milligrams daily in her In Class Practice. She rounds to the nearest ten when the stem says so: in her slide Example two, five hundred eighteen point four milligrams per day becomes five hundred twenty. Otherwise she rounds to a dose that can be given. Concept items ask what R means, how long steady state takes, and what a small dose increase near saturation does: a disproportionately large rise in C s s.", "s": "nonlin:14", "c": [[1, "dose for C<sub>ss</sub> 15 mg/L → C<sub>ss</sub> at another dose (400 mg/day → 11.5 mg/L)"], [2, "518.4 → 520 mg/day (nearest ten)"], [4, "R = dose rate; 2 to 3 weeks; small dose ↑ → large C<sub>ss</sub> ↑"]]}], "mins": 5}};
